@techreport{Wright2014a,
	type = {Issue {Brief}},
	title = {The {Scores} at {Half} {Time}: {An} update on the international discussions on the governance of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	abstract = {Marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ) represent around half of the Planet’s surface and a significant amount of its biodiversity. Over the past decades, the international community has become increasingly cognisant of the growing threats to ABNJ. To address this issue, the United Nations General Assembly (UNGA) created an Ad-Hoc Open-ended Informal Working Group (“BBNJ Working Group”) to engage in discussions on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in ABNJ. Since the commencement of discussions in 2006, the focus has mainly been on gaps in the current international framework and whether these necessitate the adoption of a new instrument. In particular, States have discussed the possible adoption of an Implementing Agreement to the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in ABNJ (UNCLOS IA). At the 2012 United Nations Conference on Sustainable Development (“Rio+20”), States agreed to decide by the end of the 69th session of the UNGA (September 2015) whether or not to launch the negotiations for the conclusion of such a new global agreement. This decision-making process spans three meetings of the BBNJ Working Group, specifically convened to discuss “the scope, parameters and feasibility of an international instrument under UNCLOS”. Two of these meetings have already taken place, in April 1-4 and June 16-19, 2014. This paper highlights the remaining challenges on the “long and winding road” towards the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in ABNJ.},
	institution = {IDDRI},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Rochette, Julien and Unger, Sebastian and Gjerde, Kristina and Ardron, Jeff},
	year = {2014},
	note = {Issue: september},
	keywords = {BBNJ, not undermine, cited-by:glen-strong-high-seas, IFB, OA:unknown, source:glen-7pubs-refs, type:grey, author:Glen Wright, EIA / SEA},
	pages = {4},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This issue brief provides a mid-process update on the international discussions concerning the governance of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). Specifically, it tracks the outcomes of the first two meetings of the Ad-Hoc Open-ended Informal Working Group (BBNJ Working Group) held in April and June 2014, and identifies the remaining challenges ahead of the United Nations General Assembly (UNGA) decision, due by September 2015, on whether to launch negotiations for a new UNCLOS Implementing Agreement (IA).

Methods
The authors (affiliated with IDDRI, IASS, and IUCN) produced a descriptive policy analysis of the diplomatic proceedings at the two 2014 BBNJ Working Group meetings. The analysis draws on the positions, statements, and arguments of States, regional groupings (EU, G77 and China, African Union, CARICOM, Pacific States), and observer organisations. It situates these discussions within the broader legal framework of UNCLOS, the 2011 "Package Deal," and the Rio+20 timeline. The brief is not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it is a synthesis of multilateral negotiation dynamics.

Key findings

A strong majority of States now support opening negotiations for a UNCLOS Implementing Agreement on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in ABNJ, with new entrants (African Union, CARICOM, Pacific States) joining the pro-IA coalition.
States broadly agree that future negotiations should be grounded in the 2011 Package Deal covering four elements: marine genetic resources (including benefit-sharing), area-based management tools (including MPAs), environmental impact assessments, and capacity-building and marine technology transfer.
An emerging consensus favours suspending the legal-principle debate (common heritage of mankind vs. freedom of the high seas) in favour of practical access-and-benefit-sharing arrangements, with the International Seabed Authority potentially serving as a model or receiving an expanded mandate.
Significant divisions persist on the scope of the IA: whether it should address only discrete legal gaps or adopt a broader vision encompassing guiding principles, strengthening existing agreements, and enhancing cooperation and coordination among sectoral and regional bodies.
States remain sharply divided on the role of the IA in fisheries management, with fishing States (US, Russia, Iceland, Japan, Korea) arguing fisheries are already covered by UNFSA and RFMOs, while others insist fishing is the greatest threat to high-seas biodiversity and must be addressed.
The 2014 meetings marked a notable shift toward transparency, with observer organisations and previously silent States (especially developing countries) contributing openly, in contrast to earlier closed-door sessions.
Next steps include a convergence report by the Co-Chairs, a final Working Group meeting in January 2015, and a UNGA decision by the 69th session (September 2015).


Limitations

The brief is a point-in-time snapshot (September 2014) of an ongoing, unresolved negotiation; outcomes and the final scope of any agreement remained undetermined.
It covers only two of the three planned BBNJ Working Group meetings; the third (January 2015) and the subsequent UNGA decision are not reflected.
As a policy brief rather than a peer-reviewed article, it lacks a formal research methodology, systematic data collection, or independent verification of the diplomatic positions reported.
The analysis necessarily reflects the authors' institutional affiliations (IDDRI, IASS, IUCN), which may shape the framing of "challenges" and the emphasis placed on certain positions.
The document does not include quantitative assessments of biodiversity threats in ABNJ or empirical evidence on the effectiveness of existing governance arrangements; these are referenced but not analysed within this text.

