@techreport{Wright2014b,
	title = {Advancing marine biodiversity protection through regional fisheries management: a review of high seas bottom fisheries closures},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	url = {https://glenwright.earth/library/glen-wright-etal-advancing-marine-biodiversity-protection-regional-fisheries-management-review-high-seas-bottom-fisheries-closures/},
	abstract = {Ocean regions that do not fall under the jurisdiction of any State, areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ or the “high seas”),1 represent almost half of the planet’s surface and a significant portion of its biodiversity. The high seas are increasingly under threat from human activities, including seabed mining, navigation and fishing. The international community has called on Regional Fisheries Management Organisations to take a number of actions to protect vulnerable marine ecosystems (VMEs) in these areas, including closing areas to bottom fishing (high seas bottom fisheries closures; HSBFCs). The aims of this paper are twofold: firstly, to review the efforts made by RFMOs to implement HSBFCs and suggest options for improving the protection of VMEs within this framework; and secondly, to provide a brief overview of options for addressing fisheries in any new international agreement on high seas biodiversity, currently under discussion. Section 2 outlines the global context, including an overview of bottom fishing and its impacts, while Section 3 details the context and process for HSBFCs. Section 4 provides an assessment of RFMO performance in the Atlantic, Pacific, Southern and Indian oceans. Section 4 considers the role RFMOs in biodiversity conservation in light of their performance in relation to HSBFCs, and highlights some pathways for strengthening their role. Section 6 concludes by summarising the issues at stake and the possible ways forward.},
	institution = {IDDRI},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Ardron, Jeff and Gjerde, Kristina and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2014},
	keywords = {fisheries, BBNJ, RFMO, not undermine, cited-by:glen-strong-high-seas, IFB, OA:unknown, source:glen-7pubs-refs, type:grey, author:Glen Wright},
	pages = {28},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2014 working paper by Wright, Ardron, Gjerde, and Rochette (IDDRI, IASS, IUCN) pursues two aims. First, it reviews the efforts made by Regional Fisheries Management Organisations (RFMOs) to implement high seas bottom fisheries closures (HSBFCs) and suggests options for improving the protection of vulnerable marine ecosystems (VMEs) within that framework. Second, it provides a brief overview of options for addressing fisheries in a potential new international agreement on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), which was under discussion at the time.

Methods
The paper is a policy and legal review rather than an empirical study. Its structure includes:

An outline of the global context, covering the expansion of high seas exploitation, the ecological impacts of bottom fishing, and the international regulatory framework (UNGA resolutions 61/105 and 64/72, the UNFSA, and the BBNJ working group process).
A description of the context and process for HSBFCs, including the four key obligations under UNGA Resolution 61/105 (impact assessments, management to prevent significant adverse impacts, "move-on" rules, and closures).
A region-by-region assessment of RFMO performance in the Atlantic, Pacific, Southern, and Indian oceans (Section 4; detailed text for this section is not fully included in the provided document).
An analysis of RFMO shortcomings and pathways for strengthening their role in biodiversity conservation, including the role of a potential new BBNJ agreement.


Key findings

Bottom fishing, especially bottom trawling, causes long-lasting or irreversible damage to deep-sea ecosystems: it destroys long-lived species, reduces seabed complexity, and decreases species diversity and faunal biomass. Recovery may take decades to centuries.
Progress by RFMOs in implementing HSBFCs has been slow. Closures have often been temporary, representative rather than comprehensive, or structured in ways that do not effectively restrict ongoing fishing activity.
Existing legal powers under the UNFSA and RFMO constitutions are not being fully utilised, and best practice is not consistently followed across regions.
Positive examples of good practice exist, particularly in the North East Atlantic (NEAFC) and the Southern Ocean (CCAMLR), but these are exceptions rather than the norm.
RFMOs are sectoral bodies and lack a comprehensive mandate to establish marine protected areas or address non-fishing threats to ABNJ biodiversity.
A new international agreement on high seas biodiversity could facilitate improvements by providing common overarching principles, objectives, and reporting requirements, and by addressing gaps that the existing RFMO framework leaves unaddressed.
RFMO measures, including HSBFCs, can only be one element of a broader framework for integrated conservation of ABNJ biodiversity.


Limitations

The paper is a policy review and does not present original empirical data or quantitative analysis of closure effectiveness.
The provided document text is partial: the full region-by-region assessment (Section 4) and the detailed discussion of RFMO shortcomings and improvement options (Section 5.1–5.2) are not included in the excerpt supplied, so specific regional findings and the authors' detailed recommendations cannot be summarised here.
The paper reflects the state of BBNJ negotiations as of late 2014 (eighth working group meeting); subsequent developments in the BBNJ process are outside its scope.
As a working paper from a single institute (IDDRI) with authors affiliated to advocacy-oriented organisations (IUCN, IASS), the framing is oriented toward strengthening conservation governance; the authors do not present a balanced assessment of the economic trade-offs for fishing nations.

