Objective
This issue brief presents the diplomatic discussions leading up to the third and final meeting of the BBNJ Working Group (20–23 January 2015), explains the historic agreement reached to open negotiations for a new international legally binding instrument on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), and outlines the procedural and substantive next steps.

Methods
The document is a policy analysis and issue brief rather than an empirical study. The authors (affiliated with IDDRI, IUCN, and IASS) review:

The timeline of BBNJ Working Group discussions from 2006 through January 2015.
The positions and coalitions of States (e.g., EU, G77+China, US, Russia, Canada, Japan, Pacific SIDS, African Union, CARICOM).
The text of the Working Group's recommendations to the UNGA, including the proposed two-step negotiation process.
The substantive scope of the instrument as defined by the 2011 "Package Deal."

The research received financial support from the French government under the "Investissements d'avenir" programme (ANR-10-LABX-14-01).

Key findings

After nearly a decade of discussions, States reached consensus to recommend that the UNGA open negotiations in 2016 for a legally binding instrument under UNCLOS dedicated to ABNJ biodiversity.
The process follows a two-step approach: (1) a Preparatory Committee (PrepCom) open to all UN Member States, specialized agencies, and observers will make substantive recommendations on draft-text elements by end of 2017; (2) the UNGA will decide by its 72nd session (September 2018) whether and when to convene an intergovernmental conference to elaborate the instrument.
Negotiations will address four topics from the 2011 Package Deal: marine genetic resources (including benefit-sharing), area-based management tools (including marine protected areas), environmental impact assessments, and capacity building and transfer of marine technology. No topic, including fisheries, was explicitly excluded.
Key points of contention included whether the instrument should be explicitly "legally binding" (fought for by EU, G77+China) versus a broader "international instrument" (preferred by US, Russia, Japan); the scope of the PrepCom mandate; and whether a fixed deadline should be set for the intergovernmental conference (no deadline was ultimately agreed).
The authors caution that the historic nature of the decision does not guarantee an easy negotiation, noting that several States (US, Canada, Japan, Russia) remained "unconvinced" of the need for a new instrument and that complex issues such as benefit-sharing for marine genetic resources and coordination with existing regional and sectoral frameworks will be challenging.
Continued sectoral and regional action to conserve ABNJ biodiversity is urged in parallel with the global negotiation process.


Limitations

The document is a policy issue brief, not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it contains no quantitative data, field observations, or statistical analysis.
It is a point-in-time snapshot (February 2015) written before the PrepCom and intergovernmental conference had taken place, so the actual outcome of the negotiations is not assessed.
The authors acknowledge that the outcome of the negotiations and the eventual entry into force of any new agreement remained unpredictable at the time of writing.
The analysis reflects the institutional and diplomatic context of the UNGA process and may not capture the full range of scientific evidence on ABNJ biodiversity threats.

