@techreport{Wright2016b,
	type = {Study},
	title = {High {Seas} {Fisheries}: {What} {Role} for a {New} {International} {Instrument}?},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	url = {https://www.iddri.org/en/publications-and-events/study/high-seas-fisheries-what-role-new-international-instrument},
	abstract = {States are currently discussing the development of a new international legally binding instrument (ILBI) on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). It is widely acknowledged that fishing is currently the activity with the largest impact on biodiversity in ABNJ. There is a growing consensus that a new agreement should include fisheries, though it is unclear how this can be achieved. Some States continue to argue that fisheries are adequately covered by existing frameworks. Fisheries are closely linked to all elements of the package of issues under discussion, particularly area-based management tools (ABMTs), including marine protected areas (MPAs) and environmental impact assessments (EIAs). Fisheries management bodies will therefore likely play a role in the implementation of various aspects of a new agreement. A new agreement can also complement and enhance existing fisheries management frameworks and contribute to advancing an integrated approach to ocean governance. An ILBI could improve integration and place complementary obligations on States, as well as provide over- arching principles to improve coherence of the global system of ocean governance.},
	urldate = {2026-09-29},
	institution = {IDDRI},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Rochette, Julien and Blom, Lucie and Currie, Duncan and Durussel, Carole and Gjerde, Kristina and Unger, Sebastian},
	year = {2016},
	note = {TITLE-CLEAN 2026-09-10: local:midword\_spaces;},
	keywords = {governance, fisheries, BBNJ, RFMO, not undermine, IFB, type:grey, author:Glen Wright, OA:open, grey-literature, mesopelagic, baseline-cites, from-iddri-2020},
	pages = {20},
	annote = {Objective
This 2016 IDDRI policy study by Wright, Rochette, Blom, Currie, Durussel, Gjerde, and Unger examines the role that fisheries could and should play in the new international legally binding instrument (ILBI) on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). The authors aim to demonstrate that there is both room and strong justification for integrating fisheries into the new agreement, and to outline concrete options for doing so.

Methods
The paper is a legal and policy analysis rather than an empirical study. The authors draw on:

Review of the existing regulatory framework for high seas fisheries (UNCLOS, UNFSA, FAO Port State Measures Agreement, RFMO mandates).
Analysis of the BBNJ Working Group deliberations, UNGA Resolution 69/292, and the first PrepCom (March–April 2016).
FAO stock assessments (2006, 2008, 2016) and UNFSA Review Conference reports (2010, 2016) on the state of high seas fish stocks.
Review of the ocean governance literature (Fanning, Mahon, Young, Ban et al.) on institutional interplay and systematic conservation planning.
Proposal of specific legal options for fisheries inclusion, structured around "package deal" elements and "overarching provisions."


Key findings

Fishing is widely acknowledged as the activity with the largest impact on biodiversity in ABNJ, yet some States still argue existing frameworks are sufficient.
FAO data cited in the paper indicate 58.1\% of global fish stocks are fully fished and 31.4\% are overfished; roughly 30\% of highly migratory tuna and over 50\% of oceanic sharks are overexploited or depleted.
Fisheries are closely linked to all elements of the BBNJ package, particularly area-based management tools (ABMTs), marine protected areas (MPAs), and environmental impact assessments (EIAs).
A new ILBI could complement and enhance existing fisheries management by: defining a common purpose for ABNJ biodiversity; establishing guiding principles (precaution, science-based management, ecosystem approach, transparency, equity); placing complementary obligations on flag States; and providing a default precautionary management regime where RFMO geographic gaps exist.
The ILBI could harmonise the ecosystem approach to fisheries (EAF) across RFMOs and institutionalise a regular global review and reporting process for RFMO performance.
The instrument could contribute to the progressive development of international law by restating existing guidelines (e.g., PSMA Article 20(3), FAO Guidelines Article 50) as concrete obligations.


Limitations

The document is a 20-page policy brief, not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it does not present original data collection or statistical analysis.
The FAO figures cited do not provide precise stock-status data specific to ABNJ; the authors note the situation is "more critical" for high seas resources but lack granular data.
The negotiation process was still in its early stages (first PrepCom, 2016), so the proposed options are speculative and subject to political change.
The paper does not address the economic or livelihood impacts of stricter fisheries obligations on developing States or small-scale fishers.
Some of the cited literature (e.g., Ban et al. 2014, Mahon et al. 2015) is referenced but only partially reproduced in the provided text, limiting full verification of supporting arguments.

