Objective
This chapter, contributed to a Springer volume on legal aspects of sustainable development (2016), aims to identify and analyse persistent gaps in the international legal framework governing the sustainable use of the oceans. Specifically, it focuses on two areas: (1) the absence of a coherent regulatory regime for areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), including marine protected areas (MPAs), environmental impact assessment (EIA), and access and benefit sharing for marine genetic resources (MGRs); and (2) the lack of a legally binding international agreement on liability and compensation for pollution damage from offshore oil and gas activities. The authors outline ongoing multilateral processes to address these gaps and propose concrete ways forward.

Methods
The chapter is a normative legal and policy analysis rather than an empirical study. The authors:

Review the architecture of existing international instruments, including UNCLOS (1982), the Convention on Biological Diversity (1992), MARPOL (1973), the London Convention (1972), the UN Fish Stocks Agreement (2001), and the Sustainable Development Goals (2014).
Trace the institutional history of the BBNJ Working Group (established 2004) and the "Package Deal" negotiations leading to the January 2015 decision to open negotiations for a new binding instrument under UNCLOS.
Examine sectoral mechanisms for area-based management in ABNJ (IMO PSSAs, ISA APEIs, RFMO closures, CBD EBSAs) to identify coordination shortcomings.
Propose design elements for a future global liability and compensation regime for offshore oil and gas pollution, drawing on comparative legal scholarship and lessons from the Deepwater Horizon disaster.


Key findings

ABNJ, covering roughly half the planet's surface, lacks any overarching regulatory framework. There is no global mechanism for establishing cross-sectoral MPAs, no mandatory EIA regime, and no agreed rules on benefit sharing for MGRs.
Despite commitments at the 2002 World Summit and the 2012 Rio+20 conference, less than 3\% of the ocean is designated as MPAs, and only about 0.5\% of ABNJ is protected, falling far short of the Aichi Target 11 goal of 10\% by 2020.
The January 2015 UNGA decision to open negotiations for a new legally binding instrument (likely an implementing agreement to UNCLOS) represents a historic step, but significant challenges remain in translating the Package Deal into a workable treaty.
Offshore oil and gas remains one of the least regulated maritime industries. No legally binding international agreement governs liability and compensation for pollution from fixed platforms.
The authors propose a future regime built on strict operator liability, joint and several liability among license holders and subcontractors, compulsory third-party insurance, a calibrated liability cap with a supplementary compensation fund, and broad judicial settlement mechanisms.


Limitations

The chapter is a policy-oriented legal analysis within an edited volume; it does not present original empirical data, field research, or quantitative modelling.
The provided text is truncated in several sections (marked with ellipses), so portions of the discussion on MGRs, EIA design, and the full oil-and-gas liability analysis are incomplete in the source material supplied here.
The proposals are forward-looking and contingent on political will; the chapter does not assess the likelihood of adoption or the enforceability of the suggested instruments.
The analysis is framed primarily from a Western, state-centric perspective on international law; the views of small island developing states, indigenous communities, and the private sector are not independently examined.

