@techreport{Iucn2018c,
	title = {Laying the {Foundations} for {Management} of a {Seamount} {Beyond} {National} {Jurisdiction}: {A} case study of the {Walters} {Shoal} in the {South} {West} {Indian} {Ocean}},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	url = {https://www.iddri.org/en/publications-and-events/report/laying-foundations-management-seamount-beyond-national-jurisdiction},
	institution = {IDDRI, IUCN, FFEM},
	author = {Guduff, Sabrina and Rochette, Julien and Simard, François and Spadone, Aurélie and Wright, Glen},
	year = {2018},
	note = {Issue: June},
	keywords = {BBNJ, ABMT / MPAs, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	pages = {1--40},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This report, produced under an IUCN-led project funded by the French Global Environment Facility (FFEM), aims to lay the groundwork for a management plan for the Walters Shoal seamount in the South West Indian Ocean (SWIO), an area beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). Specifically, it seeks to (1) describe the ecological characteristics of the Walters Shoal, (2) review the existing governance framework for the regional marine environment, and (3) identify possible options for the conservation and management of the site to support future decision-making.

Methods

A 26-day multidisciplinary expedition aboard the R/V Marion Dufresne (April–May 2017) collected data on benthic and pelagic communities, marine mammals, seabirds, large predators, and environmental/oceanographic conditions.
The expedition complemented earlier sampling campaigns on SWIO seamounts in 1964 (R/V Anton Bruun), 1988 (Vityaz), and 2009 (R/V Dr. Fridtjof Nansen).
A desk-based review of the legal and institutional governance framework was conducted, covering the Nairobi Convention, regional fisheries bodies (SIOFA, IOTC, SWIOFC), and sectoral organisations (IMO, International Seabed Authority, UNESCO/World Heritage Convention).
Options for conservation and management were assessed, including area-based management tools, marine protected areas, and dissociated water-column/seabed management.


Key findings

The Walters Shoal, located 700 km off southern Madagascar at the southern end of the Madagascar Ridge, consists of a group of seamounts and domes with its highest summit only 18 m below the surface. Its shallow depth results in a less diversified fish fauna compared to deeper SWIO seamounts, likely due to reduced food availability and the absence of complex reef structures.
The interim benthic survey recorded approximately 500 species, of which around 100 are likely new to science.
Major threats to the Walters Shoal include bottom-trawl fishing and deep-sea mining.
The governance landscape is fragmented: the Nairobi Convention has a mandate over ABNJ but limited enforcement tools; regional fisheries bodies (SIOFA, IOTC) can adopt conservation and management measures; and sectoral bodies (IMO, ISA, UNESCO) address specific activities. In 2016, improved coordination was noted among the Nairobi Convention, SWIOFC, and IOTC.
Several conservation options were identified: limiting fishing and mining impacts, establishing a marine protected area (via the Nairobi Convention, a coalition-based approach, or World Heritage inscription), and pursuing dissociated management of the water column versus the seabed.
Priority measures recommended include strengthening scientific knowledge, enhancing fisheries management, discussing extension of the Nairobi Convention into ABNJ, and improving inter-organisational cooperation.
The UNGA resolution of 24 December 2017 opening negotiations for a legally binding instrument on ABNJ biodiversity provides a broader policy context for these regional efforts.


Limitations

Analyses of pelagic (water column) samples and of marine mammal and seabird observation data were still in progress at the time of writing, so those findings are not yet available.
Globally, fewer than 300 seamounts have been studied extensively, and key hypotheses about seamount ecosystem functioning (e.g., Taylor's Column, magnetic attraction) remain unverified by field data.
The SWIO region is the least studied of the Indian Ocean's three mid-oceanic ridge systems, limiting the ecological baseline.
The report is explicitly a foundational document; it does not itself constitute a management plan or a binding legal instrument.
Several sections of the provided text (e.g., detailed discussion of fishing and mining threats, and the full treatment of MPA options) are incomplete or truncated, so the full scope of the authors' analysis could not be assessed here.

