@techreport{Durussel2018,
	title = {Strengthening {Regional} {Ocean} {Governance} for the {High} {Seas}: {Opportunities} and {Challenges} to {Improve} the {Legal} and {Institutional} {Framework} of the {Southeast} {Atlantic} and {Southeast} {Pacific}},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	url = {https://glenwright.earth/library/strengthening-regional-ocean-governance-high-seas-opportunities-challenges-improve-legal-institutional-framework-southeast-atlantic-southeast-pacific/},
	doi = {10.2312/iass.2018.025},
	abstract = {The Southeast Atlantic and Southeast Pacific regions are both characterised by their high biological productivity, supported by important oceanic currents. Recognising the need to ensure conservation and sustainable use of this biodiversity, coastal States in these regions cooperate through regional organisations to improve ocean governance, including in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). This report uses the issues under discussion in the ongoing negotiations for a new legally binding instrument and selected Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) targets as a lens through which to assess progress towards conservation and sustainable use.},
	language = {en},
	institution = {STRONG High Seas},
	author = {Durussel, Carole and Wright, Glen and Wienrich, Nicole and Boteler, Ben and Unger, Sebastian and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2018},
	note = {Publication Title: Publication Database GFZ (GFZ German Research Centre for Geosciences)},
	keywords = {governance, fisheries, BBNJ, RFMO, not undermine, IFB, type:grey, author:Glen Wright, OA:open, regional governance, mesopelagic, baseline-cites, from-iass-2021},
	pages = {92},
	annote = {Objective
This 2018 report by Durussel, Wright, Wienrich, Boteler, Unger, and Rochette (STRONG High Seas Project, IASS/IDDRI) reviews the legal and institutional frameworks governing high seas biodiversity in the Southeast Atlantic and Southeast Pacific regions. It aims to assess progress toward conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ) and to identify opportunities and challenges for strengthening regional ocean governance. The work supports the Partnership for Regional Ocean Governance (PROG) and the ongoing UN negotiations for a new legally binding instrument on biodiversity beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ).

Methods
The report employs a transdisciplinary review approach, using two analytical lenses:

The issues under discussion in the ongoing UN negotiations for a new legally binding BBNJ agreement (area-based management tools, environmental impact assessments, marine genetic resources, capacity building and transfer of marine technology).
Selected Sustainable Development Goal 14 targets (14.1 marine pollution; 14.2 and 14.5 management and protection of marine ecosystems; 14.4 IUU fishing).

The authors examine the mandates, membership, and treaty ratification status of key regional organisations in both regions, including the Permanent Commission for the South Pacific (CPPS), the Abidjan Convention, SEAFO, SPRFMO, IATTC, ICCAT, and the Benguela Current Commission. Annexes provide detailed tables of membership and agreement ratification by coastal states.

Key findings
The report identifies both progress and persistent gaps:

Considerable efforts have been made to address IUU fishing, marine pollution, and scientific cooperation in both regions.
Legal and implementation gaps remain that hamper efficient management of ABNJ ecosystems and resources.
There is limited adoption of legally binding management measures outside the fisheries sector, and limited coordination between competent organisations.
Member states are diverse in culture, language, and available capacity, particularly in the Southeast Atlantic.
Participation in regional and global agreements is varied and uneven, making it difficult to fully address BBNJ issues without an adequate legal or institutional basis.
Regional organisations have varying, non-comprehensive, or limited mandates to address BBNJ issues.
Cross-sectoral cooperation is limited; individual organisations adopt their own principles and recommendations in isolation.

The authors propose several options for strengthening governance, including advancing cross-sectoral coordination (joint programmes, memoranda of understanding), implementing common regional conservation policies, championing flag State responsibility for gaps in management, forming coalitions to promote BBNJ interests, empowering regional seas programmes to consider ABNJ, and building a robust scientific and capacity base.

Limitations
The document does not include a dedicated limitations section. However, the following constraints are apparent from the text:

The assessment is a policy review rather than an empirical study; it relies on existing legal texts, treaty ratification data, and institutional descriptions rather than primary field data.
The scope is limited to two specific regions (Southeast Atlantic and Southeast Pacific), so findings may not generalise to other high seas areas.
The BBNJ negotiation context is time-bound to 2018; subsequent developments (e.g., the 2023 adoption of the High Seas Treaty) are not reflected.
The report acknowledges that the extent and scope of CPPS competence over adjacent high seas is "not clearly legally defined," indicating residual uncertainty in the legal analysis.

