@techreport{Wright2019a,
	type = {Issue {Brief}},
	title = {High {Hopes} for the {High} {Seas}: beyond the package deal towards an ambitious treaty},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	url = {https://www.iddri.org/en/publications-and-events/issue-brief/high-hopes-high-seas-beyond-package-deal-towards-ambitious},
	abstract = {Cognisant of the growing threats to biodiversity in marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), States at the United Nations are negotiating a treaty to ensure the conservation and sustainable use of this vast global commons. These negotiations provide a unique and timely opportunity to strengthen the management regime for the global ocean, building on the vision of the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS). The treaty will cover a ‘package deal’ of issues: marine genetic resources (MGRs); area-based manage- ment tools (ABMTs), including marine protected areas (MPAs); environmental impact assessments (EIAs); and capacity building and technology transfer. In order to be effective, ambitious provisions are needed on each of these elements, including by addressing climate change and ensuring the protection of marine ecosystems. A fair and equitable treaty could further support conservation and sustainable use by strengthening existing management frameworks and providing global oversight, developing capacity, and placing science at the heart of decision making.},
	number = {9},
	urldate = {2026-09-29},
	institution = {IDDRI},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Cremers, Klaudija and Rochette, Julien and Clark, Nichola and Dunn, Daniel and Gjerde, Kristina M and Harden-davies, Harriet and Mohammed, Essam and Crespo, Guillermo Ortuño},
	year = {2019},
	keywords = {governance, fisheries, BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright, EIA / SEA, grey-literature, mesopelagic, baseline-cites, from-iddri-2020},
	pages = {8},
	annote = {Objective
This 2019 IDDRI policy brief advocates for an ambitious, effective, and equitable international legally binding instrument (ILBI) for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). It argues that the ongoing UN negotiations should go beyond the four "package deal" elements—marine genetic resources (MGRs), area-based management tools (ABMTs), environmental impact assessments (EIAs), and capacity building/technology transfer—to strengthen the overall governance framework for the global ocean, building on the vision of UNCLOS.

Methods
This document is a policy brief rather than an empirical study. It synthesizes existing literature on ocean governance, reviews current institutional frameworks (UNCLOS, RFMOs, IMO, ISA, CBD, UNFSA), and identifies gaps in the current regime. Recommendations are drawn from prior outputs of the STRONG High Seas project and referenced assessments (e.g., FAO 2018, OBIS data, Juan-Jordá et al. 2018). The authors propose specific treaty provisions across ten thematic areas. No original data collection or statistical analysis is reported.

Key findings
The brief identifies the following core gaps and recommendations:

Fragmented governance: Sectoral and regional bodies (RFMOs, IMO, ISA) operate in silos with little coordination, making cumulative-impact management and ecosystem-based management practically impossible.
Global oversight needed: A treaty should empower a global body to make multi-sector, ecosystem-based decisions, provide independent scientific review, and mandate cooperation among competent organizations.
Climate change integration: Climate change, ocean acidification, and deoxygenation should be explicitly addressed in ABMT design, EIA requirements, and capacity-building provisions.
Equity concerns: Ten developed countries account for 71\% of high seas catches and 98\% of MGR patents. The treaty should link resource access to technology transfer, establish benefit-sharing mechanisms, and consider the needs of LDCs and SIDS.
MPA designation: No global mechanism currently exists for cross-sectoral ABMTs. The treaty should enable designation of fully protected marine reserves and support marine spatial planning.
Fisheries management: RFMOs have focused almost exclusively on target stocks; approximately 45\% of highly migratory stocks are unsustainably fished. The treaty should promote ecosystem-based approaches, bycatch limits, and vessel tracking.
Science and data: Knowledge of ABNJ is severely limited. The treaty should mandate data submission to OBIS, cross-sectoral transparency standards, and a dedicated scientific body.
Monitoring, control, and surveillance (MCS): The treaty should standardize MCS rules across RFMOs, require MCS strategies in ABMT proposals, and leverage tools like Global Fishing Watch.


Limitations
The document does not include a dedicated limitations section. However, the following can be noted from the text itself:

The brief is a policy advocacy document, not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it presents normative recommendations rather than testable hypotheses.
It was written in August 2019 while negotiations were ongoing, so all recommendations are aspirational and subject to political compromise.
The authors are affiliated with organizations (IDDRI, Pew Charitable Trusts, IUCN, IIED) with stated positions on ocean governance, which may shape the framing of recommendations.
The brief does not model or quantify the likely effectiveness of the proposed provisions, nor does it assess trade-offs among the ten thematic areas.

