@techreport{Wright2018e,
	title = {Keeping an {Eye} on the {High} {Seas} {Strengthening} {Monitoring}, {Control} and {Surveillance} through a {New} {Marine} {Biodiversity} {Treaty}},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	url = {https://www.iddri.org/en/publications-and-events/report/keeping-eye-high-seas},
	doi = {10.2312/iass.2018.015},
	abstract = {Effective monitoring, control and surveillance (MCS) is critical for the success of marine conservation and management measures. Whereas States have the exclusive right to manage the marine resources within their national jurisdiction, areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ) are subject to a complex patchwork of international rules and regulations. Effective MCS of these deep and distant waters is a significant technical challenge and there is growing interest in how MCS tools and policies can be applied to this vast global commons. States at the United Nations (UN) are currently negotiating an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) for the conservation and sustainable use of the biological diversity of marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (referred to here as the “high seas treaty”). This brief explores how the negotiations could strengthen MCS in ABNJ and the contribution MCS could make to the implementation of a future treaty.},
	institution = {STRONG High Seas},
	author = {Cremers, Klaudija and Wright, Glen and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2019},
	keywords = {fisheries, BBNJ, RFMO, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	pages = {14},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This policy brief by Cremers, Wright, and Rochette (2019; DOI: 10.2312/iass.2018.015) examines how ongoing United Nations negotiations for an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) on marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ)—commonly called the "high seas treaty"—could strengthen monitoring, control, and surveillance (MCS). It also assesses the contribution MCS could make to implementing a future treaty. The work is part of the STRONG High Seas project, a five-year initiative (2017–2022) coordinated by the Institute for Advanced Sustainability Studies (IASS) in partnership with IDDRI, IUCN, and others, focused on strengthening regional ocean governance for ABNJ conservation.

Methods
The brief is a policy and legal analysis rather than an empirical study. Its evidence base draws on:

Review of the existing legal framework governing ABNJ, including UNCLOS, the UN Fish Stocks Agreement, the Port State Measures Agreement, the Cape Town Agreement, and relevant "soft law" instruments across three sectors: high seas fishing, shipping/navigation, and seabed minerals.
Assessment of current MCS tools (e.g., vessel monitoring systems, automatic identification systems, remote sensing, synthetic aperture radar, environmental DNA) and their applicability to ABNJ.
Survey of ongoing initiatives, including RFMO practices, the International Monitoring, Control and Surveillance Network (IMCS), fisheries improvement projects, and civil society efforts.
Findings from three dedicated MCS expert workshops (Paris, July 2018; Paris, April 2019; Guayaquil, November 2019) and an MCS session at the Marine Regions Forum in Berlin (October/November 2019).


Key findings

Effective MCS in ABNJ is a significant technical and governance challenge due to the vastness of the area, the complex patchwork of sectoral rules, and the absence of a single overarching authority.
Three core challenges impede effective MCS: weak flag-state responsibility (including flags of convenience), fragmented governance across sectors, and insufficient capacity among many states.
Innovative technologies (satellite tracking, remote sensing, eDNA, drones) have transformative potential to close surveillance gaps in deep and distant waters.
Co-creation of MCS systems with stakeholders, strengthened policy responses, and market-leveraging mechanisms (e.g., trade measures against IUU fishing) are complementary approaches.
The authors propose three concrete measures for the new ILBI: (1) reinforcing MCS obligations and principles in the treaty text; (2) developing a strong role for the clearing-house mechanism in facilitating MCS cooperation and information sharing; and (3) incorporating an explicit MCS strategy for area-based management tools (ABMTs) such as marine protected areas.
General obligations in the treaty—cooperation and coordination, transparency, and reporting—should be strengthened to underpin effective MCS across all package-deal components (marine genetic resources, ABMTs, environmental impact assessments, capacity building, and technology transfer).


Limitations

The document is a policy brief, not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it offers normative recommendations rather than quantitative evidence of MCS effectiveness.
The full text of Sections 3–8 (MCS tools in detail, state of play, challenges, approaches, and the ILBI-specific proposals) is not included in the provided excerpt; the summary above relies on the table of contents, partial executive summary, and front matter.
The analysis reflects the state of UN BBNJ negotiations as of 2019–2020; subsequent treaty developments (the 2023 adoption of the High Seas Treaty) are not addressed.
The brief is produced within the STRONG High Seas project, which focuses on the Southeast Atlantic and Southeast Pacific regions; generalizability to all ABNJ regions may be limited.
No information on funding conflicts of interest beyond the stated support from the German Federal Ministry for the Environment (BMU) via the International Climate Initiative is provided.

