@inproceedings{Rochette2014c,
	address = {Potsdam, Germany},
	title = {Governing the “{High} {Seas}” - {Linking} global governance and regional implementation},
	url = {https://glenwright.earth/library/governing-high-seas-linking-global-governance-regional-implementation/},
	abstract = {Marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABJN), often referred to as the “High Seas1”, represent around half of the Planet’s surface and host a significant portion of its biodiversity. Despite their global importance, there are significant governance gaps that impede effective conservation and sustainable use. Since the beginning of the 21st century, States have started to discuss, in various arenas, integrated approaches to the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in ABNJ. This debate was prompted by increased evidence of threats to these areas (e.g. through overexploitation of living marine resources, climate change, ocean acidification or pollution). At the same time, new scientific discoveries demonstrated the importance of marine genetic resources in ABNJ. Following a commitment from the Rio +20 UN Conference on Sustainable Development, current global discussions focus on the opportunity to elaborate a new international agreement under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) (an “Implementing Agreement”). In parallel, some Regional Seas Conventions have progressively extended their activities into ABNJ. In this regard, the most advanced efforts take place in the Mediterranean Sea, the North-East Atlantic and the Southern Ocean, where MPAs have already been designated in ABNJ. This workshop will explore the relationship between global (i.e. international conventions and organisations) and regional level governance in ABNJ (i.e. Regional Seas programmes, Regional Fisheries Management Organisations and other regional mechanisms such as Large Marine Ecosystems) and consider options for filling governance gaps.},
	booktitle = {Potsdam {Ocean} {Governance} {Workshop}},
	author = {Rochette, Julien and Unger, Sebastian and Wright, Glen},
	year = {2014},
	keywords = {BBNJ, ABMT / MPAs, not undermine, IFB, author:Glen Wright, OA:open, regional governance},
	pages = {1--14},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This issue paper, prepared as a background document for the 2014 Potsdam Ocean Governance Workshop, aims to explore the relationship between global governance (international conventions and organisations) and regional implementation (Regional Seas programmes, Regional Fisheries Management Organisations, and Large Marine Ecosystem mechanisms) in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). Specifically, it seeks to (1) analyse the role of regional organisations within a possible new international instrument under UNCLOS, and (2) identify options for regional organisations to engage in the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in ABNJ. The underlying assumption is that global and regional legal instruments would complement each other: a global agreement would strengthen the legal mandate for regional activities, while regional organisations would facilitate implementation of the overarching global framework.

Methods
The paper is a workshop background document combining a structured review of existing governance frameworks with a case-study analysis. It draws on UNCLOS provisions, the 1995 Fish Stocks Agreement, and the 2011 BBNJ "package deal" to frame the institutional landscape. A table-based scenario analysis examines institutional options for each stage of MPA establishment (scientific basis, proposal, designation, management, implementation, and review). The paper then reviews four regional case studies in detail: the OSPAR/NEAFC approach in the North-East Atlantic, the Pelagos Sanctuary in the Mediterranean, the CCAMLR approach in the Southern Ocean, and the Sargasso Sea Commission. It also surveys additional regional initiatives (South Pacific, South East Pacific, Western Africa, Western Indian Ocean) in a comparative table. The analysis is informed by a broad literature review of marine governance scholarship published primarily in 2013–2014.

Key findings

ABNJ covers roughly half the planet's surface and hosts significant biodiversity, yet governance gaps impede effective conservation and sustainable use.
Replicating existing regional efforts through a new global instrument would likely duplicate work, stretch limited funding, and further fragment the oceans governance landscape; regional organisations will therefore have a role in any future UNCLOS Implementing Agreement.
The most advanced regional ABNJ efforts are in the Mediterranean (Pelagos Sanctuary), the North-East Atlantic (OSPAR MPA network, {\textasciitilde}465,000 km²), and the Southern Ocean (South Orkney Islands MPA via CCAMLR).
Cooperation between competent bodies (e.g., OSPAR and NEAFC via a "Collective Arrangement") is most successful when initiated early and developed into an ongoing process.
"Ingredients for success" in regional ABNJ initiatives include agreed criteria for MPA selection, political champions, related policy goals, and long-term institutional commitment.
For regions beginning ABNJ engagement, a step-by-step approach—starting with scientific evidence, then limited protective measures by champion States, and potentially expanding the convention area—is recommended.
Interregional cooperation (e.g., twinning arrangements between OSPAR and the Abidjan Convention) could help share expertise and build momentum.


Limitations

The paper is a workshop background document rather than a peer-reviewed research article; its purpose is to frame discussion rather than present original empirical findings.
The case-study analysis focuses exclusively on MPAs, which is only one element of the broader 2011 BBNJ "package deal" (which also covers marine genetic resources, environmental impact assessments, capacity-building, and technology transfer).
The specific role of existing regional mechanisms within any future UNCLOS Implementing Agreement is explicitly stated as "yet to be determined."
Most Regional Seas programmes do not yet extend into ABNJ, and non-universal participation in regional agreements remains a structural challenge.
Capacity limitations in many regional bodies constrain their ability to tackle ABNJ challenges effectively.
The institutional options presented in the scenario table are described as "not necessarily mutually exclusive" and require further development into a coherent framework.
The authors note that even the most advanced regional examples are "still to be considered work in progress," and there is currently no systematic reflection of regional instruments within the BBNJ Working Group deliberations.

