@techreport{Warner2018,
	type = {Policy {Brief}},
	title = {Strategic {Environmental} {Assessment} ({SEA}). {Envisioning} its {Application} to {Marine} {Areas} beyond {National} {Jurisdiction} ({ABNJ})},
	url = {www.dosi-project.org},
	urldate = {2020-08-20},
	institution = {DOSI},
	author = {Warner, Robin and Wright, Glen and Turner, Phillip and Ortuño Crespo, Guillermo and Dunn, Daniel and Singh, Gerald},
	year = {2018},
	keywords = {EIA, SEA, BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, type:grey, author:Glen Wright},
	pages = {4},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2018 policy brief from the Deep-Ocean Stewardship Initiative (DOSI) aims to envision how Strategic Environmental Assessment (SEA) could be applied to marine Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ). It seeks to explain the SEA process, distinguish it from project-level Environmental Impact Assessments (EIAs), and propose how SEA provisions could be embedded in the forthcoming International Legally Binding Instrument (ILBI) under UNCLOS for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in ABNJ.

Methods
The brief is a policy and governance analysis rather than an empirical study. The authors draw on existing international frameworks and protocols to structure their argument:

Review of the 2003 Kiev Protocol on Strategic Environmental Assessment in a Transboundary Context to identify core SEA elements (environmental effects reporting, public participation, and policy review).
Comparison of SEA and EIA in terms of scope, temporal scale, and decision-making level.
Identification of three categories of analytical tools: predicting environmental and socio-economic effects (modelling, GIS, network analysis), ensuring stakeholder engagement (consultation surveys, consensus building), and analysing/comparing options (scenario analysis, multi-criteria analysis, cost-benefit analysis).
Proposal of three interlocking frameworks for an ABNJ SEA: a problem framework (mapping biodiversity, EBSAs, economic potential, cumulative impacts), a governance framework (institutional responsibilities, relevant treaty bodies, RFMOs, RSAs, IGOs, NGOs), and a strategic reference framework (linking to SDGs, RIO+20, UNCLOS, CBD, and the ILBI).
Discussion of the role of ocean observing systems (GOOS Regional Alliances, DOOS) in supplying baseline data.


Key findings

SEA is a proactive, cross-sectoral process that evaluates environmental implications of plans, programmes, and policies at broader geographic and temporal scales than a single-project EIA, and it fully embodies the precautionary approach.
SEA is especially valuable for ABNJ because multiple human activities occur concurrently over long timeframes, creating cumulative impacts that project-level EIAs cannot capture.
SEA and EIA should be vertically integrated: SEA establishes environmental objectives and baseline information that inform project-level EIAs. In practice, this integration has not consistently occurred, and in some ABNJ sectors EIAs have already been undertaken without a broader environmental vision.
Under the ILBI, a provision could require States Parties (individually or as a group) to undertake an SEA before proposing a new plan, programme, or policy. A neutral third-party funding pool and independent consultants could minimise bias, with the ILBI's Scientific/Technical Committee reviewing SEA report quality.
Because no specific SEA sequence or method has been mandated in state practice to date, the ILBI offers an opportunity to outline an adaptable regional process.
Implementing SEAs in ABNJ requires high-degree collaboration among States Parties, global and regional organisations, and may complement rather than displace existing environmental assessment regimes.


Limitations

The vast geographic extent of the open ocean and the current mosaic of sectoral management bodies create significant resource and logistic challenges.
Substantial knowledge gaps persist regarding deep-sea biodiversity, limiting the accuracy of baseline assessments.
Governance frameworks for ABNJ are sparse and fragmentary, complicating coordinated SEA implementation.
Limited resources and technical capacities among many States Parties constrain practical execution.
Many activities in ABNJ are still in a nascent stage, making long-term impact prediction uncertain.
At the larger scales required for SEA, assessments will necessarily depend more on models and proxies rather than direct observation, reducing certainty.
The authors acknowledge that SEAs for particular ABNJ sectors and regions may only evolve over time, meaning the proposed framework remains aspirational rather than operational.

