@article{gjerdeGettingYesFasttracking2022,
	title = {Getting beyond yes: fast-tracking implementation of the {United} {Nations} agreement for marine biodiversity beyond national jurisdiction},
	url = {https://www.nature.com/articles/s44183-022-00006-2},
	doi = {10.1038/s44183-022-00006-2},
	abstract = {With a new international agreement on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ Agreement) on the horizon, now is the time to start laying the foundation for successful implementation. This paper provides some initial reflections for supporting rapid, effective, and equitable implementation of the BBNJ Agreement in three priority areas: (1) bringing the Agreement into force; (2) establishing the institutional framework, including financial mechanisms; and (3) developing capacity, science, and technology. With reference to selected examples from other international processes, the paper makes suggestions for encouraging wide ratification of the BBNJ Agreement, establishing a Preparatory Commission (PrepCom), mobilizing resources, and building partnerships to advance science and capacity. The growing impacts of climate change and human activities on the global ocean necessitate urgent action, so we must begin to work on the implementation of the BBNJ Agreement as soon as possible to secure ocean health for the benefit of present and future generations.},
	number = {6},
	journal = {npj Ocean Sustainability},
	author = {Gjerde, Kristina M. and Clark, Nichola A. and Chazot, Clément and Cremers, Klaudija and Harden-Davies, Harriet and Kachelriess, Daniel and Payne, Cymie R. and Rodriguez-Chaves, Mariamalia and Spadone, Aurélie and Thiele, Torsten and Vierros, Marjo and Goettsche-Wanli, Gabriele and Wright, Glen},
	year = {2022},
	note = {Merged duplicates into this item; trashed: BZK3ZQXH
TITLE-CLEAN 2026-09-10: crossref:10.1038/s44183-022-00006-2;},
	keywords = {BBNJ, bbnj-architecture-corpus, architecture, cooperation modalities, not undermine, IFB, author:Glen Wright, OA:open, bbnj-dedupe-2026-09-08},
	annote = {Objective
This 2022 comment paper in npj Ocean Sustainability provides initial reflections on supporting rapid, effective, and equitable implementation of the forthcoming United Nations BBNJ Agreement (on conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction). The authors identify three priority areas: (1) bringing the Agreement into force through sufficient ratifications; (2) establishing the institutional framework, including financial mechanisms; and (3) developing capacity, science, and technology. The paper urges that implementation work begin as soon as possible given the growing impacts of climate change and human activities on the global ocean.

Methods
This is a policy comment/opinion paper rather than an empirical study. The authors draw on their extensive and diverse experience supporting intergovernmental processes, including UNCLOS, the UNFCCC, CITES, and the BBNJ negotiations themselves. They reference selected examples from other international treaty processes (e.g., the Ballast Water Management Convention, the Nagoya Protocol, the Montreal Protocol, the UN Fish Stocks Agreement, and the Port State Measures Agreement) to illustrate practical strategies for ratification, institutional building, and capacity development. No systematic data collection or analytical methodology is described.

Key findings
The paper offers a set of recommendations and observations rather than empirical findings. Key points include:

The existing patchwork of global and regional ocean governance instruments is ill-equipped to manage cumulative impacts of human activities and climate stressors (ocean warming, acidification, deoxygenation, marine heatwaves).
The BBNJ Agreement, as the third implementing agreement under UNCLOS, will address four topics: marine genetic resources and benefit-sharing, area-based management tools (including MPAs), environmental impact assessments, and capacity building and marine technology transfer.
Awareness raising at national, regional, and global levels—through workshops, webinars, and high-level events—is critical to securing wide ratification.
Private-sector partnerships (e.g., the Global Industry Alliance for the Ballast Water Management Convention) can facilitate entry into force and implementation.
Legal codification tools such as model laws, checklists, and legislative templates (as used by CITES, FAO, and the World Bank) can help States translate treaty obligations into national law.
Dedicated financial and technical support (e.g., GEF support for the Nagoya Protocol) can accelerate ratification, but the UN Fish Stocks Agreement's Assistance Fund has been consistently depleted, serving as a cautionary example.
A Preparatory Commission (PrepCom) should be established to prepare for the first Conference of Parties, draft rules of procedure, and consider financial needs.
Capacity needs assessments and gap analyses (as done for CITES marine species and the Nagoya Protocol) are essential to identify legal, administrative, institutional, and technical implementation needs.


Limitations

The paper is a comment/opinion piece, not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it presents "initial reflections" rather than systematic analysis or evidence-based conclusions.
At the time of writing, the BBNJ Agreement had not yet been concluded (expected early 2023), so recommendations are necessarily anticipatory and contingent on the final treaty text.
The document text provided is truncated (indicated by ellipses), meaning sections on institutional mechanisms, effective conservation in ABNJ, and the conclusion are incomplete or absent from the available text.
The authors declare no competing interests, but their institutional affiliations (IUCN, Pew Charitable Trusts, Sea Shepherd Legal, Global Ocean Trust, etc.) reflect advocacy positions that may shape the framing of recommendations.
No formal limitations section is included in the paper itself.

