@techreport{Wright2022,
	title = {Conserving the {Global} {Ocean}: {Initial} {Indications} for {Effective} {Area}-based {Management} {Tools} on the {High} {Seas}},
	url = {www.prog-ocean.org/our-work/strong-high-seas/},
	urldate = {2022-10-05},
	institution = {STRONG High Seas},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Bouvet, Morgane and Cremers, Klaudija and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2022},
	keywords = {BBNJ, ABMT / MPAs, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2022 policy report by Wright, Bouvet, Cremers, and Rochette (IDDRI, STRONG High Seas Project) aims to provide initial indications for how the international community can build on existing governance frameworks, consolidate progress in the BBNJ (marine biodiversity beyond national jurisdiction) negotiations, and lay a foundation for the effective deployment of area-based management tools (ABMTs) and marine protected areas (MPAs) in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), which comprise approximately half the Earth's surface.

Methods
The report is a policy synthesis and reflective analysis rather than an empirical study. Its structure includes:

An overview of existing ABMTs, distinguishing single-sector tools (e.g., fisheries closures, seabed mining regulations) from cross-sectoral tools such as MPAs and marine spatial planning (MSP).
A review of selected high seas management examples: fisheries management via RFMOs, cooperation in the North-East Atlantic (NEAFC/OSPAR), the Sargasso Sea, and the Southern Ocean (CCAMLR).
A set of seven "initial indications" or recommendations for advancing ABMTs through the BBNJ agreement.
Building on prior STRONG High Seas research on ecosystem-based management, environmental impact assessments, monitoring/control/surveillance, and compliance mechanisms.

The analysis draws on the BBNJ draft text published in November 2019 and the state of negotiations as of the fourth intergovernmental conference (March 2022).

Key findings

No global mechanism currently exists for the designation and coordination of MPAs and other ABMTs in ABNJ; filling this gap is a core component of the BBNJ negotiations.
ABNJ biodiversity is threatened by growing resource exploitation, climate change, ocean acidification, pollution, and emerging activities such as deep-sea mining.
Existing sector-specific tools (e.g., RFMO closures, CCAMLR protection zones) offer useful precedents but are fragmented and lack cross-sectoral integration.
Seven recommendations are offered: (1) establish a strong foundation of principles and obligations; (2) build on consensus and fill gaps in existing efforts; (3) empower the Conference of Parties; (4) learn by doing and avoid "paper parks"; (5) develop a strong scientific infrastructure; (6) do not delay monitoring, control, surveillance, and compliance mechanisms; (7) cooperate both within and outside the BBNJ agreement.
The report situates its work within SDG 14 (10\% ocean protection by 2020) and the potential 30x30 target under the post-2020 CBD framework.


Limitations

The report explicitly frames its contributions as "initial indications," acknowledging a preliminary and non-definitive character.
It is a policy brief rather than a peer-reviewed empirical study; no original quantitative data or field observations are presented.
The analysis is based on the November 2019 BBNJ draft text; the authors note that a revised draft was expected after the report's publication, meaning the legal landscape may have shifted.
The document text provided is truncated (sections 2–4 are only partially included), so the full detail of the case-study analyses and the seven recommendations is not fully available for assessment.
No dedicated "limitations" section appears in the available text; the constraints above are inferred from the report's self-description and scope.

