@article{Wright2015c,
	title = {Advancing marine biodiversity protection through regional fisheries management: {A} review of bottom fisheries closures in areas beyond national jurisdiction},
	volume = {61},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	issn = {0308-597X},
	url = {http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.marpol.2015.06.030},
	doi = {10.1016/j.marpol.2015.06.030},
	abstract = {Fishing is a significant threat to marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). Bottom fishing in particular can impact deep-sea ecosystems, and the UN General Assembly has called on regional fisheries management organisations and arrangements (RFMO/As) to take actions to regulate bottom fisheries, including to close areas to bottom fishing activities where there is likely to be significant adverse impacts to vulnerable marine ecosystems (VMEs). This paper provides an update on the current status of closures, suggesting that RFMO/A biodiversity conservation efforts continue to advance slowly. RFMO/As have been slow to implement additional closures and to act in a precautionary manner based on available scientific evidence. Existing powers are not being fully utilised and best practice is not always followed. Closures have often been temporary or representative, or have not in fact restricted ongoing fishing activity. Some positive outcomes provide examples of good practice, though RFMO/As will need to fully utilise their powers and follow best practice before authorising bottom fishing to proceed in ABNJ.},
	language = {en},
	journal = {Marine Policy},
	publisher = {Elsevier BV},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Ardron, Jeff and Gjerde, Kristina and Currie, Duncan and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2015},
	keywords = {governance, fisheries, BBNJ, VME, RFMO, not undermine, cited-by:glen-strong-high-seas, IFB, OA:unknown, source:glen-7pubs-refs, author:Glen Wright, EIA / SEA, bottom-closures-update-2026, theme:VME, baseline paper, HSBFC, theme:baseline paper, theme:HSBFC, theme:RFMO, mesopelagic, baseline-cites, from-iddri-2020},
	pages = {134--148},
	annote = {Downloaded from Sci-Hub.},
	annote = {Objective
This review paper aims to assess the efforts made by regional fisheries management organisations and arrangements (RFMO/As) to implement high seas bottom fisheries closures in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), and to suggest options for improving the protection of vulnerable marine ecosystems (VMEs) within that framework. The authors also intend to provide a basis for future research on how fisheries governance in ABNJ might be addressed through a new international agreement on high seas biodiversity.

Methods
The paper is a qualitative review and assessment. The authors:

Outline the global context of bottom fishing impacts on deep-sea ecosystems and the relevant legal and policy framework (UNGA resolutions 61/105 and 64/72, the UN Fish Stocks Agreement, FAO 2008 International Guidelines).
Detail the context and process for fisheries closures under RFMO/A mandates.
Assess RFMO/A performance across the Atlantic, Pacific, Southern, and Indian Oceans, examining specific bodies including CCAMLR, NEAFC, NAFO, SPRFMO, SIODFA, and others.
Identify positive examples of good practice and gaps in implementation.
Draw on information provided by RFMO/A staff, government departments, and published assessments (e.g., DSCC 2011, Rogers \& Gianni 2010, UNGA 2011 workshop outcomes).


Key findings

RFMO/A biodiversity conservation efforts in ABNJ continue to advance slowly; existing powers are not being fully utilised and best practice is not consistently followed.
Closures have often been temporary, "representative" (limited to fished areas), or have not in fact restricted ongoing fishing activity.
RFMO/As have been slow to act in a precautionary manner based on available scientific evidence and to follow the advice of their scientific committees in a timely way.
Bottom fishing, particularly trawling, causes long-lasting or irreversible damage to deep-sea ecosystems; recovery may take decades to centuries given the slow growth rates of deep-sea species.
Positive examples exist: CCAMLR is cited as reacting quickly to VME encounters; the NEAFC–OSPAR Memorandum of Understanding and the Madeira Process illustrate potential cooperation between Regional Seas programmes and RFMO/As; SIODFA highlights a potential (though limited) role for industry-led voluntary closures.
The authors recommend that temporary closures be made permanent where VMEs exist, that move-on rules be made more precautionary, that closures be established wherever VMEs occur rather than only in fished areas, and that RFMO/As collaborate with Regional Seas programmes and other regional bodies.
RFMO/A measures, including closures, can only be one element of a broader framework for integrated conservation of ABNJ biodiversity.


Limitations
The paper does not include a dedicated limitations section. However, several constraints are implied by the text:

The review is a point-in-time snapshot (published 2015) and does not capture subsequent developments.
The authors note that RFMO/As have been slow to make their activities publicly available, which likely constrained the completeness of the information available for review.
Challenges identified in the broader literature and UNGA workshop include lack of scientific information and data, high costs of research, and ambiguity in definitions and terminology (e.g., of VMEs), all of which affect the quality of the underlying evidence base.
As a review rather than an empirical study, the paper relies on secondary sources and self-reported RFMO/A data, and does not independently verify closure effectiveness on the ground.

