@techreport{cremersOptionsStrengtheningMonitoring2020,
	title = {Options for {Strengthening} {Monitoring}, {Control} and {Surveillance} of {Human} {Activities} in the {Southeast} {Pacific} {Region}},
	url = {https://www.iddri.org/en/publications-and-events/report/regional-ocean-governance-areas-beyond-national-jurisdiction-lessons},
	abstract = {This report provides an overview of a range of ongoing initiatives to improve governance of ABNJ at the regional level, including: novel modalities, such as the “Collective Arrange- ment for the North-East Atlantic”, whereby the OSPAR Commission and the North East Atlantic Fisheries Commission (NEAFC) seek to strengthen cooperation between organisations with a management mandate in the region; coalition-based initiatives, such as the Sargasso Sea Commission and the Pelagos Sanctuary for Mediterranean Marine Mam- mals; and the international legal framework of the Antarctic Treaty System (ATS), under which parties have agreed to develop a representative system of MPAs and have designated the world’s largest MPA in the Ross Sea. These experiences provide a number of useful lessons learnt that can facilitate further devel- opment of regional initiatives and inform the construction of an efficient and effective new international treaty. In particular, this report highlights the need for: effective mechanisms for cooperation and coordination between organisations with a management mandate in ABNJ; the role played by champions and leaders with the political will to drive the process and garner support for improved management; and the importance of developing a dynamic science-policy interface that can provide policy-relevant scientific information to decision makers and stakeholders.},
	institution = {STRONG High Seas},
	author = {Cremers, Klaudija and Wright, Glen and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2020},
	keywords = {fisheries, BBNJ, RFMO, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright, EIA / SEA},
	pages = {46},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This report, produced under the STRONG High Seas project, aims to provide concrete recommendations to the Member States of the Permanent Commission for the South Pacific (CPPS) on strengthening monitoring, control and surveillance (MCS) of human activities in the Southeast Pacific region. It situates these recommendations within the broader context of ongoing negotiations for an international legally binding instrument on marine biological diversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ), seeking to inform both regional governance improvements and the construction of a future global treaty.

Methods
The report draws on multiple sources of evidence and stakeholder engagement:

An expert workshop on "Strengthening MCS in the Southeast Pacific" held in Guayaquil, Ecuador (14–15 November 2019), organized by IDDRI and the CPPS Secretariat, where participants identified legal, institutional, and technological challenges and shared success stories.
A STRONG High Seas Dialogue Workshop in Lima, Peru (26–27 February 2020) for further discussion with regional stakeholders.
A country-by-country review of MCS frameworks and enforcement efforts in Chile, Colombia, Ecuador, and Peru, including case studies of Panama and Costa Rica.
Analysis of best practices among regional fisheries management organisations (RFMOs) operating in the Southeast Pacific.
Comparative review of governance models from other regions, including the OSPAR/NEAFC Collective Arrangement, the Sargasso Sea Commission, the Pelagos Sanctuary, and the Antarctic Treaty System.


Key findings
The report distils its analysis into three concrete proposals for strengthening MCS in the Southeast Pacific:

Improve communication, cooperation, and coordination on MCS within and between CPPS States and between regional and sectoral bodies (e.g., RFMOs, CPPS, national authorities).
Establish an effective joint data-based MCS strategy, including a regional information exchange platform and capacity-building workshops for decision-makers and compliance officers.
Ensure an appropriate penalty system, encompassing effective sanctions, prompt intervention against suspected illegal activities, and a requirement to develop an MCS strategy when designating any area-based management tool, including marine protected areas.

Additional findings include: the importance of political champions to drive the process; the value of a dynamic science-policy interface; the utility of market-based measures such as the EU yellow-card system and catch documentation schemes to deter IUU fishing; and the need for stronger legal frameworks, as illustrated by Ecuador's adoption of a new fisheries law with higher fines and vessel-detention powers. The report also highlights that control authorities can only act effectively when vessels are conducting suspicious activities or are already blacklisted, exposing a reactive rather than proactive enforcement gap.

Limitations

The provided document text is partial; full sections on national MCS reviews (Chile, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru) and the detailed options analysis (Section 4) are not fully available, limiting the depth of this summary.
The report is a policy brief rather than a peer-reviewed empirical study; it relies on stakeholder workshops and desk review rather than original quantitative data collection.
The geographic scope is limited to the Southeast Pacific (CPPS region), so findings may not generalise to other ABNJ regions without adaptation.
The analysis reflects the institutional and political context of 2019–2020; subsequent developments in BBNJ treaty negotiations and national MCS legislation are not captured.
The report is funded by the German Federal Ministry for the Environment (IKI) and explicitly notes it does not necessarily reflect the views of funding agencies, but no independent peer review is indicated.

