	doi = {10.1038/d41586-018-01594-1},
	abstract = {At the close of 2017, 14 million UK viewers tuned into the acclaimed second series of David Attenborough’s Blue Planet, making it the year’s most-watched television show. It brought the wonders of the ocean into people’s living rooms and captured the public imagination as never before. Now is the time to capitalize on this enthusiasm, and to advocate for strong, legally binding protections for the high seas — the almost two-thirds of our planet’s ocean that are beyond the control of any one State.},
	number = {7691},
	urldate = {2018-03-13},
	journal = {Nature},
	publisher = {Nature Publishing Group},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Rochette, Julien and Gjerde, Kristina M. and Levin, Lisa A.},
	month = feb,
	year = {2018},
	note = {tex.mendeley-tags: ABNJ,BBNJ,MPAs},
	keywords = {ABNJ, BBNJ, MPAs},
	annote = {Same paper as Wright 2018, which already has the PDF.},
	annote = {Same paper as Wright 2018, which already has the PDF.},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2018 Nature comment by Wright, Rochette, Gjerde, and Levin advocates for strong, legally binding international protections for the high seas — the roughly 45\% of Earth's surface that lies beyond any single state's jurisdiction. The authors argue that the public enthusiasm generated by the BBC's Blue Planet II (14 million UK viewers) should be channeled into supporting the new UN treaty negotiations launched by Resolution 72/249 (December 2017), and that the resulting agreement must include a robust system of global marine protected areas (MPAs) with firm international oversight.

Methods
This is a policy comment and advocacy piece, not an empirical study. The authors synthesize existing scientific literature, review the current patchwork of high-seas governance institutions (ISA, regional fisheries bodies, IMO, OSPAR, Sargasso Sea Commission, Antarctic Treaty System), and draw on specific case examples (e.g., the Lost City hydrothermal field mining contract, the Sargasso Sea EBSA designation, the Ross Sea MPA) to illustrate governance gaps. They cite prior research on MPA effectiveness, species ecology, and the feasibility of high-seas conservation to build their policy argument.

Key findings

Only about 4\% of the global ocean is currently protected, and hardly any MPAs cover the high seas, falling far short of the 10\% Aichi target (and the 30\% some scientists argue is necessary).
Existing high-seas governance is fragmented across multiple agencies with little communication, coherence, or scientific input; decisions are often politicized and consensus-driven.
Well-designed, large, long-term "no-take" MPAs backed by enforcement show five times more large-fish biomass than unprotected areas (Edgar et al. 2014).
New evidence dispels the assumption that wide-ranging species cannot be meaningfully protected; even deep-water species aggregate at specific feeding, spawning, and nursery sites.
Multiple scientifically grounded candidate sites already exist for initial MPAs: five proposed UNESCO World Heritage Sites, nearly 50 EBSAs, vulnerable marine ecosystems, and ISA areas of particular environmental interest.
A single set of MPAs is insufficient; a wider, representative, and connected network is needed for climate resilience and biodiversity maintenance.
Regulation of marine genetic resources (bioprospecting) remains unresolved, with no current obligation to share profits from high-seas discoveries.
The strongest legal outcome would be a new UN body with binding, top-down authority, balanced against the need for broad state participation.


Limitations

The piece is an advocacy comment, not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it presents a one-sided policy position rather than a balanced analysis of trade-offs.
The treaty it discusses was still in negotiation (expected after 2020), so its provisions, scope, and enforceability remained uncertain at the time of writing.
The authors acknowledge that no one has yet determined where, how large, or how deep high-seas MPAs should be, and that baseline data remain incomplete.
Key political uncertainties are noted but unresolved: the United States has not ratified UNCLOS; developing nations require substantial financial and technical support; and "flags of convenience" are explicitly outside the treaty's scope.
The authors concede that negotiations risk producing a weak, non-binding outcome, and that the balance between strong international oversight and respect for existing institutional mandates is unresolved.

