	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2015 policy report by Rochette and Wright (IDDRI), produced within an IUCN-led project funded by the French Global Environment Facility, aims to stimulate discussion among Western Indian Ocean (WIO) stakeholders on possible ways to engage in the governance of areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), particularly through area-based management tools. It identifies approaches developed in other world regions and highlights key issues for the WIO to consider when developing actions in ABNJ.

Methods
The report is a desk-based policy and legal review rather than an empirical study. It proceeds in two main parts:

Section 2 reviews three categories of existing area-based management approaches in ABNJ: (1) measures adopted by regional organisations (Marine Protected Areas within UNEP Regional Seas programmes and high-seas bottom fisheries closures by RFMOs); (2) coalition-based initiatives (the Pelagos Sanctuary and the Sargasso Sea Alliance); and (3) sectoral measures adopted by international organisations (IMO Particularly Sensitive Sea Areas and ISA Areas of Particular Environmental Interest).
Section 3 maps these approaches onto the WIO context, assessing the current status of relevant instruments (Nairobi Convention, SIOFA, IOTC, WIOMSA, WIO-C, etc.) and identifying specific key issues for regional discussion.

The analysis draws on legal texts (UNCLOS, UNGA resolutions, regional conventions), scientific literature on deep-sea impacts, and grey literature on governance processes.

Key findings

ABNJ covers roughly half the planet's surface and faces mounting pressures from bottom fisheries, habitat destruction, climate change, pollution, deep-sea mining, and genetic-resource exploitation.
In January 2015, the BBNJ Working Group recommended that the UNGA open negotiations in 2016 for a legally binding instrument under UNCLOS, a historic step toward a new global agreement.
Three Regional Seas (Mediterranean, Southern Ocean, North-East Atlantic) have already established MPAs in ABNJ, demonstrating that regional action is feasible while the global process is ongoing.
In the WIO, very few existing organisations or projects address ABNJ conservation. The Nairobi Convention is currently limited to national jurisdictions; no high-seas bottom fisheries closures have been established; SIOFA is in early stages; and no PSSA or APEI designations exist in the region.
Thirteen Benthic Protected Areas have been established by the Southern Indian Ocean Deep Sea Fishers Association (SIODFA), a private-sector body, rather than through a formal RFMO process.
Cross-cutting requirements for WIO action include: building the scientific case using EBSA data and threat assessments; selecting the most appropriate legal/policy pathway; championing the process through coalitions; anticipating coordination challenges among competent authorities; and securing funding.


Limitations

The report is a policy discussion document, not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it does not present original field data or quantitative analysis.
Regional initiatives reviewed are binding only on their contracting parties, leaving non-parties unregulated; the report acknowledges this gap but does not propose a binding enforcement mechanism.
The provided document text is incomplete (sections are truncated with ellipses), so the full discussion of sectoral measures (PSSA, APEI) and the complete set of WIO key issues may not be fully captured here.
As a 2015 publication, it predates the adoption of the BBNJ Agreement (2023) and subsequent developments in WIO governance, limiting its currency.
The report does not include stakeholder consultations or on-the-ground assessments of WIO fishing practices, seabed-mining exploration status, or shipping traffic patterns in ABNJ.

