@article{Gjerde2016,
	title = {Protecting {Earth}'s last conservation frontier: scientific, management and legal priorities for {MPAs} beyond national boundaries},
	volume = {26},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	issn = {10527613},
	url = {http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/aqc.2646},
	doi = {10.1002/aqc.2646},
	abstract = {1. Marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ) comprise most of Earth's interconnected ocean, hosting complex ecosystems that play key roles in sustaining life and providing important goods and services. 2. Although ABNJ encompass nearly half the planet's surface, biological diversity found in these areas remains largely unprotected. Mounting pressures generated by the escalation of human activities in ABNJ threaten vital ecosystem services and the fragile web of life that supports them. 3. Marine protected areas (MPAs) are widely acknowledged as an important tool for the conservation of biological diversity. Currently less than 1\{\%\} of ABNJ are protected, with the vast majority of MPAs located in waters within national jurisdiction. 4. The existing legal framework for protection and sustainable use of ABNJ lacks common goals, principles or standards, multi-sectoral coordination and comprehensive geographic coverage to ensure conservation or good governance grounded in science-based decision-making, transparency, accountability and effective enforcement.5. This paper highlights the urgency and importance of protecting the last conservation frontier on Earth. Key lessons for conservation inABNJ can be learned fromregional, cross-boundary and national experiences shared during the high seas governance workshop at the IUCNWorld Parks Congress in Sydney, Australia in November 2014. 6. The intent of this paper is to inform the deliberations now underway in the United Nations General Assembly to develop a new legally binding international instrument for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity in ABNJ. It also aims to encourage further initiatives to protect and preserve our last conservation frontier using currently available mechanisms and powers consistent with international law.},
	journal = {Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems},
	author = {Gjerde, Kristina M. and Reeve, Lora L. Nordtvedt and Harden-Davies, Harriet and Ardron, Jeff and Dolan, Ryan and Durussel, Carole and Earle, Sylvia and Jimenez, Jorge A. and Kalas, Peggy and Laffoley, Dan and Oral, Nilufer and Page, Richard and Ribeiro, Marta Chantal and Rochette, Julien and Spadone, Aurelie and Thiele, Torsten and Thomas, Hannah L. and Wagner, Daniel and Warner, Robin and Wilhelm, Aulani and Wright, Glen and Nordtvedt, Lora L and Harden-Davies, Harriet and Ardron, Jeff and José, San and Rica, Costa},
	year = {2016},
	note = {Merged duplicates into this item; trashed: JR7UIT3Q},
	keywords = {BBNJ, bbnj-architecture-corpus, ABMT / MPAs, architecture, not undermine, cited-by:glen-strong-high-seas, IFB, source:glen-7pubs-refs, author:Glen Wright, MGR / ABS, OA:open, bbnj-dedupe-2026-09-08},
	pages = {45--60},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This paper by Gjerde et al. (2016) addresses the urgent need to protect marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), described as Earth's last conservation frontier. The authors aim to (1) highlight the scientific, management, and legal priorities for establishing marine protected areas (MPAs) in the high seas and international seabed Area, (2) inform the United Nations General Assembly deliberations (initiated in June 2015) toward a new legally binding international instrument under UNCLOS for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity in ABNJ, and (3) encourage further protection initiatives using currently available mechanisms consistent with international law.

Methods
The paper is a policy and scientific synthesis rather than an empirical study. Its analytical basis includes:

Review of the existing legal and governance framework for ABNJ, including UNCLOS (1982), sectoral management organizations (RFMOs, IWC, IMO, ISA), and regional agreements (CCAMLR, OSPAR, Noumea Convention, Mediterranean Protocol).
Lessons drawn from a high seas governance workshop held at the IUCN World Parks Congress in Sydney, Australia (November 2014), where regional, cross-boundary, and national conservation experiences were shared.
Assessment of documented threats to ABNJ ecosystems (overfishing, pollution, seabed mining, underwater noise, ocean warming, deoxygenation, acidification) and the gaps in current governance.
Analysis of international conservation targets, including CBD Aichi Target 11 (10\% by 2020) and the IUCN Promise of Sydney (30\% MPA coverage).

Note: The full text of the sections on "National Conservation Experiences Relevant to ABNJ," "Key Elements for a New Legally-Binding Instrument," and the "Conclusion" was not provided in the source material, so those portions of the methodology and argumentation could not be summarized.

Key findings

ABNJ (high seas water column and international seabed Area) comprise over 60\% of the global ocean, yet less than 1\% is currently under protection, with the vast majority of existing MPAs located within national jurisdictions.
The existing governance regime is fragmented across sector-specific organizations that manage activities in isolation, lacking common goals, multi-sectoral coordination, comprehensive geographic coverage, and accountability frameworks for science-based decision-making, transparency, and effective enforcement.
Regional MPA networks exist in the Southern Ocean, North-East Atlantic, Pacific, and Mediterranean, but no single global instrument enables coordinated MPA implementation across ABNJ, leaving significant gaps relative to the 30\% conservation target.
UNCLOS, while comprehensive at its 1982 adoption, predates modern conservation concepts (biodiversity, precaution, ecosystem-based management, MPAs) and the scale of anthropogenic threats now affecting ABNJ, creating governance gaps that have become apparent over time.
In June 2015, the UNGA formally agreed to develop a legally binding instrument under UNCLOS for ABNJ marine biodiversity conservation, building on more than a decade of global discussions.
Scientists argue that significantly more than 10\% of the ocean must be safeguarded to maintain healthy marine ecosystems, reinforcing the 30\% target adopted at the 2014 IUCN World Parks Congress.


Limitations

The paper is a policy review and synthesis; it does not present original empirical data or quantitative analysis of MPA effectiveness in ABNJ.
The provided document text is incomplete: the sections detailing specific national conservation experiences, the proposed key elements for the new legally binding instrument, and the authors' concluding recommendations were not available for review.
The analysis reflects the state of governance and political deliberations as of 2016; subsequent developments (e.g., the 2023 High Seas Treaty) are outside the scope of this work.
The paper is advocacy-oriented, explicitly intended to inform and encourage policy action, which may limit the balance of perspectives presented.

