@techreport{Wright2018c,
	address = {Paris},
	type = {Study},
	title = {The {Long} and {Winding} {Road}: negotiating a treaty for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	url = {https://www.iddri.org/en/publications-and-events/study/long-and-winding-road-negotiating-high-seas-treaty},
	abstract = {Marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ) cover nearly half of the Earth’s surface and host a significant portion of its biodiversity. The international community, increasingly aware of the growing threats to ecosystems in ABNJ, has been informally discussing options to conserve and sustainably use its biodiversity for more than a decade. On 24 December 2017, the United Nations General Assembly (UNGA) decided to convene an intergovernmental conference (IGC) to elaborate an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) for the conservation and sustainable use of the biological diversity of ABNJ. As States continue to navigate the complex issues at stake and start to negotiate the provisions of a new agreement, it is imperative that all stakeholders have a clear and comprehensive understanding of the history of the process, elements under discussion, State positions to date, and the challenges that lie ahead. The Long and Winding Road provides a guide to the discussions for both experienced participants and newcomers to the process.},
	urldate = {2026-09-29},
	institution = {IDDRI},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Rochette, Julien and Gjerde, Kristina and Seeger, Isabel},
	year = {2018},
	keywords = {governance, fisheries, BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright, grey-literature, mesopelagic, baseline-cites, from-iddri-2020},
	pages = {82},
	annote = {Objective
This 2018 IDDRI study by Wright, Rochette, Gjerde, and Seeger serves as a comprehensive guide to the international negotiations for an International Legally Binding Instrument (ILBI) on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). The authors aim to equip both experienced participants and newcomers with a clear understanding of the negotiation history, the substantive issues under discussion, State positions to date, and the challenges ahead of the Intergovernmental Conference (IGC) convened by the UN General Assembly on 24 December 2017.

Methods
The document is a policy research synthesis rather than an empirical study. Its analytical approach includes:

Review of the existing legal and institutional framework governing ABNJ, including the 1982 UN Convention on the Law of the Sea, sectoral governance regimes, and regional initiatives.
Identification and analysis of gaps in that framework (governance principles, MPA establishment, MGR status, EIA rules, capacity-building, fisheries management, flag-state responsibility).
Chronological tracing of the negotiation process from the BBNJ Ad Hoc Working Group (2006–2015) through the Preparatory Committee (2016–2017) to the IGC decision (December 2017) and the organizational meeting (April 2018).
Categorization of State positions into three groups: advocates, facilitators, and reluctant participants.
Structured analysis of the four "Package Deal" issues agreed in 2011: marine genetic resources and benefit-sharing, area-based management tools (including MPAs), environmental impact assessments, and capacity-building/technology transfer.

The study draws on a broad literature of marine policy, international law, and ocean science, as reflected in its extensive reference list.

Key findings

ABNJ covers nearly half the Earth's surface and hosts a significant share of global biodiversity, yet faces intensifying pressures from shipping, fishing, seabed mining, and interest in marine genetic resources.
The existing governance framework is fragmented, lacking overarching principles, a global MPA mechanism, clear rules on MGR status, and uniform EIA requirements.
The 2011 "Package Deal" remains the agreed negotiating mandate, bundling four substantive issues into a single instrument.
State positions are polarized: a group of advocates pushes for a strong new treaty; facilitators seek compromise; and a group of influential States remains reluctant, arguing existing arrangements suffice.
The IGC was set to commence in September 2018, with the organizational meeting held in April 2018 to establish procedural rules.
Regional and sectoral governance mechanisms (RFMOs, regional seas conventions, the ISA) play complementary but incomplete roles and will interact with the new instrument.


Limitations

The document is a policy guide and synthesis, not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it does not present original data collection or statistical analysis.
It is a point-in-time snapshot (August 2018) and does not reflect developments after the IGC's first session.
As an IDDRI publication, it carries the institutional perspective of a Paris-based policy institute with a stated focus on sustainable development and global governance, which may shape framing and emphasis.
The forewords by Tubiana and Charles are opinion pieces and do not constitute analytical findings.
The document does not include a formal limitations section, methodological transparency statement, or conflict-of-interest disclosure beyond a general copyright and funding note (French government support via ANR-10-LABX-01).

