@article{Crespo2019,
	title = {High-seas fish biodiversity is slipping through the governance net},
	volume = {3},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	issn = {2397334X},
	url = {https://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-019-0981-4},
	doi = {10.1038/s41559-019-0981-4},
	abstract = {States at the United Nations have begun negotiating a new treaty to strengthen the legal regime for marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction. Failure to ensure the full scope of fish biodiversity is covered could result in thousands of species continuing to slip through the cracks of a fragmented global ocean governance framework.},
	language = {en},
	number = {9},
	urldate = {2020-06-18},
	journal = {Nature Ecology \& Evolution},
	publisher = {Nature Publishing Group},
	author = {Crespo, Guillermo Ortuño and Dunn, Daniel C. and Gianni, Matthew and Gjerde, Kristina and Wright, Glen and Halpin, Patrick N.},
	month = sep,
	year = {2019},
	keywords = {governance, fisheries, BBNJ, RFMO, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright, mesopelagic, baseline-cites, from-iddri-2020, from-iass-2021},
	pages = {1273--1276},
	annote = {Downloaded from Sci-Hub.},
	annote = {Objective
This 2019 comment in Nature Ecology \& Evolution (Crespo et al., DOI: 10.1038/s41559-019-0981-4) examines the gap between documented fish biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ) and the fraction of those species actually subject to population monitoring. The authors frame this gap within the context of ongoing United Nations negotiations for a new treaty to strengthen the legal regime for marine biodiversity on the high seas, warning that failure to cover the full scope of fish biodiversity could leave thousands of species unaddressed by a fragmented global governance framework.

Methods

The authors used the Ocean Biogeographic Information System (OBIS), described as the most comprehensive open-access clearinghouse for spatially referenced marine biodiversity observations, to estimate fish biodiversity in the high seas. Records were parsed spatially and taxonomically to include only fish (Superclass: Pisces) from ABNJ, drawing on 162 separate databases.
The RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database, compiled from 21 national and international management bodies, was cross-referenced with the OBIS dataset to approximate how many fish species have undergone some form of abundance assessment.
Stock assessment methods were catalogued across 51 method types in 8 categories (e.g., biomass dynamics models, statistical catch-at-age models, survey indices, VPA, yield-per-recruit models).
Complementary approaches were considered, including Ecological Risk Assessments (productivity and susceptibility analysis) for migratory elasmobranchs, and data-poor stock assessments or catch-based models that use catch trends and life-history traits.


Key findings

OBIS documents 4,018 fish species in the high seas, spanning three taxonomic classes (Agnatha, Chondrichthyes, Osteichthyes), 382 families, and 1,630 genera.
Only 331 stocks worldwide have biomass, recruitment, or landing estimates in the RAM database; of these, 295 are fish species from just 46 families, meaning the vast majority of documented high-seas fish species lack any abundance assessment.
For migratory sharks and rays, only 16.3\% have a stock assessment or ecological risk assessment, even when ERAs are included (16 species assessed, 14 without a formal stock assessment).
In the South Pacific high seas bottom fisheries, 115 of 137 species caught are bycatch, with no catch limits and unknown abundance status for those non-target species.
OBIS records are heavily spatially biased toward the Central and North Atlantic, and taxonomic completeness indicators show low coverage across large portions of ABNJ, meaning the true biodiversity is substantially underestimated.


Limitations

The document is supplementary information accompanying a short comment, not a full research article; it provides context and data summaries rather than original analytical results.
OBIS data carry clear geographic biases, with most records concentrated in the North Atlantic, so the 4,018-species figure is acknowledged as an underestimate.
Stock assessments, while the most robust tool, are not the only method for evaluating abundance; the authors note that data-poor assessments and catch-based models still require information that existing fisheries management institutions do not capture for most ABNJ species.
The cross-reference between OBIS and the RAM database yields only an "initial approximation" of monitored species, as the two datasets differ in scope, taxonomic resolution, and geographic coverage.
No specific quantitative estimate of the total number of unmonitored high-seas fish species is provided; the paper highlights the gap qualitatively rather than producing a single definitive figure.

