@techreport{GjerdeK.BotelerB.DurusselC.RochetteJ.UngerS.2018a,
	title = {Conservation and {Sustainable} {Use} of {Marine} {Biodiversity} in {Areas} {Beyond} {National} {Jurisdiction}: {Options} for {Underpinning} a {Strong} {Global} {BBNJ} {Agreement} through {Regional} and {Sectoral} {Governance}},
	url = {www.prog-ocean.org/our-work/strong-high-seas/},
	doi = {10.2312/iass.2018.015},
	abstract = {Gjerde, K., Boteler, B., Durussel, C., Rochette, J., Unger, S., Wright‚ G., ‘Conservation and Sustainable Use of Marine Biodiversity in Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction: Options for Underpinning a Strong Global BBNJ Agreement through Regional and Sectoral Governance’, STRONG High Seas Project, 2018.},
	institution = {STRONG High Seas},
	author = {Gjerde, Kristina and Boteler, Ben and Durussel, Carole and Rochette, Julien and Unger, Sebastian and Wright, Glen},
	year = {2018},
	keywords = {fisheries, BBNJ, RFMO, not undermine, IFB, type:grey, author:Glen Wright, OA:open, regional governance},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2018 policy brief from the STRONG High Seas project examines how regional ocean governance in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ) can underpin a strong global BBNJ (Biodiversity Beyond National Jurisdiction) agreement. It reviews experiences from existing regional and sectoral initiatives and identifies pathways for future action to strengthen the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in the high seas and the Area.

Methods
The document is a decision-maker-oriented policy brief produced within the five-year STRONG High Seas project (2017–2022), coordinated by the Institute for Advanced Sustainability Studies (IASS) in partnership with IDDRI, BirdLife International, the Ocean Institute, Universidad Católica del Norte, and WWF. The approach includes:

Review and comparative analysis of nine existing regional and sectoral ABNJ governance initiatives (e.g., the NE Atlantic Arrangement, Sargasso Sea Commission, Pelagos Sanctuary, Antarctic Treaty System, RFMOs, EBSAs under the CBD, and emerging efforts in the Western Indian Ocean, SE Atlantic, and South Pacific).
Identification of enabling conditions and difficulties for each initiative in a structured comparative table.
Extraction of lessons learned to inform the ongoing UN negotiations for a new legally-binding BBNJ instrument.
Transdisciplinary scientific assessments and stakeholder engagement (governments, private sector, scientists, civil society) in target regions (Southeast Atlantic and Southeast Pacific).


Key findings

Coordination is critical: Effective mechanisms (formal protocols, new institutional structures, regional stakeholder platforms) are needed to harmonize the work of multiple organizations with overlapping mandates in ABNJ.
Political champions matter: Initial steps toward regional ABNJ governance are typically driven by at least one committed state, organization, or stakeholder group willing to lead and build support.
Science–policy interface: A dynamic interface between marine science and policy is essential; knowledge gaps and uncertainty slow decision-making, while a supportive scientific context increases willingness to address ABNJ issues.
Legal framework gap: A new legally-binding international instrument is needed to bring coherence to a fragmented and inefficient governance system, support inter-sectoral cooperation, and provide momentum for regional integration mechanisms.
Initiative-specific challenges: The NE Atlantic Arrangement proved time-consuming and staff-intensive; the Sargasso Sea Commission has made limited concrete management progress; the Antarctic Treaty System faces increasingly tense negotiations over additional MPAs; the Pelagos Sanctuary's long-standing IUCN designation remains unrealized; RFMOs frequently act against their own scientific advice; and EBSAs have not yet triggered management action in sectoral bodies.
Regional momentum is growing: The Western Indian Ocean (Nairobi Convention), SE Atlantic (Abidjan Convention), and South Pacific (CPPS) are progressively integrating ABNJ into their work programmes, indicating expanding regional engagement.


Limitations

The document is a policy brief rather than a peer-reviewed empirical study; it synthesizes and interprets existing initiatives rather than presenting original quantitative data.
Several reviewed initiatives show limited or stalled progress (e.g., Sargasso Sea Commission, Pelagos Sanctuary), and the brief does not provide a detailed causal analysis of why these efforts have not yet yielded concrete management outcomes.
The analysis is necessarily selective, focusing on nine named initiatives; other regional or sectoral efforts may exist but are not covered.
The brief was produced in the context of ongoing BBNJ negotiations, so its recommendations are forward-looking and contingent on political outcomes that had not yet materialized at the time of writing.
The document does not include a formal limitations section, methodological transparency statement, or systematic literature review; the scope and selection criteria for the reviewed initiatives are not explicitly justified.

