@article{harden-daviesRightsNaturePerspectives2020,
	title = {Rights of {Nature}: {Perspectives} for {Global} {Ocean} {Stewardship}},
	volume = {122},
	copyright = {All rights reserved},
	issn = {0308-597X},
	shorttitle = {Rights of {Nature}},
	url = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.marpol.2020.104059},
	doi = {10.1016/j.marpol.2020.104059},
	abstract = {The development of a new international legally binding instrument for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ agreement) is in the final negotiation phase. Legal recognition of rights of nature is emerging worldwide as a fresh imperative to preserve ecological integrity, safeguard human wellbeing, broaden participation in decision-making, and give a voice to nature – but so far exclusively within national jurisdiction. In this paper, we consider how a Rights of Nature perspective might inform the BBNJ agreement. We examine Rights of Nature laws and identify four characteristics relating to: i) rights; ii) connectivity; iii) reciprocity; and iv) representation and implementation. We argue that a Rights of Nature perspective can reinforce existing ocean governance norms, inspire new measures to enhance the effectiveness and equitability of the BBNJ agreement and enable global ocean stewardship in ABNJ.},
	urldate = {2025-08-08},
	journal = {Marine Policy},
	author = {Harden-Davies, Harriet and Humphries, Fran and Maloney, Michelle and Wright, Glen and Gjerde, Kristina and Vierros, Marjo},
	month = dec,
	year = {2020},
	keywords = {BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	pages = {104059},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
The authors aim to explore how a Rights of Nature perspective might inform the ongoing negotiations for a new international legally binding instrument on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity beyond national jurisdiction (the BBNJ agreement). The paper is explicitly framed as an exploratory exercise to consider how Rights of Nature thinking could help reframe and overcome intractable challenges in BBNJ governance, rather than to produce detailed treaty proposals or a comprehensive review of Rights of Nature law.

Methods
The authors adopt a legal-analytical and comparative approach. Specifically, they:

Review existing Rights of Nature laws and court decisions across multiple jurisdictions (Ecuador, Bolivia, New Zealand, Colombia, India, Uganda, Bangladesh, and others), drawing on a compiled table of illustrative examples.
Identify and articulate four defining characteristics of Rights of Nature approaches: (i) rights, (ii) connectivity, (iii) reciprocity, and (iv) representation and implementation.
Outline key challenges in the BBNJ negotiation context, including fragmented governance, limited coordination between international and regional bodies, and the absence of global oversight for ABNJ.
Suggest four ways Rights of Nature approaches could inspire novel solutions for the BBNJ agreement, including the concept of a "Council of Ocean Custodians" to give a voice to the ocean.


Key findings

ABNJ covers nearly half the Earth's surface yet suffers from significant governance gaps: international obligations to protect marine ecosystems have not been effectively discharged, coordination is limited, and there is no global oversight mechanism.
Conventional environmental law tools (precautionary principle, environmental impact assessment, sustainable development) have proven inadequate to prevent biodiversity loss and ecosystem degradation.
The four characteristics of Rights of Nature laws—rights, connectivity, reciprocity, and representation/implementation—align with and can reinforce established ocean governance norms under UNCLOS.
A Rights of Nature perspective can inspire new measures to enhance both the effectiveness and equitability of the BBNJ agreement, moving beyond a purely anthropocentric, rights-of-states framework.
Recognising nature as not merely human property and affirming a common human responsibility to safeguard natural systems offers a reframing that could enable genuine global ocean stewardship in ABNJ.
Rights of Nature developments are gaining traction at the UN level (Harmony with Nature resolutions, IUCN decision-making principles) and are beginning to appear on the ocean governance agenda, including a feasibility study for recognising the Pacific Ocean as a legal entity.


Limitations

The authors explicitly state the paper is not intended to generate detailed proposals for BBNJ treaty provisions nor to provide a comprehensive review of Rights of Nature law.
All existing Rights of Nature laws operate exclusively within national jurisdiction; no precedent yet exists for applying such a framework at the international/ABNJ level, making the transfer speculative.
Several court decisions recognising rights of nature have been subsequently challenged or overturned (e.g., in India and the USA), raising questions about legal durability.
Commentators question the efficacy of rights-based approaches in the absence of wider societal transformations; implementation measures and institutional responsibilities remain under-defined in many jurisdictions.
The provided document text is incomplete (sections are truncated with "[…]"), so the full argumentation in Sections 3, 4, and 5 could not be assessed in detail.

