@article{cremersStrengtheningMonitoringControl2020,
	title = {Strengthening monitoring, control and surveillance of human activities in marine areas beyond national jurisdiction: {Challenges} and opportunities for an international legally binding instrument},
	volume = {122},
	issn = {0308-597X},
	shorttitle = {Strengthening monitoring, control and surveillance of human activities in marine areas beyond national jurisdiction},
	url = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.marpol.2020.103976},
	doi = {10.1016/j.marpol.2020.103976},
	abstract = {Monitoring, control and surveillance (MCS) is critical for the success of marine conservation and management. This raises specific challenges in the deep and distant waters of marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), which is characterised by a fragmented governance framework and reliance on flag States to ensure control over vessels. States at the United Nations are currently negotiating an international legally binding instrument for the conservation and sustainable use of the biological diversity of marine areas beyond national jurisdiction and there is a growing interest in how MCS tools and policies can contribute to the management of this vast global commons. The paper provides some suggested pathways for strengthening MCS in ABNJ, as well as three concrete proposals for provisions that could be included in the future international instrument.},
	language = {en},
	urldate = {2025-08-11},
	journal = {Marine Policy},
	publisher = {Elsevier BV},
	author = {Cremers, Klaudija and Wright, Glen and Rochette, Julien},
	month = dec,
	year = {2020},
	keywords = {governance, fisheries, BBNJ, RFMO, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright, mesopelagic, baseline-cites, from-iass-2021},
	pages = {103976},
	annote = {Downloaded from Sci-Hub.},
	annote = {Objective
The paper examines how a future international legally binding instrument (ILBI) for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ) can strengthen monitoring, control and surveillance (MCS) of human activities, and conversely how MCS tools and policies can support implementation of the forthcoming treaty. The authors aim to identify pathways for improving MCS in the deep and distant waters of ABNJ and to offer concrete provisions for inclusion in the BBNJ negotiations.

Methods
This is a normative policy and legal analysis rather than an empirical study. The authors:

Review existing international legal instruments relevant to MCS in ABNJ, including UNCLOS, the Convention on Biological Diversity, the UN Fish Stock Agreement, the Port State Measures Agreement, IMO pollution conventions, and International Seabed Authority regulations.
Survey the range of MCS tools and technologies, from traditional observers and logbooks to vessel tracking (AIS, VMS), electronic monitoring systems, machine learning, and drones.
Identify structural challenges in the ABNJ governance framework, particularly reliance on flag State responsibility, governance gaps, and capacity deficits.
Analyse the draft BBNJ treaty text (as of 2020) to assess where MCS provisions could be strengthened.
Formulate three concrete proposals for the future ILBI.


Key findings
The authors identify a two-way relationship between MCS and the ILBI: MCS is essential for enforcing future management measures and protected areas, while the instrument can reinforce and complement existing MCS obligations. Three concrete proposals are advanced:

Reinforce MCS obligations and principles: Explicitly include transparency in the general principles article; extend the area beyond national jurisdiction management tool (ABMT) compliance and enforcement provisions to the entire agreement; and urge flag, port, and coastal States to ensure compliance, drawing on language from the UNFSA preamble.
Develop a strong role for the clearing-house mechanism: Specify that it shall serve as a platform for sharing best MCS practices, exchanging data on MCS activities, matching capacity-building needs, and obliging flag States to report on marine genetic resources accessed from ABNJ.
Require a MCS strategy with management proposals: Mandate that States submitting proposals for area-based management tools or marine protected areas include a MCS strategy outlining available technological tools, institutional frameworks, and resources needed for effective compliance.

The paper also highlights that flags of convenience, free-rider behaviour, and the absence of genuine links between vessels and flag States significantly undermine enforcement in ABNJ. Innovative and cost-effective technologies are identified as potentially transformative for closing these gaps.

Limitations

The analysis is normative and forward-looking; it does not evaluate the empirical effectiveness of existing MCS regimes or present case-study evidence of compliance outcomes.
Many key provisions in the draft BBNJ treaty text remained in brackets at the time of writing, so the authors acknowledge uncertainty about what the final instrument will contain and how the "not undermine" principle will be interpreted in practice.
The paper focuses primarily on legal and institutional design; it does not model the economic costs of implementing the proposed MCS strategies or assess feasibility for developing States in detail.
The scope is limited to the BBNJ negotiation context; broader interactions with sectoral bodies (e.g., RFMOs, ISA) are noted but not deeply analysed.
As a 2020 publication, the paper predates the finalisation of the BBNJ Agreement and subsequent developments in ABNJ governance.

