@article{wrightHighSeasTreaty2023,
	title = {Commentary—{A} {High} {Seas} {Treaty} on the {Horizon}: {Progress} and {Prospects} for the {Intergovernmental} {Conference}},
	volume = {37},
	issn = {2211-6001},
	url = {https://brill.com/view/journals/ocyo/37/1/article-p30_3.xml},
	doi = {10.1163/22116001-03701004},
	abstract = {After more than a decade of discussions, States at the United Nations are nearing agreement on a new international legally binding instrument for the conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity in marine areas beyond national jurisdiction. The instrument would resolve longstanding questions over the use of marine genetic resources, put in place a process for the des-ignation of marine protected areas, strengthen the framework for environ-mental impact assessment, and enhance capacity building and technology transfer. 

At the fifth meeting of the Intergovernmental Conference in August 2022, delegations showed significantly more flexibility and made con-siderable progress towards consensus. Many issues nonetheless remained unresolved and the meeting ultimately could not deliver the clean text needed to negotiate the final trade-offs. This short commentary reviews the progress made, identifies key outstanding issues, and highlights the next steps in the process to finalize the high seas treaty.},
	number = {1},
	urldate = {2025-08-11},
	journal = {Ocean Yearbook},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Cremers, Klaudija and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2023},
	note = {TITLE-FINAL 2026-09-10: ocr\_crossref; was: A High Seas Treaty on the Horizon: Progress and Prospects for the Intergovernmental Conference},
	keywords = {BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	pages = {30--36},
	annote = {Objective
This commentary by Wright, Cremers, and Rochette (2023) reviews the progress made at the fifth session of the Intergovernmental Conference (IGC5, August 15–26, 2022) on a new legally binding instrument for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ). The authors aim to identify key outstanding issues among the four negotiating elements—marine genetic resources (MGRs), area-based management tools (ABMTs)/marine protected areas (MPAs), environmental impact assessments (EIAs), and capacity-building and technology transfer (CBTT)—and to highlight the next steps toward finalizing the high seas treaty.

Methods
The piece is a short analytical commentary (7 pages) published in the Ocean Yearbook (Vol. 37). It does not employ empirical or experimental methods. Instead, the authors draw on:

Review of the two draft treaty texts distributed during IGC5 (the "refreshed text" of August 21 and the "further refreshed draft text" of August 26, 2022).
Analysis of informal proposals, small-group negotiations, and plenary statements reported during the session.
Engagement with the positions of developed States, developing States, the Core Latin American Group (CLAM), Pacific Small Island Developing States (PSIDS), and other delegations.
Reference to prior scholarship on BBNJ governance, the "not undermining" requirement, and implementation mechanisms.


Key findings

Overall progress: Delegations showed significantly more flexibility than in prior sessions, heeding calls from the 2022 UN Oceans Conference. However, the meeting could not deliver the clean text needed for final trade-offs, and no authoritative record of the current state of play exists.
MGRs: Several key developed States expressed willingness to accept some form of monetary benefit-sharing (flat-rate access fee or royalty-like payments), a notable shift from the prior "freedom of the high seas" position. The common heritage of mankind (CHM) principle remains in brackets in the draft.
ABMTs/MPAs: Negotiators approached consensus on preparation, consultation, and decision-making provisions. The draft envisions a "comprehensive system" of ABMTs and an "ecologically representative and well-connected network" of MPAs. Support emerged for a majority-voting mechanism where consensus fails.
EIAs: Delegations remain sharply divided. Developed States favor light-touch, limited international oversight; developing States push for an internationalized process. Disagreement persists on definitions, thresholds, the role of a future Scientific and Technical Body, and whether global minimum standards or non-binding guidelines should apply.
CBTT: Discussions were less polarizing. Delegates approached consensus on a dedicated CBTT committee and monitoring provisions, but vacillated on whether to include an indicative list of activities in the treaty text.
Institutional arrangements: No agreement was reached on a secretariat (dedicated vs. housed under DOALOS). The relationship to existing bodies (RFMOs, Regional Seas Programmes) remains highly controversial. Two options for implementation and compliance are on the table.
Next steps: The Conference President will request the UN General Assembly to schedule a resumed IGC5. Preparatory work should begin in three priority areas: securing ratifications, establishing core institutions and financial mechanisms, and developing capacity, science, and technology.


Limitations

The work is a brief commentary rather than a full empirical or legal study; it provides a snapshot of negotiations at a single point in time (August 2022) and does not track subsequent developments.
The authors note the absence of an authoritative record of the state of play after IGC5, meaning their analysis of "progress" relies on informal reporting and the two draft texts, which may not fully capture all compromises.
The highly tentative compromises reached in-person may not survive the months-long hiatus before the resumed session, limiting the durability of the findings.
Procedural concerns raised by small delegations (lack of interpretation, inability to follow parallel groups) suggest that the negotiating record itself is incomplete, which constrains any external analysis.
The piece does not include a formal limitations section; the above are inferred from the text's own caveats and the nature of the genre.

