@techreport{wrightFishingTwilightZone2020,
	title = {Fishing in the {Twilight} {Zone}: {Illuminating} governance challenges at the next fisheries frontier},
	shorttitle = {Replay},
	url = {https://www.iddri.org/sites/default/files/PDF/Publications/Catalogue%20Iddri/Etude/202011-ST0620EN-mesopelagic_0.pdf},
	abstract = {The mesopelagic, or "twilight zone" - the waters of the open ocean at a depth of approximately 150-1,000 metres - hosts significant fish stocks. These fish are unpalatable but proposals are emerging to process them into fishmeal and nutritional supplements. As this vast midwater realm plays a role in the carbon cycle and food webs, the development of new fisheries could have global ramifications. Yet our scientific understanding is limited and the existing governance framework may not be ready to effectively manage mesopelagic fisheries.

This webinar introduces the science and ecology of the mesopelagic zone and explores the potential governance challenges for the sustainable development of new fisheries.},
	language = {en},
	urldate = {2025-08-11},
	institution = {IDDRI},
	author = {Wright, Glen and {Gjerde, Kristina} and {Finkelstein, Aria} and {Currie, Duncan}},
	year = {2020},
	note = {Publication Title: IDDRI Study
Issue: 6},
	keywords = {governance, fisheries, featured, grey-literature, 2020-2026, mesopelagic},
	annote = {Objective
This 2020 IDDRI study by Wright, Gjerde, Finkelstein, and Currie aims to illuminate the governance challenges posed by emerging commercial interest in the mesopelagic ("twilight") zone (approximately 150–1,000 m depth) in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). Specifically, the authors seek to (1) introduce the science of mesopelagic ecosystems and the trajectory of fishery development proposals, (2) assess the adequacy of the existing ABNJ regulatory framework, and (3) evaluate governance options, including regulation through regional fisheries management organisations (RFMOs), environmental impact assessment (EIA) under a future BBNJ treaty, and a near-term moratorium on mesopelagic fishing in international waters.

Methods
The study is a desk-based policy and legal analysis rather than an empirical research project. The authors:

Review the current state of scientific knowledge on mesopelagic ecosystems, including diel vertical migration, the biological carbon pump, and biomass estimates.
Survey the existing international legal framework, including UNCLOS (1982), the CBD (1992), the UN Fish Stocks Agreement (1995), UNGA resolution 61/105 (2006), and non-binding guidance instruments.
Identify specific governance gaps (limited science, weak EIA obligations, inadequate institutional architecture).
Compare and assess five options for strengthening governance: proactive RFMO engagement, international guidelines, a UNGA resolution, a high-seas BBNJ treaty, and a moratorium on mesopelagic fishing.


Key findings

The mesopelagic zone spans roughly 60\% of the planet's surface and about 20\% of the ocean's volume; the bulk of the world's fish live there by number and biomass.
Through diel vertical migration and the biological pump, mesopelagic species play a critical role in the global carbon cycle and marine food webs; disruption could have profound global ramifications.
Scientific understanding of mesopelagic ecosystems remains extremely limited, yet commercial proposals to harvest these stocks for fishmeal and nutritional supplements are emerging.
The existing governance framework (UNCLOS, UNFSA, RFMOs) is inadequate: RFMOs have been slow to integrate biodiversity into management, obligations on EIA for exploratory fisheries are weak, and the institutional architecture does not match the unique characteristics of a mesopelagic fishery.
A future BBNJ agreement could strengthen environmental assessments, enable designation of marine protected areas, and complement existing RFMO work.
In the near term, a moratorium on mesopelagic fishing in international waters would allow time for scientific knowledge to develop and for appropriate governance mechanisms to be established before authorisation of any fishery.
The authors stress that the precautionary principle must be strongly applied given the risk of disrupting critical life-support systems.


Limitations

The document is a policy study (IDDRI Study N°06/20), not a peer-reviewed empirical article; it does not present original field data or modelling.
The authors themselves note "considerable gaps in our scientific knowledge" of mesopelagic ecosystems, meaning the ecological baseline on which governance recommendations rest is incomplete.
The BBNJ treaty was still under negotiation at the time of writing (November 2020), so the analysis of its potential governance tools is prospective rather than evaluative of an adopted instrument.
The provided document text is partial; sections on specific RFMO case studies, detailed EIA/SEA comparisons, and the full annex of ongoing research projects are truncated, limiting the depth of methodological detail available for this summary.
As a 2020 publication, the study does not reflect subsequent developments in BBNJ negotiations, RFMO actions, or new mesopelagic research that may have altered the governance landscape.

