@techreport{cremersPreliminaryAnalysisDraft2020,
	type = {Study},
	title = {A preliminary analysis of the drafthigh seas biodiversity treaty},
	copyright = {CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication},
	abstract = {In 2017, following more than a decade of informal discussions, States at the United Nations decided to convene an intergovernmental conference (IGC) to negotiate an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) for the conservation and sustainable use of the biological diversity of marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ). The negotiations for an ILBI are based on a package of issues agreed in 2011, namely: marine genetic resources (MGRs), including questions on the sharing of benefits; measures such as area-based management tools (ABMTs), including marine protected areas (MPAs); environmental impact assessments (EIA); and capacity-building and the transfer of marine technology. United Nations General Assembly (UNGA) Resolution 72/249 provides for four meetings of the IGC. The final session is scheduled to take place from 23 March to 3 April 2020, at the United Nations (UN) Headquarters in New York. This round of negotiations will be based on the revised draft text (released 27 November 2019). This paper provides an initial analysis of the draft text, highlighting key areas of progress and contention, as well as identifying some possible options for strengthening the text.},
	language = {en},
	number = {1},
	institution = {IDDRI},
	author = {Cremers, Klaudija and {Wright, Glen} and {Rochette, Julien} and {Gjerde, Kristina} and {Harden-Davies, Harriet}},
	year = {2020},
	note = {TITLE-CLEAN 2026-09-10: local:midword\_spaces;},
	keywords = {EIA, BBNJ, ABMT / MPAs, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, type:grey, author:Glen Wright, MGR / ABS},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This paper provides a preliminary analysis of the revised draft text (released 27 November 2019) for the BBNJ treaty — an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) for the conservation and sustainable use of biological diversity in marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). The authors aim to highlight key areas of progress and contention in the draft and to identify possible options for strengthening the text ahead of the final Intergovernmental Conference (IGC) session scheduled for 23 March – 3 April 2020.

Methods
The study is a desk-based legal and policy analysis of the draft treaty text (12 parts, 70 articles, 2 annexes). The authors examine:

General provisions and institutional arrangements (Parts I and VI), including the Preamble, objectives, scope, principles, and the proposed governance architecture (COP, scientific and technical body, secretariat, clearing-house mechanism).
The four "package deal" elements: marine genetic resources and access/benefit sharing (Part II); area-based management tools and MPAs (Part III); environmental impact assessments (Part IV); and capacity-building and transfer of marine technology (Part V).

Provisions are assessed against existing instruments (UNCLOS, UNFSA, CBD) and prior negotiation rounds. The authors identify bracketed options, unresolved questions, and gaps, then propose specific textual amendments or additions to strengthen the draft.

Key findings

Many key provisions remain in brackets; the treaty's ultimate ambition depends on choices made in the final negotiation round.
The Preamble is limited and could better reference the inherent value of marine biodiversity, threats (biodiversity loss, climate change), science-based decision-making, and enforcement.
The "long-term" qualifier in the treaty's objective should be retained to align with the ecosystem approach and UNFSA.
The common heritage of mankind principle remains contested and is bracketed; its scope and application are undefined.
Institutional arrangements (COP, scientific/technical body, secretariat, clearing-house mechanism) are outlined but lack detail on functions, funding, and operational modalities. Voting rules should permit decisions beyond consensus to avoid deadlock.
On MGRs: no consensus exists on definitions, scope (derivatives, commodities), whether benefit-sharing is mandatory or voluntary, or whether it includes monetary benefits.
On ABMTs/MPAs: the draft fills a governance gap but leaves open the COP's decision-making powers, the role of existing bodies, proposal requirements (e.g., duration, monitoring/enforcement), and interim/emergency measures.
On EIAs: thresholds, cumulative and transboundary impacts, strategic environmental assessments, and the legal effect of assessments (advisory vs. binding) remain unresolved. Responsibility for conducting assessments is placed with States Parties rather than activity proponents.
On capacity-building: modalities (voluntary vs. mandatory) and reporting obligations are undetermined.
International cooperation and complementary measures under existing frameworks are crucial to the treaty's success; the "not undermining" requirement should be paired with accountability of existing institutions.


Limitations

The analysis is explicitly preliminary and was written before the final IGC session; the draft text it examines was still subject to change.
Scope is limited to Parts I–VI (general provisions, institutional arrangements, and the four package deal elements); issues beyond the package deal are addressed in a separate publication (Wright et al., 2019).
Because many provisions are bracketed or offer multiple alternatives, the authors can only assess options rather than a settled text, making conclusions inherently speculative.
The paper is a policy study (IDDRI Study N°01/2020) rather than a peer-reviewed journal article, though it draws on peer-reviewed literature.
No empirical or quantitative assessment of the draft's potential effectiveness is provided; the analysis is normative and textual.

