@techreport{wrightDiggingDeepCritical2022,
	title = {Digging deep: critical questions remain in the rush to regulate seabed mining},
	abstract = {The 1982 United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) defines areas of maritime jurisdiction and sets out the rights and obligations of States, thus serving as a “Constitution for the ocean”. Whereas Coastal States have exclusive rights to harvest minerals within their national jurisdiction, mineral resources of the seabed beyond national jurisdiction (“the Area”) are subject to a dedicated international legal framework. Under UNCLOS, seabed minerals in the Area are part of the common heritage of humankind: they cannot be appropriated by any one State and benefits arising from their exploitation must be shared. The International Seabed Authority (ISA) is responsible for regulating mining in the international seabed Area and protecting the marine environment. Having operated in relative obscurity since its inception in 1994, the Authority is now firmly in the spotlight, especially since Nauru triggered a rule in the international legal framework in June 2021 that requires adoption of exploitation regulations by 9 July 2023 (the “two-year rule”). At the same time, calls to halt development of seabed mining are growing, including from States, scientists, and the private sector. The ISA is at an inflection point, and States must carefully consider next steps as strong political engagement and action will be essential for finding a way forward. This Issue Brief aims to help States and stakeholders to understand the legal framework and the current state of play, with a particular focus on the two-year rule. It outlines possible pathways to averting deadlock and instituting a precautionary pause on the development of mining activities.},
	institution = {IDDRI},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Cremers, Klaudija and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2022},
	keywords = {BBNJ, ISA, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright, EIA / SEA},
	pages = {8},
	annote = {[role]
lead author
[type]
report
[selected]
},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This Issue Brief (IDDRI, May 2023) aims to help States and stakeholders understand the legal framework governing seabed mineral extraction in the international seabed Area, the current state of regulatory development at the International Seabed Authority (ISA), and the legal uncertainties surrounding the "two-year rule" triggered by Nauru in June 2021. It outlines possible pathways to avert deadlock in the ISA Council and to institute a precautionary pause on mining activities.

Methods
The document is a policy and legal analysis brief rather than an empirical study. It proceeds through the following analytical steps:

Overview of the UNCLOS legal framework and the ISA's institutional structure (Assembly, Council, Legal and Technical Commission).
Review of the state of play on exploitation regulations and the triggering of the two-year rule by Nauru.
Analysis of legal uncertainties in interpreting the two-year rule, including the roles of the Council and the LTC in a provisional approval process.
Summary of outcomes from the 28th Council meeting (March 16–31, 2023).
Assessment of possible pathways forward: a Council vote, an ITLOS advisory opinion, or an Assembly general policy implementing a precautionary pause.


Key findings

The ISA is at an inflection point: exploitation regulations remain under-developed, the two-year deadline (9 July 2023) is imminent, and there is no consensus on the interpretation of key legal provisions.
The governance framework is not yet ready to regulate seabed mining in line with the ISA's mandate to protect the marine environment and ensure benefits for humankind as a whole.
Significant legal uncertainty exists regarding what "consider and provisionally approve" means under the two-year rule, the respective roles of the Council and LTC, and the consequences of provisional approval.
The March 2023 Council decision provided limited clarity, did not define directives the Council may give to the LTC, and left the approval process in a "legal loophole."
Scientific evidence is insufficient to assess environmental risks; valid environmental baselines may take years or decades to establish.
A growing coalition of 14 States, over 700 scientists, major corporations, UN bodies, and financial institutions calls for a halt or moratorium on deep-sea mining.
The Assembly, as the supreme organ of the ISA, could adopt a general policy implementing a precautionary pause, drawing on precedents such as the IWC whaling moratorium and the UN driftnet moratorium.


Limitations

The brief is a policy analysis from a single institution (IDDRI, a French think tank) and reflects a particular institutional perspective; it is not a peer-reviewed empirical study.
The analysis is highly time-sensitive, written ahead of the July 2023 Council and Assembly meetings; developments after May 2023 are not captured.
The legal interpretation of the two-year rule is acknowledged as contested, with "compelling legal arguments" available for multiple readings; the brief does not resolve these disputes.
The document notes that the exploitation regulations are still under negotiation, so the regulatory landscape it describes is provisional and subject to change.
The brief does not include primary data on environmental impacts, economic costs, or benefit-sharing mechanisms; it relies on cited secondary sources and legal texts.
Information on the specific positions of individual States beyond those explicitly named (e.g., the African Group, France, Nauru) is limited.

