	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2023 IDDRI Policy Brief (N°01/23) by Gjerde, Clark, Chazot, Cremers, Harden-Davies, Kachelriess, Payne, Rodriguez-Chaves, Spadone, Thiele, Vierros, Goettsche-Wanli, and Wright provides initial reflections to support rapid, effective, and equitable implementation of the BBNJ Agreement (conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity beyond national jurisdiction). The authors identify three priority areas for preparatory action: (1) bringing the Agreement into force, (2) establishing the institutional framework including financial mechanisms, and (3) developing capacity, science, and technology. The brief was published in February 2023, ahead of the expected conclusion of negotiations at IGC5 (February 20–March 3, 2023).

Methods
The document is a normative policy brief rather than an empirical study. It draws on comparative analysis of existing international treaty processes (UNCLOS, the UN Fish Stocks Agreement, the Port State Measures Agreement, the Ballast Water Convention, and the Paris Climate Agreement) to identify lessons for accelerating ratification and implementation. It is based on the authors' earlier 2022 article in NPJ Ocean Sustainability (Gjerde et al., 2022) and references the most recent BBNJ "further refreshed draft text." No original data collection, modelling, or statistical analysis is reported.

Key findings
The brief offers prescriptive recommendations across three domains:

Bringing the Agreement into force: Preparatory work to encourage ratification should begin immediately. Recommended actions include targeted outreach (regional workshops, webinars, high-level events), a special treaty event at signature, technical and financial support for national codification (model laws, legal checklists, legislative guides), and innovative funding mechanisms to support implementation before entry into force. The authors note that UNCLOS required 60 ratifications and took 12 years, while the UN Fish Stocks Agreement (30 ratifications) took 6 years, underscoring the need for momentum.
Building institutional mechanisms: A Preparatory Commission (PrepCom) should be established by IGC or UNGA resolution to prepare for the first COP. The PrepCom could create interim working groups (scientific/technical, finance, ABS) to develop institutional structures, financial mechanisms, clearing-house mechanisms, and implementation/compliance guidance. A dedicated ABS working group could prepare expert rosters and interim benefit-sharing guidelines.
Developing capacity, science, and technology: The authors recommend national and regional needs assessments, targeted financial support, and collaboration among UN agencies, NGOs, and academia. Specific preparatory actions include supporting first MPA proposals, building EIA/SEA capacity, advancing MGR-related scientific partnerships, and developing a process for incorporating traditional knowledge. A High Seas Finance Implementation Mechanism supported by public and private partners is proposed to enable preparatory work before the treaty enters into force and to transition toward a stable BBNJ funding model.


Limitations
The document does not include a formal limitations section. However, several constraints are evident from the text itself:

The brief was written before the BBNJ Agreement was concluded; it references a "further refreshed draft text" rather than a final instrument, so specific article references and thresholds (e.g., 30 vs. 60 ratifications) remained unresolved at the time of writing.
As a policy brief, it is prescriptive and normative; it does not present empirical evidence, quantitative modelling, or systematic review of implementation outcomes.
The recommendations are broad and high-level; operational details (e.g., specific funding amounts, institutional design choices) are left to future PrepCom and COP processes.
The brief does not address potential political obstacles to ratification, divergent national interests, or enforcement challenges in detail.

