Objective
This 2014 policy briefing by Celine Marcy and Glen Wright (Institute for Sustainable Development and International Relations, IDDRI), presented at the International Oceans Institute in Malta, reviews the state of negotiations toward a new binding international instrument on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ). It maps the timeline of the BBNJ (Biodiversity Beyond National Jurisdiction) Working Group process, examines the four-pillar "Package Deal" agreed in 2011, identifies points of consensus and contention among States, and outlines next steps toward a potential 2016 negotiation mandate.

Methods
The document is a descriptive policy briefing rather than an empirical study. It synthesises information from BBNJ Working Group meetings (April and June 2014), the 2012 Rio+20 outcome, and relevant regional frameworks. The authors structure their analysis around the four elements of the Package Deal, use the designation of marine protected areas (MPAs) in ABNJ as a worked example to illustrate unresolved procedural questions, and survey interim regional approaches (OSPAR/NEAFC, ATS/CCAMLR, Sargasso Sea Commission) as models for potential implementation.

Key findings

The 2011 Package Deal comprises four elements: (1) marine genetic resources and benefit-sharing, (2) area-based management tools including MPAs, (3) environmental impact assessments, and (4) capacity-building and transfer of marine technology.
A "coalition of the willing" was forming, with progressive involvement of developing states (Pacific nations, CARICOM, African Union) and greater civil-society inclusion, though a number of States were impeding consensus.
Limited points of agreement included: maintaining the Rio+20 deadline, the primacy of UNCLOS, retention of the Package Deal structure, suspension of the "common heritage" versus "freedom" debate, and respect for the mandates of existing sectoral organisations.
Overarching unresolved issues span legal gaps versus implementation gaps, the practical meaning of respecting existing institutional mandates, fisheries governance, the international-versus-regional divide, and the institutional structure of any new agreement.
MPA designation in ABNJ raises complex procedural questions: scientific criteria (VME, EBSA, OSPAR/Mediterranean/IMO/ISA standards), who may propose areas, who reviews proposals, and whether a new scientific/technical committee is needed or existing institutions should be used.
Interim regional mechanisms (North Atlantic, Antarctic, Sargasso Sea) demonstrate that area-based management is feasible but fragmented, reinforcing the need for a coherent global instrument that still utilises existing tools.
Projected next steps: a BBNJ "Convergence Report," further State submissions, a final BBNJ meeting in January 2015, a UNGA decision in August/September 2015, and possible commencement of negotiations in 2016.


Limitations

The document is a conference presentation (slide-based briefing), not a peer-reviewed article, so it lacks formal methodology, citations, and critical depth.
It is a point-in-time snapshot (December 2014) of an ongoing negotiation; outcomes after that date are not reflected.
The "Further reading" section is empty, and several analytical points (e.g., the MPA designation table) are presented as open questions rather than resolved findings.
The briefing does not provide a detailed legal analysis of specific UNCLOS provisions or a comparative assessment of the regional mechanisms mentioned.
Authorship is listed as "Wright" in the metadata, but the document credits both Celine Marcy and Glen Wright; the metadata does not capture the full authorship.

