@misc{wrightMarineGeneticResources2012,
	title = {Marine {Genetic} {Resources} in {Areas} {Beyond} {National} {Jurisdiction}: an annotated bibliography},
	author = {Wright, Glen},
	month = feb,
	year = {2012},
	keywords = {BBNJ, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright, MGR / ABS},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
Glen Wright (2012), a PhD candidate at the Australian National University School of Law, compiled an annotated bibliography of 33 scholarly works (published 2000–2010) addressing the legal regulation of Marine Genetic Resources (MGR) in Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ). The work maps the state of legal scholarship on this topic, identifies gaps in the existing international legal framework, and catalogues the range of proposed regulatory solutions debated in international fora such as the CBD, the ICP on Oceans and the Law of the Sea, and the IWG.

Methods
The document is a structured annotated bibliography rather than an original empirical study. Each of the 33 entries includes:

A full citation of the source.
A brief summary of the source's argument or focus.
The "approach advocated" by the author (e.g., new treaty, ISA expansion, implementing agreement, compulsory licensing).
Detailed notes extracting key legal arguments, relevant treaty provisions (UNCLOS Parts VII, XI, XIII; CBD; TRIPS), and positions of developed versus developing states.

A glossary of approximately 40 acronyms (ABNJ, CHM, FOTHS, ISA, MGR, etc.) is provided at the outset to orient the reader. The sources span law journals, IUCN position papers, institutional reports (IDDRI, CBD Secretariat), and policy analyses.

Key findings
Across the 33 annotated sources, several recurring themes emerge:

Legal uncertainty over MGR status: There is no consensus on whether MGR in ABNJ fall under Freedom of the High Seas (UNCLOS Part VII), the Common Heritage of Mankind regime (Part XI), or require an entirely new legal instrument. Developed states generally favour FOTHS; developing states (G77, China) argue MGR are part of the CHM and should be regulated by the ISA.
Regulatory gaps: UNCLOS does not specifically define or regulate bioprospecting or MGR. There is no mandatory EIA or monitoring regime for MSR/bioprospecting in ABNJ, and no equitable benefit-sharing mechanism exists outside the Area.
IP and benefit-sharing tension: Multiple sources (Lawson \& Downing 2002; Salpin \& Germani 2007; Prows 2007; Zewers 2007) identify a fundamental conflict between TRIPS patent rights and the UNCLOS/CBD objectives of conservation and benefit-sharing for all mankind.
Proposed solutions: The most frequently advocated options include an implementing agreement to UNCLOS (La Fayette 2009; Lowry 2007; IUCN 2008), expansion of the ISA mandate (Bonney 2006; Scovazzi 2004; SBSTTA reports), a new standalone treaty (Matz 2002), MPAs, compulsory licensing mechanisms (Zewers 2007), and material transfer agreements (Putterman 1995).
MSR vs. bioprospecting distinction: Several sources note the difficulty of distinguishing pure scientific research from commercial bioprospecting, complicating any regulatory design.


Limitations

The work is a secondary compilation; it does not present original legal analysis or empirical data but synthesises the positions of other authors.
The source pool is limited to 2000–2010 publications, so it does not reflect subsequent developments (e.g., the 2018 BBNJ negotiations or the 2023 Agreement).
Two entries (McLaughlin 2003; Safrin 2004) are explicitly noted as addressing MGR within national jurisdiction rather than ABNJ, which is outside the stated scope.
One cited EU proposal (Bossar et al. 2010) is flagged as unavailable on the EU website, limiting verifiability.
As a 2012 snapshot, the legal and political landscape (ICP outcomes, IWG deliberations) was still in flux, and the bibliography reflects an unresolved debate rather than a settled legal position.

