@techreport{glenwrightMarineRenewableEnergy2015,
	title = {Marine {Renewable} {Energy}: {Community}, {Consultation} and {Planning} ({Summary} of the {International} network for {Social} {Studies} of {Marine} {Energy} ({ISSMER}) online seminar series, {November} 2014 - {January} 2015)},
	abstract = {The MRE industry is at a crucial moment. Devices are now moving from engineering drawings to full-scale prototypes, and the first commercial scale wave and tide energy farms have been announced and are being planned. Around the world countries are assessing the potential energy resource in their national waters, new developments in national electricity network infrastructures are in the planning stages, and large areas of the sea are being allocated to this new industry. This process of commercialisation is creating new interactions between environment, technology, and society. These take place in coastal communities, socio-economic markets, between other users of the sea, with policy-makers, amongst MRE companies, and between governments. ISSMER aims to support collaboration with these diverse communities, and to coordinate international research activities in the social studies of MRE.},
	institution = {ISSMER},
	author = {{Glen Wright}},
	year = {2015},
	keywords = {author:Glen Wright, EIA / SEA},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This document summarizes the International Network for Social Studies of Marine Energy (ISSMER) online seminar series held between November 2014 and January 2015. ISSMER was established to bring together interdisciplinary researchers (socio-cultural, economic, geographic, philosophical, organisational, and political) working on the social dimensions of marine renewable energy (MRE). The series addressed three thematic areas—Community, Consultation, and Planning—at a moment when MRE devices were transitioning from engineering drawings to full-scale prototypes and the first commercial-scale wave and tide energy farms were being planned.

Methods
The document is a summary of three online seminars, each featuring multiple presenters followed by group discussion. It is not a traditional empirical study but a synthesis of expert presentations and facilitated dialogue:

Community (27 Nov 2014): Presentations by Sandy Kerr (Heriot-Watt), Glen Wright (IDDRI), and Simon Jude (Cranfield) on community benefits, rights and ownership in marine space, and community burden.
Consultation (15 Dec 2014): Presentations by Carly McLachlan (Tyndall Centre), Bouke Wiersma (Exeter), and Laura Watts (IT Copenhagen) on consultation expectations, local knowledge in Guernsey, and the "Energy Walk" engagement method.
Planning (14 Jan 2015): Presentations by Shelley Macdougall (Acadia), Tavis Potts (Aberdeen), and Kate Johnson (Heriot-Watt) on the value of project delay, the Blue Economy concept, and the politics of marine spatial planning.


Key findings

Community benefit payments in MRE are shaped by power relationships; where communities hold planning power, fair shares are more likely, whereas offshore MRE local authorities have limited influence, and payments often serve to facilitate consenting rather than reflect genuine benefit-sharing.
MRE's large, permanent, nearshore spatial requirements conflict with traditional "freedom of the seas" principles, indigenous rights, and other existing marine permissions, effectively privatising public marine space.
Developer insolvencies create risk for communities that have invested public money in MRE infrastructure with no recourse if projects fail, while MRE is uniquely asked to make community payments that other energy sources are not.
Consultation processes in MRE face conflicting stakeholder expectations, "consultation fatigue" from overlapping processes, and ambiguity over whether consultation aims at democratic legitimacy or advancing the industry.
Upstream consultation (pre-design) can identify community attitudes, suitable locations, and reduce conflict, as illustrated by the Guernsey case study.
The option to delay MRE projects has quantifiable economic value (estimable via Black-Scholes option pricing), and developers routinely use risk-reward calculations to defer investment when uncertainty is high.
The "Blue Economy" paradigm is critiqued for prioritising marine industrial expansion over environmental sustainability and for lacking a formal definition.
Marine spatial planning (MSP) is inherently political, with three broad governance approaches (state-controlled, market-based, consultative/democratic), and the diffuse nature of marine communities makes planning more complex than on land.


Limitations
The document does not explicitly state limitations. However, it is a summary of seminar discussions rather than a peer-reviewed empirical study, so findings reflect expert opinion and case examples rather than systematic evidence. The scope is limited to three thematic seminars over a three-month period. The document does not report on data collection, sample sizes, or analytical methods beyond the presenters' individual case studies. Future directions noted by ISSMER (broadening to the full Blue Economy, internationalising to developing countries, and investigating political power structures) suggest the current scope is narrow and primarily focused on developed, English-speaking contexts.
