@article{wrightHistoricTalksCould2016,
	title = {Historic {UNtalks} couldsave the high seas},
	url = {https://www.iddri.org/fr/publications-et-evenements/publication-scientifique/historic-un-talks-could-save-high-seas},
	doi = {10.64628/aak.jt4fdq3p3},
	abstract = {Since the adoption of the United Nations (UN) Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) in 1982, human activities in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ)1 have developed exponentially. Existing activities such as shipping and fishing have intensified and expanded, while a range of new activities are under development.2 Climate change and ocean acidification are predicted to compound the impacts of these activities and place further pressure on marine ecosystems.3 In 2004, the UN General Assembly (UNGA) created the Ad Hoc Open-ended Informal Working Group to study issues relating to the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity beyond areas of national jurisdiction (the Working Group).4 The focus of the Working Group was mainly been on weaknesses and gaps in the current international framework and whether these necessitate the adoption of a new instrument. In January 2015, the Working Group recommended to the UNGA that it “decide to develop an international legallybinding instrument under the Convention on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction”.5 A Preparatory Committee (PrepCom) will meet at UN Headquarters for a total of 4 weeks in 2016 and 2017 in order to prepare substantive recommendations on elements of a draft text.6 The PrepCom is to report to the UNGA, which will then decide by September 2018 on the convening and starting date of an intergovernmental conference.},
	language = {fr},
	urldate = {2025-08-17},
	journal = {Australian Environment Review},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Rochette, Julien},
	month = aug,
	year = {2016},
	note = {TITLE-CLEAN 2026-09-10: local:midword\_spaces;},
	keywords = {BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright, EIA / SEA},
	pages = {164--167},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This policy brief by Wright and Rochette (2016), published in the Australian Environment Review, examines the trajectory of UN negotiations toward a legally binding instrument under UNCLOS for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). It situates the first Preparatory Committee (PrepCom) meeting within the broader history of the Ad Hoc Working Group (2004–2015) and identifies the key governance gaps, points of contention, and challenges ahead for the intergovernmental process.

Methods
The authors employ a qualitative policy-analysis approach. They review the institutional and legal history of ocean governance (UNCLOS 1982, the Working Group, the 2011 "Package Deal" agreement), catalogue the structural weaknesses of the existing framework, and report on the proceedings of the first PrepCom session (30 April – 8 May 2016, New York). The analysis draws on UN documents, IISD Earth Negotiations Bulletins, and the authors' own institutional knowledge from IDDRI. No empirical data collection or quantitative methods are used.

Key findings

The current ocean governance framework is characterised as fragmented, with multiple overlapping mandates (FAO, RFMOs, ISA, IMO) and no comprehensive set of overarching principles for ABNJ.
Eight specific governance gaps are identified: absence of global MPA rules, legal uncertainty over marine genetic resources (MGRs), lack of global EIA/SEA rules, limited capacity building, ineffective high-seas fisheries governance, and the "genuine link" flag-state problem.
The 2011 "Package Deal" frames negotiations around four issues: MGRs and benefit-sharing, area-based management tools (including MPAs), environmental impact assessments, and capacity building/technology transfer.
The MGRs question remains the most intractable: the G77+China bloc favours the "common heritage of mankind" principle, while others insist on "freedom of the high seas." The EU called for a pragmatic benefit-sharing mechanism that sidesteps the ideological status question.
Most states supported a science-based MPA network, though a few resisted MPAs as an end in themselves, demanding they be threat-specific and time-limited.
Discussion of fisheries shifted from "whether to include" to "how to include," with most delegations acknowledging destructive bottom trawling as the greatest threat to ABNJ biodiversity.
EIA and capacity-building discussions were less contentious in principle but lacked practical detail on thresholds, compliance, and institutional mechanisms.
Additional negotiation challenges flagged include funding for implementation, institutional arrangements, and ensuring the new instrument does not undermine existing bodies' mandates.


Limitations

The document is a policy brief rather than a peer-reviewed empirical study; it contains no original data collection, statistical analysis, or formal methodology section.
It reflects the state of negotiations only as of the first PrepCom session (May 2016); the final outcome of the intergovernmental conference (scheduled after September 2018) was still unknown at the time of writing.
The authors are affiliated with IDDRI, a think tank with stated policy positions on ocean governance, which may shape the framing and emphasis of the analysis.
The brief does not engage in depth with the scientific evidence base for specific conservation measures (e.g., MPA effectiveness, EIA outcomes), relying instead on cited literature for background.
No information is provided on peer-review status, funding sources, or conflicts of interest beyond the authors' institutional affiliation.

