@techreport{rochetteToutComprendreProchaines2016,
	title = {Tout comprendre des prochaines négociations sur la gouvernance de la haute mer},
	language = {fr},
	institution = {IDDRI},
	author = {Rochette, Julien and Wright, Glen},
	month = mar,
	year = {2016},
	note = {TITLE-FINAL 2026-09-10: ocr\_local\_collapse; was: Tout comprendre des prochaines négociations sur la gouvernance de la haute mer
TITLE-REVERT 2026-09-10: restored pre-strict; bad note=ocr\_local\_collapse},
	keywords = {BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2016 policy brief by Rochette and Wright (IDDRI) aims to explain the key issues surrounding the upcoming negotiations for a legally binding international agreement on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ, or "ZAJN" in French). It was published ahead of the first session of the UN Preparatory Committee, scheduled for 28 March – 8 April 2016 in New York, and is structured as a ten-point explainer for a general and policy audience.

Methods
The document is a policy brief rather than an empirical research study. It synthesizes existing legal frameworks (notably the 1982 UN Convention on the Law of the Sea), publicly available data on ocean activities, and the political history of ABNJ governance discussions since the early 2000s. No original data collection, modelling, or peer-reviewed analytical method is described. The authors present the material as a structured overview of ten key questions.

Key findings

ABNJ comprises two legally distinct spaces: the high seas (water column beyond 200-nautical-mile EEZs, governed by a principle of freedom) and "the Area" (seabed and subsoil beyond continental shelves, designated common heritage of humanity).
ABNJ covers roughly half the planet's surface and hosts exceptional biodiversity, including deep-sea fish, polymetallic nodules, hydrothermal vents, seamounts, and cold-water corals.
Human pressures have grown exponentially since 1982: approximately 90 \% of global trade is maritime; high-seas fishing has expanded as coastal stocks depleted; 26 mineral exploration contracts have been issued; bioprospecting is expanding; and emerging activities (offshore aquaculture, geoengineering) are under study.
Significant legal gaps persist: no mechanism exists to establish marine protected areas in the high seas; no mandatory environmental impact assessment regime applies; and genetic-resource exploitation follows a "first come, first served" rule that Southern countries view as inequitable.
A political divide emerged early: the EU and many others favoured a new binding agreement, while the United States, Russia, and Canada preferred better implementation of existing instruments. In 2015, states agreed to open formal negotiations.
The negotiation process is two-stage: a Preparatory Committee (2016–2017) followed by a decision before September 2018 to convene an intergovernmental conference. The authors judge it unlikely the agreement will be concluded before 2020.
Four thematic pillars frame the negotiations: (1) marine genetic resources and benefit-sharing; (2) area-based management tools including marine protected areas; (3) environmental impact assessments; and (4) capacity-building and marine technology transfer.


Limitations

The document is a short policy flyer (two pages), not a peer-reviewed article; it contains no formal references, citations, or methodological apparatus.
It reflects the institutional perspective of IDDRI, a French think tank, and the EU negotiating position, which may colour the framing of the "legal gaps" and the urgency of a new agreement.
All information is time-bound to March 2016; subsequent developments in the BBNJ negotiations (which ultimately produced the 2023 High Seas Treaty) are, by definition, absent.
No empirical data, quantitative analysis, or comparative case study is presented; claims about biodiversity, trade volumes, and contract numbers are stated without sourcing.
The document does not discuss the views of indigenous or small-island developing states in depth, nor does it address enforcement and compliance mechanisms in detail.

