@techreport{wrightSeaChangeNegotiating2016,
	title = {Sea change: {Negotiating} a new agreement on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction},
	language = {en},
	institution = {IDDRI},
	author = {Wright, Glen and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2016},
	keywords = {EIA, BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, type:grey, author:Glen Wright},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This issue brief, produced by the Institut du développement durable et des relations internationales (IDDRI), provides a concise overview of the state of negotiations for a new international legally binding instrument on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), to be developed under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS). It aims to inform readers about the governance gaps in ABNJ, the history of the BBNJ Working Group process, the positions of major State groupings, and the key challenges facing the upcoming Preparatory Committee (PrepCom) negotiations.

Methods
The brief is a desk-based policy analysis. The authors draw on:

Review of the existing international ocean governance framework (UNCLOS, Aichi Targets, SDGs, and sectoral instruments managed by FAO, ISA, IMO, and RFMOs).
Chronological summary of the nine meetings of the Ad Hoc Open-ended Informal Working Group (BBNJ Working Group) from 2006 to January 2015, including intersessional workshops.
Analysis of State positions as expressed in Working Group sessions, grouped by bloc (EU; G77, China, Mexico; Africa, Caribbean, Pacific; and reluctant States such as the US, Canada, Russia).
Identification of substantive negotiation issues (the "Package Deal" and institutional questions) based on UN documents, including UNGA Resolution 69/292 (June 2015).


Key findings

ABNJ covers nearly half the Earth's surface and holds significant biodiversity, yet the current governance framework is fragmented, lacks a global MPA mechanism, has legal uncertainty over marine genetic resources (MGRs), and has no global EIA/SEA rules.
In June 2015, the UNGA adopted Resolution 69/292, launching negotiations for a new legally binding instrument under UNCLOS — a historic decision following nearly ten years of informal discussions.
The agreed "Package Deal" comprises four substantive elements: MGRs (including benefit-sharing); area-based management tools (including MPAs); environmental impact assessments; and capacity building and transfer of marine technology.
The EU has been the leading proponent of an Implementing Agreement to UNCLOS, occupying a middle ground between the "common heritage of mankind" and "freedom of the high seas" principles on MGRs.
The G77, China, and Mexico pushed for an access-and-benefit-sharing (ABS) mechanism for MGRs and strong capacity-building provisions, while showing some flexibility on the legal status of MGRs.
African, Caribbean, and Pacific States became increasingly vocal in 2014–2015, arguing that a binding agreement is the only feasible solution for equitable benefit-sharing.
A small number of States (US, Canada, Russia) expressed reluctance, arguing the existing framework suffices and that a new agreement would add little value, though all States ultimately agreed by consensus to proceed.
Key negotiation challenges identified include reconciling competing principles on MGRs, designing MPA criteria and enforcement, establishing EIA thresholds and processes, creating institutional arrangements without undermining existing bodies, improving high-seas fisheries governance, and securing equitable funding.


Limitations

The brief is explicitly the short version of a longer study (IDDRI Study N°01/16), so depth of analysis is necessarily limited.
It is a snapshot as of March 2016, preceding the first PrepCom session; it does not reflect any negotiation outcomes or subsequent developments.
The authors note that the summary of State positions is "necessarily only an overview" and that the G77, being a large and varied group, does not maintain unified positions on all issues.
The document does not include primary legal text analysis or empirical data on the ecological state of ABNJ ecosystems; it focuses on the political and institutional dimensions of the negotiation process.

