@techreport{cremersOptionsStrengtheningMonitoring2021,
	title = {Options for {Strengthening} {Monitoring}, {Control} and {Surveillance} of {Human} {Activities} in the {Southeast} {Atlantic} {Region}},
	abstract = {Effective monitoring, control and surveillance (MCS) of human activities is critical for the conservation and sustainable use of the ocean. This is particularly important in the Southeast Atlantic, where highly productive waters foster strong fishing pressure. The STRONG High Seas project (“Strengthening Regional Ocean Governance for the High Seas”) explores ways to enhance the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ) through enhanced regional governance, including the strengthening of MCS measures.

In this context, IDDRI and the Secretariat of the Abidjan Convention organised a two-day online experts’ workshop entitled “Monitoring, Control and Surveillance of Human Activities in Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ) in Western Africa” (5 and 6 May 2021). The workshop gathered around 40 MCS experts of the Southeast Atlantic region, with representatives from governments, regional organisations, NGOs and academia. 

Based on the experts’ workshop, literature reviews and interviews with relevant stakeholders, this report explores the challenges of MCS in the Southeast Atlantic region, highlighting best practice examples, ongoing initiatives developed both at national and regional levels, and provides recommendations for decision-makers to strengthen MCS.

The report concludes that Southeast Atlantic States can strengthen MCS in the region through improving the collection and sharing of data, enhanced cooperation and coordination, the harmonization of legislation and deterrent sanctions, investing in capacity-building and through more involvement in global and regional processes.},
	language = {en},
	institution = {STRONG High Seas},
	author = {Cremers, Klaudija and Bouvet, Morgane and Wright, Glen and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2021},
	keywords = {fisheries, BBNJ, RFMO, featured, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This report, produced under the STRONG High Seas project ("Strengthening Regional Ocean Governance for the High Seas"), aims to explore challenges of Monitoring, Control and Surveillance (MCS) of human activities in the Southeast Atlantic region and to provide actionable recommendations for decision-makers. The broader goal is to enhance the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ) through strengthened regional governance and MCS measures, particularly in a region where highly productive waters attract intense fishing pressure.

Methods
The report draws on three complementary sources of evidence:

A two-day online experts' workshop (5–6 May 2021) co-organised by IDDRI and the Secretariat of the Abidjan Convention, gathering approximately 40–45 MCS experts representing governments, regional organisations, NGOs, and academia from the Southeast Atlantic region.
Structured literature reviews covering existing MCS frameworks, regional agreements, and best-practice examples.
Semi-structured interviews with relevant stakeholders, including representatives from RFMOs, port authorities, civil society organisations (e.g., Stop Illegal Fishing, Environmental Justice Foundation), and international bodies (e.g., European Fisheries Control Agency, FAO).

The work is situated within the five-year STRONG High Seas project (June 2017 – May 2022), coordinated by IASS with IDDRI as implementing partner, and contributes to the Partnership for Regional Ocean Governance (PROG).

Key findings
The report identifies five interrelated pillars through which Southeast Atlantic States can strengthen MCS:

Data collection and sharing: Generalise use of low-cost tools such as AIS and VMS; improve cross-border data exchange; create or extend operational-level task forces with cross-border hot pursuit functions.
Cooperation and coordination: Expand the Abidjan Convention BBNJ Working Group mandate to cover MCS; adopt MoUs (similar to the Abuja MoU) with mandates extending beyond port State control to all human activities in ABNJ; create the COREP regional MCS centre and the SADC MCSCC.
Harmonisation of legislation and deterrent sanctions: Establish national MCS strategies; apply consistent, escalating sanctions for repeat offenders; refuse flag or fishing authorisation to vessels linked to shell companies; close Flag of Convenience registries; implement the right of hot pursuit; adopt a regional observer programme and harmonised port inspection programme.
Capacity-building: Maintain dedicated MCS budgets; develop inter-agency cooperation and training across the legal chain; pool resources where appropriate.
Global and regional processes: Ratify and implement relevant international agreements; reassess fisheries partnership agreements; strengthen MCS provisions in the BBNJ treaty under negotiation; contribute to WTO fisheries-subsidy negotiations; set global minimum traceability standards; implement catch documentation schemes and eco-labelling.

The report also highlights the strategic role of ports in MCS, the Port State Measures Agreement (PSMA), and the Abuja MoU, and documents best-practice examples such as the Port Task Force Ghana and the West Africa Task Force.

Limitations
The provided document text does not include a dedicated limitations section. The report is a policy-oriented synthesis based on a single workshop, literature reviews, and stakeholder interviews rather than primary empirical data collection. Its scope is limited to the Southeast Atlantic region, and recommendations are framed for decision-makers rather than tested through implementation. No quantitative assessment of MCS effectiveness or cost-benefit analysis is presented in the available text.
