Objective
This policy brief, produced under the STRONG High Seas Project, aims to explore how integrated ecosystem-based management (EBM) in marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ) can be advanced at the regional level. It examines how the then-negotiating BBNJ Agreement (an international legally binding instrument under UNCLOS for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in ABNJ) can build on experiences in other legally binding agreements to strengthen regional cooperation, coordination, and coherence. The authors seek to develop concrete options for strengthening the President's draft text of the BBNJ Agreement to advance EBM.

Methods
The authors employ a policy-analysis and comparative-legal approach. Specifically, they:

Review the May 2019 President's draft text of the BBNJ Agreement to identify where the text could be strengthened to advance EBM.
Draw on prior STRONG High Seas Project publications that assessed existing regional ocean governance frameworks in the Southeast Atlantic and Southeast Pacific.
Compare provisions in existing instruments (UNCLOS, CBD, UNFSA, CMS, the Madrid Protocol, ISA regulations, and UNGA resolutions) to identify transferable obligations and principles.
Identify five "building blocks" for EBM and map specific options for the BBNJ Agreement against examples from those instruments, presented in a summary table.


Key findings
The brief identifies five building blocks for advancing EBM through the BBNJ Agreement:

A robust global body (e.g., a Conference of Parties) capable of taking decisions and adopting recommendations.
A suite of regional mechanisms for integrated policy development and coordination.
Effective science-policy advisory mechanisms.
Overarching environmental obligations and principles (e.g., precautionary approach, in-situ conservation of ecosystems and natural habitats).
Operational principles to ensure good governance.

Key substantive recommendations include:

Elaborating the duty to cooperate (from UNCLOS, CBD, UNFSA) with specific obligations to pursue conservation measures directly and through competent organizations, including new ones where necessary.
Extending conservation obligations to ecosystem-based approaches, a network of global MPAs, and protection of marine migratory species throughout their range.
Mainstreaming biodiversity into national planning, sectoral and cross-sectoral policies, and environmental/strategic assessments.
Adopting more specific precautionary standards (reference points, environmental indicators, triggers, pre-agreed responses) and requiring that absence of information not be used to postpone conservation measures.
Building regional platforms for cooperation, including duties to ensure compatibility of measures, account for ecological and biological connectivity, and support the special requirements of developing States.

The authors note that ABNJ covers nearly half the Earth's surface and that the current fragmented, sectoral governance regime (IMO, ISA, RFMOs, etc.) is widely recognized as insufficient for ensuring sustainability of marine resources and ecosystems.

Limitations

The document is a policy brief, not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it offers normative recommendations rather than testable hypotheses or quantitative findings.
The analysis is based on an advance, unedited draft text of the BBNJ Agreement (May 2019) that was still subject to negotiation, so the recommendations are provisional and contingent on the final treaty text.
The brief focuses on two specific regions (Southeast Atlantic and Southeast Pacific) for its empirical grounding, limiting generalizability to other ABNJ regions.
Parts of the document text provided for this summary are truncated (marked with "[…]"), so some intermediate arguments and the full scope of the comparative table may not be fully represented here.
The brief does not include a formal limitations section; the points above are inferred from the document's scope, genre, and stated purpose.

