	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
Rochette and Wright (2016), presenting on behalf of IDDRI at an Informal Meeting in Costa Rica (17–18 February 2016), aimed to develop options for Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) in Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ). The work was prepared in support of negotiations toward a new international instrument for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity beyond national jurisdiction.

Methods
The document is a policy and legal analysis structured as a presentation. It proceeds through the following analytical steps:

Defines EIA (citing CBD 1992, Art. 14) and outlines the standard EIA process (screening, scoping, assessment, notification, reporting, decision-making).
Reviews the history and current state of EIA within national jurisdictions, noting adoption in over 100 jurisdictions.
Identifies trends, including growing use, poor quality, and the emergence of marine-specific processes.
Analyses the legal basis for EIA in ABNJ under UNCLOS (Arts. 192, 204–206) and customary international law (ICJ Pulp Mills 2010; ITLOS Seabed Disputes Chamber 2011).
Maps existing sectoral and regional EIA provisions (ISA/Part XI, London Convention, Fish Stocks Agreement, Barcelona Convention, Madrid Protocol, Espoo Convention and Kiev Protocol).
Identifies activity sectors not yet covered by EIA obligations in ABNJ.
Proposes what a new agreement could add and raises open discussion questions for negotiators.


Key findings

Despite widespread adoption, EIA outcomes are mixed: decision-makers retain wide discretion and final outcomes are rarely directly restricted.
Marine EIA is inherently difficult due to poorly understood physical processes, high variability, depth, pressure, remoteness, and high cost.
UNCLOS imposes an obligation to assess potential effects of planned activities (Art. 206) but lacks a uniform, enforceable EIA process for ABNJ.
Existing sectoral instruments cover seabed mining, dumping, and some fisheries, but leave significant gaps: cables and pipelines, marine scientific research, bioprospecting, shipping, floating installations, military activities, and emerging uses of the seas.
Regional agreements (e.g., Barcelona Convention) contain only loosely worded suggestions rather than binding EIA obligations.
The Madrid Protocol (Antarctica) and Espoo Convention (transboundary EIA) offer more structured models, including multi-level screening and Strategic Environmental Assessment.
A new ABNJ instrument could reaffirm the EIA obligation, set a clear uniform process, provide for cumulative assessment and SEA, fill coverage gaps, act as a safety net complementing MPAs, and offer technical support to developing States.


Limitations

The document is a policy brief and meeting presentation, not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it does not present original data or field research.
It identifies gaps and raises discussion questions (threshold for EIA, coverage scope, responsible party, decision-making powers, review mechanisms, institutional arrangements) but does not resolve them or propose specific treaty language.
The analysis of "good practice" and stakeholder involvement remains at a general, normative level without case-study evidence of effectiveness in marine contexts.
No information is provided on the authors' institutional funding, potential conflicts of interest, or the extent to which the views reflect IDDRI's advocacy position.

