Objective
The Marine Regions Forum was established as a new participatory dialogue platform to strengthen regional ocean governance. Recognizing that the transboundary nature of the marine environment and the complexity of ocean issues require collaborative action across sectors and territorial boundaries, the Forum aims to bring together decision-makers, scientists, civil society representatives, and other actors from diverse marine regions for exchange outside of formal governance processes. Its broader goal is to enable collective responses, support the development of governance solutions that catalyse transformative change toward sustainable ocean use and conservation, and ultimately advance the implementation of the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development.

Methods
The project employed a transdisciplinary, co-creative approach developed jointly by the Institute for Advanced Sustainability Studies (IASS, Potsdam), the Institute for Sustainable Development and International Relations (IDDRI, Paris), and TMG – Think Tank for Sustainability (Berlin), on behalf of the German Environment Agency. Key methodological elements included:

An Advisory Board of over 25 experts from intergovernmental organizations, regional seas conventions, academia, civil society, and the private sector provided ongoing guidance.
A Steering Group comprising representatives from the European Commission, national ministries (Germany, France, Sweden), and UN bodies oversaw implementation.
The first major event was an international conference held in Berlin in 2019 under the theme "Achieving a healthy ocean – Regional ocean governance beyond 2020," attended by approximately 200 international ocean actors. The programme combined plenary sessions, multi-stakeholder dialogue sessions, an exhibition ("The Ocean Realm"), social events, and a public side event.
A follow-up online workshop was held on 28 January 2021, titled "Ocean Governance and COVID-19 – Building resilience for marine regions," featuring a keynote, regional reflections, and three parallel breakout groups addressing socio-economic impacts, recovery options, and consequences for regional cooperation.
Outcomes were disseminated through key messages, a conference report, IISD ENB+ daily reports, and video materials.


Key findings
The report is primarily a project and implementation report rather than a hypothesis-driven study. The principal findings and conclusions drawn from the first phase include:

Dialogue spaces such as the Marine Regions Forum have demonstrated potential to facilitate progress in ocean governance and sustainability transformations by complementing existing formal processes, enabling cross-sectoral multi-stakeholder exchange, and channeling emerging recommendations into formal policy channels.
Cooperation among stakeholders and coordination of policies and actions within and between marine regions are identified as critical levers for ocean sustainability, yet remain far from being widely implemented in practice.
The 2021 workshop highlighted that the COVID-19 pandemic affected all marine regions, disrupting ocean-dependent livelihoods (tourism, fisheries, shipping) and straining regional governance institutions, while also revealing opportunities for joint ecosystem restoration and cross-sectoral collaboration.
The co-creative, multi-stakeholder design of the Forum was considered a critical factor in its success, with the Advisory Board and Steering Group contributing "valuable and constructive guidance, suggestions and collaboration."


Limitations
The document is a final project report commissioned by the German Environment Agency, not a peer-reviewed research article, and does not contain a dedicated limitations section. Several constraints can be noted from the available text:

The scope is limited to the first phase of the Forum (the 2019 conference and the January 2021 online workshop); long-term impact on formal governance processes is not yet assessed.
The provided text consists largely of front matter, acknowledgements, the abstract, table of contents, and appendices; the full analytical body (Sections 1–3) is not included, so detailed findings, participant feedback data, and outcome tracking are not directly verifiable from the excerpt.
As a stakeholder forum, the outputs reflect the perspectives of participating actors (predominantly from Europe, the Pacific, and select African and Asian regions) and may not capture the views of all marine regions or smaller coastal communities.
The report does not present a formal evaluation framework or quantitative metrics to measure the Forum's effectiveness in changing governance outcomes.

