	author = {Cremers, Klaudija and Wright, Glen and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2020},
	keywords = {fisheries, BBNJ, RFMO, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	pages = {46},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2020 policy report by Cremers, Wright, and Rochette (Institute for Sustainable Development and International Relations, IDDRI), produced under the STRONG High Seas project, aims to explore how the ongoing BBNJ (Biodiversity Beyond National Jurisdiction) negotiations can strengthen Monitoring, Control and Surveillance (MCS) in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). The report seeks to (1) provide an overview of existing legal frameworks, technological tools, and MCS initiatives; (2) identify challenges to effective MCS in the deep and distant waters of ABNJ; and (3) offer suggested pathways and three concrete proposals for provisions that could be incorporated into the future international legally binding instrument (ILBI).

Methods
The report is a policy analysis rather than an empirical study. Its analytical approach draws on several sources of input:

Review of existing legal frameworks, including treaty law (UNCLOS, UNFSA, PSMA, Cape Town Agreement) and "soft law" instruments governing high seas fishing, shipping/navigation, and seabed minerals.
Survey of MCS technological tools (e.g., VMS, AIS, remote sensing, eDNA, drones) and existing MCS initiatives (RFMOs, the International MCS Network, fisheries improvement projects, civil society platforms such as Global Fishing Watch).
Findings from three dedicated MCS expert workshops (Paris, July 2018 – technological tools; Paris, April 2019 – policy options; Guayaquil, November 2019 – regional cooperation in the Southeast Pacific) and an MCS session at the Marine Regions Forum in Berlin (October/November 2019).
Analysis of governance challenges (flag State responsibility, fragmented institutional architecture, capacity gaps) and of four broad approaches to strengthening MCS: leveraging innovative technologies, co-creating MCS systems, strengthening policy responses, and using market mechanisms.


Key findings

Effective MCS in ABNJ remains a major challenge because of a fragmented governance framework and heavy reliance on flag States to exercise control over vessels, a system weakened by flags of convenience and limited enforcement capacity.
The BBNJ ILBI presents a significant opportunity to close MCS gaps by embedding explicit MCS obligations, transparency and reporting requirements, and a strengthened clearing-house mechanism into a single global instrument.
Three concrete proposals are advanced for the future instrument: (1) reinforcing MCS obligations and principles (cooperation, coordination, transparency, reporting); (2) developing a strong operational role for the clearing-house mechanism to facilitate information sharing and coordination; and (3) incorporating a dedicated MCS strategy within area-based management tool (ABMT) proposals so that protection zones are enforceable from the outset.
Innovative technologies (satellite tracking, remote sensing, eDNA metabarcoding, drones) have transformative potential but must be paired with institutional and policy reforms to be effective.
Market-based levers (trade measures, catch documentation schemes, fisheries improvement projects) can complement state-based enforcement, particularly where flag State capacity is weak.


Limitations

The document text provided for this summary consists primarily of front matter, the table of contents, an abbreviations list, acknowledgements, and the reference section. The full analytical body (Sections 1–8) is not included, so the summary above relies on the abstract, table of contents, and project description rather than the report's detailed argumentation.
As a policy report rather than a peer-reviewed empirical study, it does not report statistical findings, effect sizes, or a formal limitations section. Its "limitations" are therefore inferred: the analysis is necessarily forward-looking (the BBNJ ILBI had not yet been adopted in 2020), and the three proposals are normative recommendations whose feasibility depends on political negotiation outcomes.
The report's regional focus, as part of the STRONG High Seas project, is on the Southeast Pacific and Southeast Atlantic; generalizability to other ABNJ regions is not explicitly tested in the provided text.

