Objective
The Marine Regions Forum 2019, held in Berlin from 30 September to 2 October 2019, was convened to establish a cross-sectoral, cross-boundary multi-stakeholder platform for regional ocean governance. Its stated purpose was to fill a critical gap in the ocean policy landscape by bringing together diverse ocean actors to exchange experiences, discuss common problems, and develop new cross-sectoral solutions for achieving Sustainable Development Goal 14 (Life Below Water). The Forum aimed to catalyse the transformation of ocean governance through regional actions and to provide solution-oriented input into ongoing global processes, including the BBNJ negotiations, the Decade of Ocean Science for Sustainable Development (2021–2030), and the 2030 Agenda.

Methods
The Forum was structured as a participatory, knowledge-based platform at the science–policy–society interface. Over 200 experts from 50 countries participated, representing academia, research, policy, NGOs, industry, the arts, and media. The programme comprised:

Three daily plenary sessions (opening, mid-conference, and closing) with keynote speakers and panellists from multiple sectors and marine regions.
Eighteen parallel dialogue sessions organised under three themes: (1) Achieving SDG 14, (2) Underpinning global processes, and (3) Knowledge for ocean action.
Side activities including exhibitions, a VR ocean experience, a photo exhibition, a marine spatial planning board game, and a "Meet \& Greet" networking space.
A public event, "Berlin am Meer / Berlin by the Sea," featuring artistic intervention, dialogue, and film.

The Forum was organised by the Institute for Advanced Sustainability Studies (IASS), the Institute for Sustainable Development and International Relations (IDDRI), and TMG – Think Tank for Sustainability, under the Partnership for Regional Ocean Governance (PROG), with hosting by Germany's Federal Ministry for the Environment and the European Commission.

Key findings
The report foregrounds several overarching messages drawn from the plenary and dialogue sessions:

The ocean is in crisis: biodiversity is declining due to overfishing, pollution, and climate-driven warming, acidification, and deoxygenation, threatening ecosystem services on which billions of livelihoods depend.
Regional actors and institutions must provide platforms for cross-sectoral cooperation that link local stakeholders, national governments, and international institutions.
Scientists and policymakers need to be brought together to identify effective responses to pressing challenges, with transparent processes for accelerated implementation.
SDG 14 cannot be achieved by individual countries acting alone; governance structures must be as interconnected as the ocean itself.
Effectively managed networks of marine protected areas, including on the high seas under the BBNJ instrument, represent a historic opportunity.
The year 2020 is identified as a critical stock-taking moment to assess whether current actions are sufficiently ambitious.
The Forum positions itself as a mechanism to dismantle structural barriers in ocean governance and to foster integrated, cross-sectoral approaches beyond formal negotiation silos.


Limitations

The report explicitly states that statements, conclusions, and recommendations reflect the views of individual participants and do not necessarily represent the opinions of the organisers or a consensus among all workshop participants.
As a conference report rather than a peer-reviewed research article, it lacks a formal methodology, hypothesis-testing framework, or systematic evidence base.
The provided document text includes primarily the foreword, welcome, agenda, and organisational information; the detailed session summaries (pages 18–95) covering the specific findings of the eighteen dialogue sessions are not fully reproduced here, limiting the depth of findings that can be summarised.
The Forum's recommendations are aspirational and solution-oriented rather than empirically validated; no quantitative impact assessment or follow-up evaluation is included.

