Objective
This 2019 policy brief by Wright, Rochette, and colleagues (produced under the STRONG High Seas project) aims to review ongoing regional initiatives for the governance of areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ) and extract lessons that can inform the design of a new, legally binding international treaty on high seas biodiversity (the BBNJ agreement). Specifically, the report seeks to demonstrate how regional and sectoral cooperation can underpin a strong global BBNJ agreement, facilitate cross-sectoral coordination, and support the implementation of area-based management tools such as marine protected areas (MPAs).

Methods
The report is a transdisciplinary policy review rather than an empirical study. It draws on the STRONG High Seas project's scientific assessments and stakeholder engagement to synthesise existing regional governance arrangements. The authors examine a range of case studies and institutional mechanisms, including:

The "Collective Arrangement for the North-East Atlantic," a cooperation mechanism between OSPAR and NEAFC.
The Sargasso Sea Commission, a coalition-based initiative for high seas protection.
The Pelagos Sanctuary for Mediterranean Marine Mammals, a multilateral state cooperation example.
Regional fisheries closures in the Northwest Atlantic and Southern Indian Ocean.
The Antarctic Treaty System, under which the world's largest MPA (Ross Sea) was designated.
The Ecologically or Biologically Significant Areas (EBSAs) process for identifying priority sites.

The authors also reference the broader institutional landscape (UNCLOS, UNFSA, CBD, IMO, ISA) and ongoing BBNJ Working Group negotiations to situate regional action within the global framework.

Key findings

ABNJ covers nearly half the Earth's surface and hosts a significant share of global biodiversity, yet the existing governance regime is fragmented and ineffective.
Regional cooperation provides an appropriate scale for ecosystem-based management and facilitates political consensus among states sharing common interests.
Effective cross-sectoral coordination between organisations with management mandates (e.g., fisheries bodies and environmental conventions) is essential but remains underdeveloped.
Champions and leaders with sustained political will are critical to driving regional processes and garnering support for improved management.
A dynamic science-policy interface is needed to deliver timely, policy-relevant scientific information to decision-makers.
The Antarctic Treaty System demonstrates that a representative MPA system in ABNJ is achievable through multilateral cooperation.
The forthcoming BBNJ treaty represents an opportunity to bring coherence to the fragmented regime by providing additional support for cross-sectoral cooperation and fresh impetus for regional integration mechanisms.


Limitations
The provided text does not contain a dedicated limitations section. The document is a policy brief rather than a peer-reviewed empirical study, and its scope is limited to a selection of regional examples (predominantly in the North-East Atlantic, Mediterranean, Southern Ocean, and Sargasso Sea). The full analytical depth of sections 3–6 is not fully reproduced in the supplied text, and the report explicitly notes that it does not necessarily reflect the views of its funding agencies (the German Federal Ministry for the Environment via the International Climate Initiative). No quantitative assessment of governance effectiveness is presented in the available text.
