Objective
This 2022 briefing note by Glen Wright (Iddri) advises negotiators of the future BBNJ (Biodiversity Beyond National Jurisdiction) treaty on how to ensure effective implementation of the agreement. The central concern is the establishment of a dedicated implementation and compliance committee, which the author argues has received insufficient attention relative to the broader "Package deal" under negotiation.

Methods
The document is a policy briefing rather than an empirical study. Its analytical approach consists of:

Reviewing the current state of BBNJ negotiations, noting that the draft compliance article remains "in brackets" and lacks structural detail.
Surveying lessons from other international environmental agreements regarding the timing and mechanism of compliance-body creation.
Presenting three structured options for institutionalizing a compliance committee, each accompanied by proposed treaty language (in both French and English).

The work is produced within the STRONG High Seas project (2017–2022), a five-year initiative coordinated by IASS Potsdam with Iddri and other partners to strengthen regional ocean governance for high-seas biodiversity.

Key findings
The briefing identifies three viable pathways for negotiators:

Option 1 – COP-mandated creation: Amend Article 48(4) to require the Conference of the Parties to establish an implementation and compliance committee as a subsidiary body. The author warns that deferring creation risks indefinite delay or blocking by parties opposed to compliance mechanisms.
Option 2 – Direct treaty provision: Insert a new Article 53(4) that establishes the committee directly in the treaty text (analogous to Article 15 of the Minamata Convention on Mercury). The committee would be facilitative in nature, attentive to national capabilities, and empowered to examine both individual and systemic compliance issues and make recommendations to the COP.
Option 3 – Initial COP decisions: Prioritize adoption of detailed decisions at the first COP to define the committee's foundational elements, including: a non-confrontational, transparent, and consultative approach; composition (Party representatives, experts, civil-society participation); an independent mandate to address non-compliance; triggering mechanisms (self-reporting, COP/Secretariat/Committee initiation, observer input); and consequences such as a warning to a non-compliant Party.

The author emphasizes that a strong compliance mechanism is essential for the treaty's overall effectiveness and that early institutionalization sends a clear priority signal.

Limitations

The document is a single-author policy briefing, not a peer-reviewed research article; it presents normative recommendations rather than empirical findings.
It is produced by an advocacy-oriented think tank (Iddri) within a funded project (STRONG High Seas), which may reflect institutional positions on treaty design.
No systematic comparative analysis of existing compliance mechanisms across treaties is provided; references to other agreements (e.g., Minamata) are illustrative rather than analytical.
The proposed treaty language is presented as options without a cost-benefit or feasibility assessment of each pathway.
The briefing does not address potential political resistance in detail beyond a general warning about blocking by dissenting parties.

