@techreport{bouvetEnsuringEffectiveImplementation2022a,
	title = {Ensuring {Effective} {Implementation} of a {High} {Seas} {Biodiversity} {Treaty}: {Lessons} {Learned} and {Options} for an {Implementation} and {Compliance} {Committee}},
	institution = {STRONG High Seas},
	author = {Bouvet, Morgane and Wright, Glen and Kechelriess, Daniel and Cremers, Klaudija and Rochette, Julien},
	year = {2022},
	keywords = {BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	pages = {22},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2022 policy report by Bouvet, Wright, Kachelriess, Cremers, and Rochette (STRONG High Seas Project) examines how to ensure effective implementation of the forthcoming BBNJ Agreement (an international legally binding instrument on marine biodiversity beyond national jurisdiction). The authors draw on lessons from existing multilateral environmental agreements (MEAs) to propose concrete options for establishing an Implementation and Compliance Committee within the treaty framework.

Methods
The study is a qualitative policy analysis combining several approaches:

Extensive review of the academic and grey literature on compliance mechanisms in international environmental law.
Close examination of the implementation and compliance provisions in seven MEAs: CITES, the Alpine Convention, the Aarhus Convention, the Minamata Convention on Mercury, the Paris Agreement, the Kyoto Protocol, and the Montreal Protocol.
Targeted interviews with experts who have first-hand experience negotiating or operating these compliance mechanisms (e.g., representatives from the CITES, Alpine, and Aarhus Convention processes).
Analysis of the current draft text of the BBNJ Agreement (as of the third IGC, early 2022) to identify gaps in compliance provisions.
Development of three alternative treaty-text options for establishing a Committee, with proposed wording provided in an annex.


Key findings

MEAs that embed a dedicated compliance committee directly in the treaty text (e.g., Minamata, Paris) avoid the politicised delays and indefinite postponements that plague instruments where the committee must be created by a later CoP decision.
Effective committees adopt a facilitative, transparent, and non-confrontational approach, encouraging Parties to self-identify implementation difficulties and seek assistance rather than facing adversarial proceedings.
Consensus-only decision-making can allow a single Party to stall progress; the authors recommend permitting majority voting once consensus efforts are exhausted.
Civil society should be invited to submit information on suspected non-compliance, and ideally to hold observer or representative seats on the Committee.
Coercive measures (e.g., cautions, trade-related consequences) can serve as a strong incentive when used sparingly as a last resort, balanced against assistance and capacity-building.
Three options are proposed for the BBNJ treaty: (1) require the CoP to establish a Committee; (2) establish the Committee directly in the treaty text with high-level principles; (3) prioritise a detailed first-CoP decision on the Committee's modalities, mandate, triggers, composition, and consequences of non-compliance.


Limitations

The BBNJ Agreement was still under negotiation at the time of writing (fourth IGC scheduled for March 2022), so all recommendations are prospective and contingent on the final treaty text.
The comparative analysis covers only seven MEAs, which may not capture the full range of compliance designs (e.g., no examination of the Ramsar Convention, CBD, or sectoral fisheries agreements).
The report is a project policy brief rather than a peer-reviewed journal article; it does not present original empirical data on the measurable effectiveness of different compliance mechanisms.
The provided document text is partially truncated (several sections in the MEA case-study chapter are abbreviated), limiting the depth of detail available for each agreement's specific compliance architecture.
The authors' institutional affiliations (IDDRI, Sea Shepherd Legal) and the project's regional focus (Southeast Atlantic and Southeast Pacific) may shape the framing of recommendations, though the analysis itself draws broadly on global practice.

