@techreport{stronghighseasHighlevelExpertMeeting2019,
	address = {New York, USA},
	title = {High-level expert meeting: {Towards} an {Effective} {High} {Seas} {Treaty}: {Building} on and {Strengthening} {Regional} {Ocean} {Governance}},
	institution = {STRONG High Seas},
	author = {{STRONG High Seas}},
	month = mar,
	year = {2019},
	keywords = {BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	annote = {[role]
organiser
facilitator
speaker
[type]
workshop
},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This document summarizes a high-level expert meeting held on 30 March 2019 in New York, hosted by Germany and the STRONG High Seas project alongside the second Intergovernmental Conference (IGC) working towards a legally binding high seas treaty. The meeting's purpose was to facilitate informal discussion among approximately 30 experts on the legal and institutional framework of a future agreement for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ), with particular focus on options for establishing marine protected areas (MPAs) and promoting coherence of area-based management tools (ABMTs) under a new treaty. It built on a prior expert meeting held during the first round of negotiations in September 2018, which gathered 65 participants to discuss the role of regional and sectoral ocean governance in underpinning a strong high seas agreement.

Methods
The meeting was structured as a single-day informal expert discussion held under the Chatham House rule, meaning participants could speak freely but statements could not be attributed. The format included:

A welcome and introductory presentation by Guido Genrich (Head of the German Delegation) and Dr. Carole Durussel (co-lead of the STRONG High Seas Project), followed by a "setting the scene" presentation by Glen Wright (IDDRI/STRONG High Seas).
Session 1: Designation and establishment of protected areas and management tools, examining which regional and sectoral bodies currently designate MPAs, cross-sectoral cooperation challenges, and how MPAs/ABMTs would be proposed under a new agreement.
Session 2: Implementation of protected areas and management tools, addressing how existing ABMTs would be treated, coordination mechanisms, and State obligations for effective implementation.
Session 3: Compliance and oversight, exploring technological and policy tools for monitoring, control, and surveillance (MCS) in ABNJ, existing MCS mandates, and the role of regional organizations in compliance.
A closing discussion on key recommendations.

Participants included representatives from governments (Germany, UK, France, Chile, Barbados), international organizations (European Commission, IUCN, WWF, UN Environment, IIED), academic institutions (Duke University, University of Strathclyde, IASS, IDDRI), and civil society (Pew Charitable Trusts, High Seas Alliance, Greenpeace, Sargasso Sea Commission, CPPS).

Key findings
The document is a meeting summary and agenda record rather than a research publication with formal findings. It does not report specific conclusions, data, or analytical results. The text confirms that delegations and observers to the IGC were considering the role of existing organizations and instruments but had not yet converged on consensus regarding modalities for building on existing governance mechanisms within a future treaty framework. The document records the questions posed for discussion across the three sessions but does not document the outcomes or specific recommendations that emerged from the Chatham House rule discussions.

Limitations

The document is a brief meeting summary (7 pages) rather than a peer-reviewed scholarly article; it contains no formal findings, data analysis, or cited literature.
Discussions were held under the Chatham House rule, so specific statements, positions, and recommendations cannot be attributed or verified from this text.
No formal outcomes, resolutions, or key recommendations from the closing discussion are documented in the provided text.
The document does not include a bibliography, methodology section, or analytical framework beyond the agenda questions.
It reflects the perspectives and framing of the STRONG High Seas project and its German host, which may introduce a particular institutional bias toward strengthening regional governance mechanisms.

