@techreport{wrightSummaryReportInterregional2022,
	title = {Summary report: {Inter}-regional {Dialogues} on {High} {Seas} {Governance}},
	abstract = {Member States at the United Nations (UN) are currently negotiating a new treaty for the conservation and sustainable use of high seas biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). While in-person negotiations at the intergovernmental conference (IGC) were interrupted by the global pandemic, intersessional discussions continued online and States continued to exchange views, reflect on their positions, and consider how an agreement might ultimately be concluded. 

Seeking to facilitate these exchanges, the STRONG High Seas project and regional partners convened four inter-regional expert meetings, connecting colleagues across continents to share experiences and discuss high seas governance issues.},
	institution = {STRONG High Seas},
	author = {Wright, Glen},
	year = {2022},
	keywords = {BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	annote = {[role]
author
[type]
report
},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This report documents four inter-regional expert meetings convened by the STRONG High Seas project and regional partners to facilitate dialogue on high seas governance. The meetings were designed to connect stakeholders across continents, share experiences, and inform ongoing United Nations negotiations for a new treaty on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ/ABNJ).

Methods
Two sets of workshops were organised: one in collaboration with the Permanent Commission for the South Pacific (CPPS) and the Office of the Pacific Ocean Commissioner (OPOC) connecting Pacific stakeholders, and another with the Abidjan and Nairobi Conventions connecting stakeholders from both sides of the African continent. Over 70 experts and stakeholders participated, including representatives from national governments, UN delegations, Regional Seas programmes, RFMOs, LME programmes, intergovernmental organisations, funding bodies, scientific organisations, and NGOs. Each meeting followed a structure of informal introductions in breakout groups, an expert presentation on the state of play and key issues, and extended open discussion. The meetings were held under the Chatham House rule, so no specific speakers or organisations are quoted or cited in the report.

Key findings

The BBNJ negotiations represent a unique opportunity to strengthen the global ocean governance framework, yet cross-regional cooperation remains limited despite a strong appetite for collaboration.
Regional organisations occupy a unique position to facilitate coordinated solutions, and their diversity is a strength. However, it remains unclear how their roles will integrate with an eventual BBNJ framework.
Regional specificities were identified: the Southeast Pacific faces EEZ management challenges and IUU fishing; the Southwest Pacific emphasises cultural recognition and SIDS circumstances; the Southeast Atlantic has limited binding management measures; the West Indian Ocean relies heavily on biodiversity for livelihoods.
The governance system for the ocean is fragmented, with diverse organisations and asymmetries in capacity, mandate, and ambition. The BBNJ treaty must provide a framework to coordinate these heterogeneous arrangements.
Area-based management tools (ABMTs) remain unresolved in negotiations, with a growing tendency toward pluralistic approaches that complement existing bodies while empowering the future Conference of Parties.
Financing flows too slowly and is often project-based with burdensome reporting. Long-term structures for cross-regional dialogue are needed, as current platforms are donor-funded and time-limited.
Tools such as Strategic Environmental Assessments, EBSA identification under the CBD, and the Antarctic Treaty System offer lessons for regional cooperation and coordinated governance.
The CBD applies to State activities regardless of location, countering a common misconception that it does not extend beyond national jurisdiction.


Limitations

The Chatham House rule prevents attribution of specific views to named speakers or organisations, limiting the report's evidentiary value.
The dialogues were facilitated through ad hoc, donor-funded, time-limited projects, raising concerns about the sustainability and longevity of the cooperation structures discussed.
Negotiations for the BBNJ treaty were still ongoing at the time of writing; arrangements for ABMTs, the mandate of the future CoP, and the role of regional organisations had not been finalised.
Many regional organisations have limited capacity to fulfil existing mandates, let alone drive BBNJ discussions forward, and often focus primarily on coastal rather than high seas issues.
Mutual understanding and scientific knowledge of how activities under one governance regime affect those under another remain limited across regions.

