@inproceedings{TechnologicalToolsMonitoring2018,
	address = {Sciences Po, Paris, France},
	title = {Technological tools for {Monitoring}, {Control} and {Surveillance} in {Marine} {Areas} {Beyond} {National} {Jurisdiction}},
	abstract = {Monitoring Control and Surveillance (MCS) will be crucial to ensuring compliance with management measures developed under a future international agreement on Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ). Tools for MCS have been drastically extended by a range of new technologies and techniques using previously unavailable or inaccessible data sources, while there is no clear and singular MCS solution. A key challenge facing MCS is an excess, not a lack, of data available through the different technologies which is further compounded by potential skills shortages for data interpretation. There may be resistance to MCS tools by e.g. the fisheries sector concerned about control over the industry, while some fishers may also view MCS as way to prove that their fish was sustainably caught and provide them with a market advantage. The key challenge currently facing MCS tools is a lack of appropriate policy approaches and governance mechanisms for the design and implementation of the available technologies. Any future successful options will need to be co-created with stakeholder to ensure appropriate design and early buy-in amongst different sectors.},
	publisher = {STRONG High Seas},
	month = jul,
	year = {2018},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
The workshop, held on 10 July 2018 at Sciences Po, Paris, aimed to take stock of existing technological tools for Monitoring, Control and Surveillance (MCS) in Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ), consider possible future technological developments, and identify opportunities and challenges for effective MCS. The outputs were intended to feed into ongoing international negotiations on a future ABNJ agreement and to support regional decision-makers in the Southeast Pacific and Southeast Atlantic regions. The workshop was part of the five-year STRONG High Seas project (Strengthening Regional Ocean Governance for the High Seas).

Methods
The event was a structured workshop bringing together 15 participants representing policy research institutes, the fisheries industry, MCS technology providers, environmental organizations, Regional Fisheries Management Organizations (RFMOs), and international NGOs. The programme comprised four sessions:

Satellite technologies ("Eyes in the sky")
Onboard monitoring technologies ("Boats on the water")
Compliance and control mechanisms
Discussion of opportunities and challenges for MCS in ABNJ

Presenters included representatives from OceanMind, Argos Froyanes, the Tuna Compliance Network, and the FAO, with discussants from Pew Charitable Trusts, RSPB, and NEAFC. A second workshop planned for late 2018 was to build on these results to discuss policy options.

Key findings

MCS will be crucial for ensuring compliance with management measures under a future ABNJ agreement, and MCS technologies can also contribute ecological data for protected-area design and impact assessments.
The technological landscape has expanded dramatically (SAR, VIIRS, AIS, VMS, S-AIS), but no single technology is a panacea; costs, access, reliability, coverage, ease of manipulation, and privacy must all be weighed.
The central data challenge is an excess of data rather than a shortage, compounded by skills shortages in data interpretation among relevant entities.
Stakeholder interests are diverse: some fisheries operators resist MCS as industry control, while others see it as a means to demonstrate sustainable catch and gain market advantage. S-AIS data is attractive for its free accessibility, yet vessels may switch AIS off for competitive or security reasons.
The principal barrier to effective MCS is not technology but the fragmented governance framework: limited coordination among flag states, RFMOs, and port authorities, compounded by flags of convenience and IUU fishing.
Participants agreed that strong, unified MCS requirements would make existing technical tools sufficient, and that the ABNJ negotiations themselves could create a framework for more efficient MCS.
Successful MCS options must be co-created with stakeholders to ensure appropriate design and early buy-in across sectors.


Limitations

The document is a workshop summary rather than a peer-reviewed research article; it records discussions and key messages rather than presenting original empirical data or a formal analytical framework.
Only 15 participants took part, limiting the breadth of perspectives captured, and the document does not detail the extent of disagreement or unresolved tensions among them.
No specific MCS solution or policy recommendation is proposed; the workshop explicitly notes the absence of a "clear and singular MCS solution."
The document does not address quantitative cost-benefit analyses, technical performance benchmarks, or comparative evaluations of the technologies discussed.
The governance and policy gaps identified (fragmented regimes, skills shortages, stakeholder resistance) are acknowledged but not resolved within the scope of this summary.

