Objective
The workshop, held on 16 April 2019 at Sciences Po in Paris, aimed to explore options for strengthening Monitoring, Control and Surveillance (MCS) through a new international instrument on high seas biodiversity (areas beyond national jurisdiction, or ABNJ) then under negotiation at the United Nations. It was convened as part of the STRONG High Seas project (Strengthening Regional Ocean Governance for the High Seas), a five-year initiative working with science and policy actors in the Southeast Pacific and Southeast Atlantic regions to improve regional coordination and inform high seas governance.
