	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
The workshop, held on 3 September 2018 in New York as part of the STRONG High Seas project, aimed to build capacity among ministry representatives and UN negotiators from the Southeast Pacific and Southeast Atlantic regions ahead of the first formal round of BBNJ negotiations. Specific objectives included: providing background on the BBNJ negotiation process; deepening understanding of opportunities and challenges for the two target regions; highlighting the role of regional ocean governance in implementing a future BBNJ agreement under UNCLOS; and creating a networking and mutual-learning space among participants.

Methods
The workshop was one of three components of the BBNJ Training for UN Negotiations (the others being daily debriefing meetings and a High-Level Expert Meeting on 8 September 2018). The day's format comprised:

Three expert presentations: Glen Wright (IDDRI) on the state of play of the negotiations; Charlotte Salpin (DOALOS) on the legal framework for ABNJ and the PrepComm process; and Kristina Gjerde (IUCN) on the relevance of regional and sectoral ocean governance.
An open plenary discussion on opportunities and challenges for participants' countries and regions.
Three parallel working groups, each moderated by two experts, focused on the BBNJ "package deal" elements: (I) Marine Genetic Resources including capacity building/technology transfer; (II) Area-based Management Tools including MPAs; (III) Environmental Impact Assessments. Each group prepared a summary presentation for plenary.
A closing plenary discussion synthesizing working-group outcomes and remaining questions.

Twenty-one ministry representatives, UN negotiators, and representatives from scientific institutions and NGOs participated.

Key findings

Formal BBNJ negotiations commenced in September 2018 following a long preparatory process (Working Group from 2004, package deal agreement in 2011, PrepComm established 2016, IGC convened by UNGA Resolution 72/249).
State positions are sharply divided: the EU, G77/China, Mexico, and ACP States strongly advocate a new agreement; Australia, New Zealand, Canada, and Norway seek compromises; the US, Japan, Iceland, South Korea, and Russia are reluctant.
The current ABNJ legal and governance framework is fragmented across regional and sectoral conventions, creating coherence challenges.
Regional and sectoral ocean governance is essential for implementing a strong international instrument, but is hindered by differing geographical coverage, limited capacity, divergent priorities, and poor understanding of ecological connectivity.
On MGRs: definition and scope remain unclear; benefit-sharing modalities are complex; strengthening UNCLOS technology-transfer provisions could support benefit-sharing.
On ABMTs/MPAs: global standards for sectoral and cross-sectoral tools are lacking; the BBNJ process offers a unique opportunity to establish shared governance principles, a global MPA network, and a compliance mechanism.
On EIAs: no existing mechanism covers high-seas activities; unresolved questions include which activities require an EIA, how to account for transboundary and cumulative impacts, funding responsibility, and the legal effect of an EIA.


Limitations

The document is a one-day workshop summary, not a peer-reviewed research article; it records discussions rather than presenting original empirical findings.
Participation was limited to 21 individuals from two specific regions (Southeast Pacific and Southeast Atlantic), limiting generalizability.
No formal conclusions, policy recommendations, or follow-up action items are documented beyond the working-group summaries presented in plenary.
The workshop preceded the first round of negotiations, so no assessment of negotiation outcomes is included.

