Objective
The 2015 FAO workshop, organized by the Global Ocean Forum and the Food and Agriculture Organization as part of the GEF/FAO Common Oceans Program, aimed to strengthen the linkage between global and regional governance of marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), which comprise 64\% of the ocean surface. Specific objectives included:

Assessing knowledge, ongoing trends, and capacity relevant to sustainable fisheries management and biodiversity conservation in ABNJ at national, regional, and global levels.
Fostering cross-sectoral linkages for improved information-sharing on ABNJ across sectors and between global and regional levels.
Sharing lessons learned, best practices, and emerging trends in ABNJ research, development, and management from various world regions.
Providing a synthesis of the current state of knowledge on global and regional policy processes to address knowledge gaps and improve sustainable use of fishery resources and biodiversity conservation in ABNJ.


Methods
The workshop was held 17–20 February 2015 at FAO headquarters in Rome, Italy. It brought together representatives from government decision-makers, industry, UN delegates, intergovernmental organizations, NGOs, policy experts, legal scholars, and academics. The structure comprised:

Plenary sessions covering the importance of ABNJ, major uses and threats (fisheries, deep-sea mining, shipping, submarine cables, climate change), and the imperative of capacity development.
Three regional sessions addressing the South Atlantic and Indian Ocean, the Pacific, and the North Atlantic and Mediterranean, each featuring presentations from regional fisheries management organizations and convention secretariats.
Regional break-out discussions to identify current knowledge, emerging best practices, and gaps in capacity, management, and scientific knowledge.
Pre-workshop activities including Community of Practice meetings on fisheries/biodiversity/climate change and multi-sector area-based planning, and a meeting of the ABNJ Public Outreach Network.


Key findings
As a workshop planning and agenda document, this text does not report original research findings. However, it establishes the following contextual points drawn from the background and session descriptions:

ABNJ ecosystems include the water column and seabed of the high seas and are of great ecological, socioeconomic, and cultural importance, yet their distance from coastal areas makes sustainable management challenging.
Important regional initiatives were already underway in the Northeast Atlantic, Sargasso Sea, Indian Ocean, and Pacific Islands to adapt institutional processes toward ecosystem-based management, multiple-use area-based management, and environmental impact assessment.
Widespread agreement existed on the need to improve conservation and sustainable use in ABNJ at both global and regional levels, with ABNJ issues rising to the top of the global ocean agenda.
Expected outputs from the break-out groups were to form a state-of-the-art synthesis of science and policy/management in fisheries, biodiversity conservation, and other major ABNJ uses for each region.


Limitations
The document is a workshop agenda and background brief rather than a peer-reviewed research publication, so it contains no empirical results, statistical analyses, or formal conclusions. The text does not include a dedicated limitations section. Notable constraints evident from the document include:

Several listed speakers were marked as "not confirmed," indicating the agenda was a working draft.
The document does not report the actual outcomes, resolutions, or policy recommendations produced during the workshop; it only outlines the intended structure and expected outputs.
No post-workshop synthesis, participant feedback, or follow-up actions are included in this text.

