@inproceedings{wrightEnvironmentalImpactAssessment2017,
	address = {Brussels, Belgium},
	title = {Environmental impact assessment in {Areas} {Beyond} {National} {Jurisdiction}: challenges and opportunities for {Small}-{Island} {Developing} {States}},
	url = {https://sidsworkshop.iode.org/docs/Bookofabstracts.pdf},
	urldate = {2025-08-17},
	booktitle = {Healthy {Oceans}, {Resilient} {Islands}: {SIDS} {Capacity} {Development} {Aspects} in the {BBNJ} {Process}},
	publisher = {Government of Belgium},
	author = {Wright, Glen},
	month = mar,
	year = {2017},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2017 presentation by Glen Wright (IDDRI, Paris), delivered at a Brussels workshop on SIDS capacity development in the BBNJ process, examines the current state of Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) in Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ). Its central aim is to identify the specific challenges SIDS face in meeting EIA obligations in the high seas and to outline opportunities that a forthcoming BBNJ agreement could create for these states.

Methods
The work is a policy review and analytical presentation rather than an empirical study. Wright draws on:

Primary legal instruments: CBD Article 14 (1992), UNCLOS Articles 192 and 206, the 1994 Part XI Agreement, the London Convention/Protocol, the Fish Stocks Agreement, the Madrid Protocol, and the Espoo Convention.
Secondary literature on EIA trends, outcomes, and effectiveness (Morgan 2012; Cashmore 2004; Jay et al. 2007; Blasiak et al. 2016; IUCN 2015; UNEP/CBD 2009; Warner 2015).
A structured review of the EIA process (screening, scoping, assessment, notification, reporting, decision-making) and a discussion of good-practice criteria.

The analysis then maps these findings onto the BBNJ negotiation context to assess what a new global agreement could add.

Key findings

Current EIA landscape: EIA use is widespread and growing, but quality is often poor and the process is threatened by economic imperatives. Marine-specific EIA and Strategic Environmental Assessment (SEA) are emerging but remain underdeveloped.
Fragmented legal framework in ABNJ: Obligations under UNCLOS Article 206 require assessment only where there are "reasonable grounds" of substantial harm. Sectoral and regional provisions (Part XI Agreement, London Protocol, Fish Stocks Agreement, Madrid Protocol, Espoo Convention) create a patchwork with no unified, cumulative, or transboundary assessment mechanism.
Potential of a new BBNJ agreement: A coherent global instrument could reaffirm EIA obligations, set a clear and uniform process, extend coverage to activities outside existing sectoral regimes, and introduce cumulative assessment, transboundary EIA, and SEA.
Challenges for EIA in ABNJ: Marine environments are highly variable and poorly understood; research is costlier and more difficult at sea; data are sparse; stakeholders are dispersed; and governance provisions are unclear and fragmented.
Challenges for SIDS: Few SIDS have the institutional or technical capacity to carry out or oversee EIA in ABNJ. There is a risk of a "race to the bottom" or "EIA of convenience" if obligations are imposed without adequate support.
Opportunities for SIDS: A strong BBNJ agreement could maximise SIDS participation through consultation, transparency, and information-sharing; use EIA as a lever for capacity-building and technology transfer (CB\&TT); create new financing mechanisms for EIA; enable joint EIAs and "twinning" arrangements; and position SIDS as a strong collective voice for environmental protection in new global bodies.


Limitations

The document is a conference presentation (Brussels, 8–9 March 2017), so it is necessarily concise and does not include the depth of argument, data, or peer review expected of a full journal article.
No empirical case studies or quantitative evidence are presented; the analysis is normative and legal-policy in nature.
The BBNJ negotiation process has evolved substantially since 2017 (the agreement was adopted in 2023), so the "potential contribution" discussed here may no longer reflect the final text.
The document does not explicitly discuss its own methodological limitations or the scope of its literature review.

