@misc{wrightNewInternationalAgreement2014,
	address = {Sydney, Australia},
	title = {Towards a {New} {International} {Agreement} on {High} {Seas} {Biodiversity}},
	author = {Wright, Glen},
	month = nov,
	year = {2014},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This presentation by Glen Wright (Institute for Sustainable Development and International Relations, IDDRI, Paris; University of New South Wales), delivered on 19 November 2014, outlines the state of progress and remaining challenges toward a new international agreement on high seas biodiversity. It situates the Biodiversity Beyond National Jurisdiction (BBNJ) Working Group process within the broader UN framework and assesses where negotiations stood at that point in time.

Methods
The document is a policy briefing/presentation rather than an empirical research study. It proceeds by:

Reviewing the timeline of BBNJ-related events from 2006 (formation of the Working Group) through 2014–2015 (anticipated UNGA decision).
Summarising the four-pillar "Package Deal" agreed in 2011.
Describing the June 2014 BBNJ meeting as a turning point, noting the emergence of a "coalition of the willing" including Pacific nations, CARICOM, the African Union, and civil society.
Identifying limited points of agreement among states and the remaining "balle lines" (areas of deep disagreement).
Noting existing regional organisations (OSPAR, NEAFC, ATS, CCAMLR, Sargasso Sea Commission) as interim governance mechanisms.


Key findings

Significant gaps exist in the legal framework governing the high seas, distinct from implementation gaps, and are compounded by rapid intensification of activities and cumulative impacts.
The 2011 Package Deal establishes four negotiating pillars: (1) marine genetic resources and benefit-sharing, (2) area-based management tools including marine protected areas, (3) environmental impact assessments, and (4) capacity-building and transfer of marine technology.
Points of agreement by mid-2014 included maintaining the Rio+20 deadline, affirming the primacy of UNCLOS, retaining the Package Deal structure, suspending the "common heritage" versus freedom-of-the-seas debate, and respecting the mandates of existing organisations.
Unresolved "balle lines" spanned the scope of the agreement (legal gaps vs. broader vision), the practical meaning of respecting existing mandates (especially fisheries), the international-versus-regional balance, and the institutional structure of any new body.
The anticipated next steps were a Convergence Report by BBNJ chairs, further state submissions, a January Working Group session, and a recommendation to the UNGA (August/September 2015) to open formal negotiations.


Limitations

The document is a short presentation (approximately 10 slides) rather than a full-length scholarly article; it provides no empirical data, formal analysis, or literature review.
It is a point-in-time snapshot (November 2014) and does not assess outcomes of the subsequent January 2015 Working Group or the 2015 UNGA decision.
The slide titled "The holdouts" contains no visible text in the provided document, so the specific states or positions opposing the process are not identified.
No formal methodology, citations, or references are included, limiting the ability to trace the evidentiary basis for the claims made.
The document does not discuss the content or legal text of any draft agreement, only the political and procedural trajectory.

