@inproceedings{wrightBottomFisheriesClosures2015,
	title = {Bottom {Fisheries} {Closures} in {Areas} {Beyond} {National} {Jurisdiction}},
	booktitle = {Deep {Ocean} {Stewardship} {Initiative} ({DOSI}) webinar series: {Deep}-­{Sea} {Promises} and {Challenges}},
	author = {Wright, Glen},
	month = oct,
	year = {2015},
	keywords = {webinar},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This review paper, published in Marine Policy (2015), assesses the current status of bottom fisheries closures in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). It evaluates whether Regional Fisheries Management Organisations and Arrangements (RFMO/As) are adequately implementing closures to protect vulnerable marine ecosystems (VMEs) in line with UNGA Resolution 61/105 (2006) and the FAO International Guidelines (2008), and identifies where best practice is not being followed.

Methods
The paper is a descriptive review rather than an empirical study. The authors (Wright, Ardron, Gjerde, Currie, and Rochette) systematically examined:

The legal and policy framework, including UNGA Resolution 61/105 and the 2008 FAO Guidelines defining VMEs and significant adverse impacts (SAIs).
The number, scope, and permanence of closures adopted by each RFMO across eight regions: North-East Atlantic (NEAFC), North-West Atlantic (NAFO), South-East Atlantic (SEAFO), Southern Ocean (CCAMLR), Indian Ocean (SIOFA), North Pacific (NPOC), South Pacific (SPRFMO), and the Mediterranean (GFCM).
The "state of play" in each region, including ongoing debates, scientific committee advice, and recent decisions (e.g., NAFO's September 2015 seamount ban).
The FAO VME database and map, noted as a new tool for tracking closures.


Key findings

RFMO/A biodiversity conservation efforts are advancing only slowly. Existing legal powers are not being fully utilised, and best practice is not consistently followed.
Closures are often temporary (e.g., NEAFC closures expiring 2017; NAFO closures expiring 2020), representative of VMEs rather than covering their full extent, or have not effectively restricted ongoing fishing activity.
Scientific evidence in several regions (notably NAFO and SEAFO) indicates that VMEs are far more extensive than the small areas actually closed, leaving substantial seamount and ridge systems open to bottom fishing.
Several regions have no formal closures at all: the Indian Ocean (SIOFA), North Pacific (NPOC), and South Pacific (SPRFMO) had no VME closures as of 2015.
Positive examples include NAFO's 2015 decision to ban all bottom fishing on seamounts in its area, SEAFO making its closures permanent, and CCAMLR's prohibition of commercial bottom trawling in the Southern Ocean.
The authors argue that closing areas to bottom fishing is the only failsafe method for avoiding SAIs to VMEs, and that other management measures are underutilised and potentially ineffective.


Limitations

The paper is a policy review and webinar presentation, not a primary empirical study; it relies on secondary reporting of RFMO decisions and scientific committee advice.
Findings reflect the state of play as of mid-to-late 2015; subsequent RFMO decisions are not captured.
Several RFMOs discussed (NPOC, SPRFMO, SIOFA) were not yet fully functional, limiting the depth of analysis possible for those regions.
The document does not include quantitative assessments of fishing effort within or outside closures, nor does it model ecological recovery outcomes.
No formal limitations section is provided in the source text; the above are inferred from the scope and format of the work.

