@techreport{InternationalInstrumentConservation2015,
	title = {An {International} {Instrument} on {Conservation} and {Sustainable} {Use} of {Biodiversity} in {Marine} {Areas} beyond {National} {Jurisdiction}: {Matrix} of {Suggestions}},
	institution = {IUCN},
	month = dec,
	year = {2015},
	keywords = {BBNJ, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This document, prepared in December 2015 by an international group of legal experts under the IUCN Environmental Law Centre, serves as a negotiation tool for the UN Preparatory Committee (PrepCom) tasked with developing substantive recommendations for a draft legally binding international instrument on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). It aims to facilitate discussions on the package of issues agreed in 2011 and to offer structured suggestions on elements common to multilateral environmental agreements, in support of UNGA Resolution 69/292 (June 2015).

Methods
The document is a policy and legal analysis rather than an empirical study. Its approach consists of:

Organizing suggestions into six thematic areas: (1) General Principles, Obligations and Scope; (2) Area-Based Management Tools (ABMTs) including Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) and Marine Spatial Planning; (3) Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) and Strategic Environmental Assessment (SEA); (4) Access and Benefit Sharing (ABS) of Marine Genetic Resources (MGR); (5) Capacity Building and Technology Transfer; and (6) Institutional and Operational Aspects.
Presenting, for each sub-topic, a matrix of numbered suggestions with detailed proposals and cross-references to existing treaties and instruments (e.g., UNCLOS, CBD, Nagoya Protocol, CCAMLR, OSPAR, FAO agreements, Antarctic Treaty protocols).
Drawing on comparative analysis of existing multilateral agreements to identify workable institutional, procedural, and substantive models for the new ABNJ instrument.


Key findings
As a suggestions matrix, the document's principal contributions are its structured recommendations across the six thematic areas:

ABMTs and MPAs: Proposes obligations to cooperate in establishing MPAs, criteria and guidelines for designation, management plans with specified contents and measures, a management authority, compliance and enforcement duties, and a global mapping and data base of ABMTs.
EIA/SEA: Recommends a general obligation for EIAs/SEAs, defined thresholds, responsibility allocation, environmental impact statements, notification and consultation with other Parties, public and stakeholder participation, and monitoring and reporting requirements.
ABS/MGR: Suggests definitions for MGR and ABS, general principles, access and benefit-sharing obligations (monetary and non-monetary), mechanisms for commercial research benefits, and a monitoring and enforcement framework for the ABS system.
Capacity Building and Technology Transfer: Calls for provisions on capacity building and transfer of marine technology to developing country Parties.
Institutional and Operational Aspects: Proposes a basic institutional structure, a Clearing-House mechanism (with options to build on the Nagoya Protocol Clearing House or create a new one), a financial mechanism (including a possible global fund or fees from ABNJ activities), reporting and compliance procedures (including a Compliance Committee, incentives, consequences for non-compliance, and inspection systems), implementation and enforcement duties (flag State, port State, and national jurisdiction), and dispute resolution drawing on UNCLOS Part XV or a new mechanism.


Limitations
The document does not contain a formal limitations section. However, the following constraints are evident from the text:

It is explicitly a "living document" and a suggestions matrix, not a final or binding legal text; its purpose is to facilitate negotiation rather than to prescribe outcomes.
It presents multiple alternative suggestions for each topic (e.g., three options for a Clearing-House, five options for a financial mechanism), leaving substantive choices to the PrepCom and ultimately the Parties.
Several cross-references in the document are broken or incomplete (e.g., "section Error! Reference source not found"), indicating the text was in a draft or working state at the time of publication.
The document does not include empirical data, cost-benefit analyses, or scientific assessments; its grounding is entirely in existing treaty language and institutional practice.
It does not address all potential issues in ABNJ governance (e.g., specific species-level conservation measures, climate change interactions, or detailed IP provisions beyond a brief reference to the TRIPS Agreement in the abbreviations list).

