@inproceedings{rochetteWhatRoleNairobi2015,
	address = {Mahé, Seychelles},
	title = {What role for the {Nairobi} {Convention} in the high seas?},
	url = {https://glenwright.earth/library/what-role-nairobi-convention-high-seas/},
	booktitle = {8th {Conference} of {Parties} to the {Nairobi} {Convention} for the {Protection}, {Management} and {Development} of the {Marine} and {Coastal} {Environment} of the {Western} {Indian} {Ocean} {Region}},
	publisher = {IDDRI, FFEM, IUCN},
	author = {Rochette, Julien and Wright, Glen and Spadone, Aurélie},
	month = jun,
	year = {2015},
	keywords = {BBNJ, ABMT / MPAs, not undermine, IFB, author:Glen Wright, OA:open, regional governance},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
The presentation, delivered at COP 8 of the Nairobi Convention in Mahé, Seychelles (22 June 2015) by researchers from the Institute for Sustainable Development and International Relations (IDDRI), Paris, aims to explore the potential role of the Nairobi Convention in governing the high seas, specifically Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ). It examines existing approaches for developing area-based management tools at the regional level and draws lessons from prior experiences to inform future action.

Methods
The document is a slide presentation rather than a full research article. It reviews and categorises several existing governance approaches for ABNJ at the regional level:

Coalition-based approaches, illustrated by the Pelagos Sanctuary and the Sargasso Sea Alliance.
International sectoral measures, including Particularly Sensitive Sea Areas (PSSAs) and Areas of Particular Environmental Interest (APEIs).
Approaches based on regional organisations, specifically the UN Environment Programme Regional Seas programmes.

The authors then synthesise lessons learnt from Regional Seas programmes' experiences, organised around three themes: interests, challenges, and enabling conditions. No empirical data collection, modelling, or formal analytical framework is described in the available text.

Key findings
The presentation identifies the following points drawn from Regional Seas programmes' experiences:

Interests: Advancing ABNJ governance while the UNCLOS Implementation Agreement (IA) negotiations are ongoing; raising awareness of the importance of conserving marine biodiversity in ABNJ; and developing scientific knowledge and management tools.
Challenges: Cooperation and coordination with other competent organisations.
Enabling conditions: An explicit mandate in ABNJ; championing the process; and securing funding to develop agreed activities.

The document closes with the open question "What role for the Nairobi Convention in the high seas?" without providing a definitive answer or set of recommendations in the available text.

Limitations

The source material is a brief slide presentation, not a peer-reviewed article or policy report. It lacks depth, detailed argumentation, and supporting evidence.
No specific conclusions, policy recommendations, or proposed actions for the Nairobi Convention are articulated in the available text.
The document does not include references, a literature review, or methodological detail beyond naming the approaches examined.
The full scope of the presentation (e.g., any additional slides, speaker notes, or discussion outcomes from COP 8) is not captured in the provided text.
Information on the specific institutional context of the Nairobi Convention's existing mandate and how it would interact with the UNCLOS IA process is not elaborated.

