@inproceedings{wrightNegotiationsNewAgreement2016,
	address = {Mahé, Seychelles},
	title = {Negotiations for a new agreement on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in {ABNJ}},
	author = {Wright, Glen},
	month = oct,
	year = {2016},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This presentation by Glen Wright (Institute for Sustainable Development and International Relations, IDDRI), delivered at the "Area Based Planning tools and Regional Cooperation in the Western Indian Ocean" conference in Mahé, Seychelles (13–14 October 2016), aims to brief participants on the status and trajectory of negotiations for a new legally binding agreement on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), commonly referred to as the BBNJ process. It situates these negotiations within the broader context of increasing human activities in the high seas and the need for area-based management tools and regional cooperation.

Methods
The document is a conference presentation rather than a peer-reviewed research article. It does not employ a formal research methodology. Instead, it synthesizes information from:

The UN negotiation timeline, from the 2006 establishment of the BBNJ Working Group through the 2011 "Package Deal," the 2012 Rio+20 commitment, and the 2014–2016 PrepCom meetings.
Published literature, including Merrie et al. (2014) on governance challenges in ABNJ and Block et al. (2014) on apex marine predator movements.
Outcomes and positions recorded at PrepCom 2 (August/September 2016) across the four pillars of the Package Deal.


Key findings

Four-pillar structure: The 2011 Package Deal established the negotiating framework: (1) marine genetic resources and benefit-sharing, (2) area-based management tools including marine protected areas (MPAs), (3) environmental impact assessments (EIAs), and (4) capacity-building and technology transfer.
Progress in convergence: By PrepCom 2 (2016), areas of convergence had emerged in each pillar. For MPAs, agreement centered on a science-based, ecosystem approach and transparent adoption processes. For EIAs, transparency and public availability of reports were broadly supported. For MGRs, non-monetary benefit-sharing and respect for continental shelf rights were noted. Capacity-building was recognized as cross-cutting and vital.
Remaining divergences: Significant unresolved issues persisted, including MPA designation and enforcement, EIA triggers and strategic assessments, the legal status of MGRs and whether derivatives should be covered, and the scope and financing of technology transfer.
Overarching challenges: The presentation highlights tensions between legal gaps and implementation gaps, a narrow implementing agreement versus an ambitious vision, the relationship to existing institutional mandates (e.g., fisheries), and the balance between international and regional governance.
Political dynamics: A "coalition of the willing" was forming, with progressive involvement of Pacific nations, CARICOM, the African Union, and civil society, though some states continued to impede consensus.


Limitations

The document is a conference briefing, not a peer-reviewed article; it lacks a formal research design, data collection, or analytical framework.
It reflects a single point in time (October 2016) in an ongoing negotiation, so all findings are provisional and subject to change.
It provides no empirical data, case studies, or quantitative analysis of ABNJ biodiversity or ecosystem services beyond referencing external graphics.
The scope is limited to the status of multilateral negotiations and does not assess the effectiveness of existing regional instruments or on-the-ground implementation.
Authorship is singular, and no co-authors or institutional review process is indicated.

