@inproceedings{ConservationSustainableUse2016,
	address = {Singapore},
	title = {Conservation and {Sustainable} {Use} of {Marine} {Biologi} cal {Diversity} of {Areas} {Beyond} {National} {Jurisdiction}: {Preparing} for the {Prep} {Com}},
	isbn = {978-92-1-361370-2},
	doi = {10.18356/29045c1f-en},
	author = {Areas, O F and National, Beyond},
	month = feb,
	year = {2016},
	note = {TITLE-CLEAN 2026-09-10: local:missing\_spaces;
TITLE-CLEAN-RESTORE 2026-09-10: from log trunc; was: Conservation and Sustainable Use of Marine Biological Diversity of Areas Beyond National Jurisdictio},
	keywords = {participant},
	annote = {Same paper as Conservation and Sustainable Use of Marine Biologi cal Di..., which already has the PDF.},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This report documents a workshop convened by the Centre for International Law (CIL), National University of Singapore, on 3–4 February 2016. Its purpose was to assist governmental and non-governmental delegations in preparing for the BBNJ Preparatory Committee (PrepCom) established by UN General Assembly Resolution A/RES/69/292 (19 June 2015). The PrepCom was tasked with developing elements of an international legally binding instrument under UNCLOS to address the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ), including marine genetic resources (MGRs), area-based management tools, environmental impact assessments (EIAs), and capacity building and transfer of marine technology.

Methods
The workshop comprised seven structured sessions attended by over 100 participants representing diverse state and non-state interests. The PrepCom Chairman, Ambassador Eden Charles, was present. Each session followed a format of short presentations by subject-matter experts followed by open discussion. Sessions covered:

Background and context (UNCLOS, its implementing agreements, BBNJ process overview)
Conservation and sustainable use of BBNJ
Management tools and institutional arrangements
Marine genetic resources
Access and benefit sharing (ABS)
Capacity building and transfer of marine technology
Mandate and work programme of the PrepCom

Annexes include a list of 17 fundamental unanswered questions and a glossary of defined terms.

Key findings

Deep ideological divides persist between states favouring the common heritage of mankind (CHM) principle and those asserting high-seas freedom for MGRs; participants suggested a pragmatic, sui generis regime as a middle ground.
The 1995 Fish Stocks Agreement was identified as a more suitable structural model for the BBNJ instrument than the 1994 Implementing Agreement, with its Preamble and Parts I and VIII–XIII potentially adaptable with minimal changes.
Fragmentation of the existing ocean governance regime (IMO, FAO, ISA, regional bodies) creates gaps in coordination, cumulative-impact assessment, and consistent area-based management tools (EBSAs, PSSAs, VMEs, APEIs).
No common EIA standards exist across marine and maritime activities, hindering baseline establishment and cumulative-impact monitoring.
The distinction between marine scientific research and bioprospecting remains unresolved, with implications for data sharing and appropriation of MGRs.
Consensus was identified as the only viable path forward; the PrepCom's role is to agree on elements, not final treaty text.
Capacity building and technology transfer (UNCLOS Part XIV) require revitalisation and clearer financial mechanisms.


Limitations

The document is a workshop proceedings report, not a peer-reviewed research article; it records discussions rather than presenting original empirical findings.
The authorship metadata ("Areas, National") appears to be an indexing error; no individual authors are credited in the text.
The provided text is truncated (Session Five on ABS is incomplete), so the full range of ABS discussions is not captured.
The report reflects a single point in time (February 2016) in an evolving multilateral negotiation and does not track subsequent PrepCom outcomes.
Positions expressed represent the views of participants and do not constitute a unified recommendation.

