@inproceedings{wrightEnvironmentalImpactAssessment2017a,
	address = {Lisbon, Portugal},
	title = {Environmental {Impact} {Assessment} in areas beyond national jurisdiction},
	booktitle = {Workshop on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity beyond areas of national jurisdiction through a new legally binding instrument under {UNCLOS}},
	publisher = {Government of Portugal, High Seas Alliance},
	author = {Wright, Glen},
	month = mar,
	year = {2017},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This workshop presentation by Glen Wright (IDDRI, 2017) examines the role of Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ) in the context of negotiating a new legally binding instrument under UNCLOS. The presentation, delivered at the Portuguese Diplomatic Institute in Lisbon (1–3 March 2017), aims to review EIA trends and outcomes, identify good practice, assess the potential contribution of a new agreement, and propose ways to advance the ongoing negotiation discussion.

Methods
The document is a structured workshop discussion rather than an empirical study. Wright draws on:

Review of existing EIA literature (Morgan 2012; Cashmore 2004; Jay et al. 2007; Blasiak et al. 2016).
Analysis of Chair's overviews from PrepCom 2 and PrepCom 3 of the ABNJ negotiations, including state and NGO submissions.
Examination of relevant international instruments: UNCLOS Articles 204–206, the Madrid Protocol (1991), the Espoo Convention (1991), the Kiev Protocol on Strategic Environmental Assessment, and CBD Guidelines.
Identification of areas of convergence, emerging convergence, and remaining points of divergence among negotiating parties.


Key findings

EIA use is widespread and growing, but poor-quality EIA is common and the process is often threatened by economic imperatives; outcomes for decision-making remain mixed.
Good practice in EIA includes a focus on biodiversity and impacts, transparency, stakeholder involvement, a review mechanism, and the ability to impose mitigation conditions or disallow activities posing substantial harm.
A new ABNJ agreement could reaffirm EIA obligations, bring coherence to the global EIA system, set a clear and uniform process, and extend coverage to cumulative, transboundary, and strategic environmental assessment (SEA).
Four areas of convergence were identified at PrepCom 2: contribution to conservation and sustainable use; non-undermining of existing instruments; transparency and stakeholder involvement; and public availability of reports.
Twenty-six areas for further discussion remain, with key themes including thresholds, procedure, oversight, transboundary EIA, and SEA.
Building on UNCLOS Articles 204–206 (threshold of "substantial pollution or significant and harmful changes") does not advance the discussion much beyond existing areas of convergence.
Emerging areas of convergence include an obligation to conduct EIAs, state responsibility, ecosystem-based and precautionary principles, minimum content of EIA statements, and building on existing instruments.
NGOs contributed actively and extensively to the discussion, as reflected in the Chair's non-paper.


Limitations

The document is a workshop presentation and discussion paper, not a peer-reviewed empirical study; it presents a state-of-play snapshot rather than original research findings.
The negotiation process was still in early stages; Wright notes "a long way to go to narrow down the options," so conclusions are provisional.
General challenges are acknowledged but not resolved: lack of marine environmental knowledge, technical and cost barriers, tension between good practice and the status quo, and the trade-off between pragmatism and ambition.
Specific institutional design questions (which body oversees EIA, role of existing vs. new institutions, management of a potentially huge range of stakeholders) remain open.
The document does not provide quantitative evidence on EIA effectiveness in ABNJ contexts; it relies on qualitative literature review and negotiation analysis.

