@inproceedings{iucnBiodiversityNationalJurisdictions2017,
	address = {Gland, Switzerland},
	title = {Biodiversity {Beyond} {National} {Jurisdictions}: {Area}-based {Management} {Tools}, including {Marine} {Protected} {Areas}},
	author = {{IUCN}},
	month = may,
	year = {2017},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This IUCN workshop document (May 2017) outlines the rationale, objectives, and agenda for a two-day workshop on Area-based Management Tools (ABMTs), including Marine Protected Areas (MPAs), in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). The workshop aimed to provide scientific, legal, and policy input for UN General Assembly (UNGA) discussions on a new implementing agreement under UNCLOS. Specific goals included exploring the role of ABMTs in advancing conservation and sustainable use, examining designation and management procedures for ABMT networks, and identifying mechanisms for institutional cooperation. The document also sought to explore sectoral ABMTs, common criteria, and regional implementation approaches, with concrete case studies in the South West Indian Ocean and the North East Atlantic.

Methods
The workshop was held over two days (16–17 May 2017) at IUCN Headquarters in Gland, Switzerland, with 20–25 participants including scientists, lawyers, managers, and experts from relevant organisations. Discussions followed Chatham House rules, with participants speaking in personal capacities. The agenda was structured around four half-day sessions tracking the process of ABMT identification through compliance:

Session 1: Identification and validation of potential ABMTs, including criteria, scientific information sources, and straddling areas.
Session 2: Validation, designation, and declaration processes, including Marine Spatial Planning, EBSAs, VMEs, PSSAs, and ISA/APEI processes.
Session 3: Implementation and management, covering responsible bodies, regional organisations, monitoring, and cross-sectoral cooperation.
Session 4: Control, surveillance, and compliance mechanisms, including the role of regional organisations and stakeholders.

Transversal questions addressed across sessions included definitions, MPA network importance, sectoral conservation measures, legal effects of designation, capacity building, technology transfer, and financial mechanisms.

Key findings
