Objective
This document is not a scholarly research paper but rather the agenda and program for a one-day training event on BBNJ (Biodiversity Beyond National Jurisdiction) negotiations, hosted in 2018. The stated purpose of the training was to prepare participants for upcoming UN BBNJ negotiations and to strengthen regional ocean governance for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ). The event was organized under the STRONG High Seas project, a five-year initiative coordinated by the Institute for Advanced Sustainability Studies (IASS) in partnership with the Secretariat of the Permanent Commission for the South Pacific (CPPS) and the Abidjan Convention Secretariat, targeting the Southeast Pacific and Southeast Atlantic regions.

Methods
The training was structured as a single-day workshop combining presentations, guided discussions, and small-group working sessions. The format included:

Keynote presentations on what to expect from the BBNJ negotiations (Charlotte Salpin, DOALOS) and the state of play of the negotiations (Glen Wright, IDDRI).
A presentation on the relevance of regional and sectoral ocean governance to implementing a strong international instrument (Kristina Gjerde, IUCN).
An open plenary discussion on opportunities and challenges of the BBNJ negotiations for participants' countries and regions.
Three moderated working groups focusing on the BBNJ "building blocks": (I) Marine Genetic Resources including capacity building and technology transfer; (II) Area-based Management Tools including MPAs; and (III) Environmental Impact Assessments. Each group prepared a summary presentation for the plenary.
A closing plenary discussion synthesizing working group outcomes.

Hosts included Germany's Federal Ministry for the Environment (BMU), the CPPS Secretariat, the Abidjan Convention Secretariat, and the STRONG High Seas Project. The project was funded through the International Climate Initiative (IKI) with support from the German Bundestag.

Key findings
No research findings, data, or analytical results are reported in this document. It is an event agenda and does not present original research, hypotheses, or empirical results. The document does, however, identify the three core "building blocks" of the BBNJ negotiations as the focal deliverables: marine genetic resources (including benefit-sharing, capacity building, and technology transfer), area-based management tools (including marine protected areas), and environmental impact assessments. It also frames the broader goal of coordinating regional and sectoral governance to support an integrated, ecosystem-based management approach for ABNJ.

Limitations

This is a program/agenda document, not a peer-reviewed article or research report. It contains no methodology, data, results, or conclusions in the scholarly sense.
No participant list, attendance figures, or outcomes of the working group discussions are recorded in the text provided.
The document does not specify the exact date, venue, or number of participants.
