Objective
This document is a training briefing prepared by Glen Wright (IDDRI) for the IKI STRONG High Seas project, delivered on 3 September 2018 in New York. Its purpose is to orient participants on the state of play of the BBNJ (Biodiversity Beyond National Jurisdiction) negotiations immediately ahead of the opening of formal intergovernmental negotiations. It aims to summarise the negotiation timeline, current state positions, and the key substantive issues under discussion.

Methods
The document is a structured presentation rather than a peer-reviewed research study. It draws on:

A chronological review of the BBNJ process from the 2004 Working Group through the 2016–2017 Preparatory Committee sessions.
A categorisation of state positions into three groups (advocates, facilitators, reluctant participants) based on observed behaviour in informal and formal UN fora.
A thematic breakdown of the four "Package Deal" elements (marine genetic resources, area-based management tools, environmental impact assessments, capacity building and technology transfer) and overarching institutional questions.

No empirical data collection, statistical analysis, or original fieldwork is described. The content reflects the author's institutional perspective (IDDRI) and the IKI STRONG High Seas project framing.

Key findings

Timeline: The BBNJ process spans from the 2004 Working Group, through the 2011 "Package Deal" agreement, the 2012 Rio+20 commitment, 2014–2015 Working Group meetings, 2016–2017 PrepCom sessions, to the start of formal negotiations in September 2018, with four two-week Intergovernmental Conference sessions planned for 2018–2020.
Core tensions: Persistent differences among states include new treaty versus improved implementation of existing law, Common Heritage of Mankind versus high seas freedoms, and conservation versus marine genetic resources and capacity building.
State positions (as of September 2018): Advocates for a new instrument include the EU, G77/China and Mexico, and African, Caribbean, and Pacific states. Facilitators seeking compromise include Australia, New Zealand, Canada, and Norway. Reluctant but active participants include the US, Japan, Iceland, South Korea, and Russia.
Key substantive issues: For MGRs – legal status, biodiscovery complexity, and benefit-sharing form. For ABMTs – effective mechanisms, types, duration, and relationship to existing bodies. For EIA – scope, responsibility, and legal effect. For capacity building – coordination, voluntary vs. compulsory obligations, and data/technology sharing modalities.
Overarching elements: Institutional structure, monitoring and review, the "not undermining" principle, fisheries interface, MCS, adjacency/compatibility, and funding.


Limitations

The document is a point-in-time snapshot (September 2018) produced before formal negotiations commenced; it does not reflect outcomes of the 2018–2020 IGC sessions or the eventual 2023 High Seas Treaty.
It is a training/briefing presentation, not a peer-reviewed analytical article; no methodology for categorising state positions is detailed.
The perspective is that of a single NGO (IDDRI) and a specific project (IKI STRONG High Seas), which may shape emphasis and framing.
No citations, footnotes, or references to primary UN documents are provided within the text itself.

