Objective
This 2018 workshop presentation by Glen Wright (IDDRI, Paris) reviews the state of Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) in Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (ABNJ), with particular attention to fisheries management. It aims to map existing legal obligations, identify gaps in current practice, and raise questions for strengthening EIA frameworks in the high seas context.

Methods
The document is a policy and legal review delivered as a workshop talk (Paris, 17 May 2018). It does not employ empirical or experimental methods. Instead, it:

Defines the EIA process (screening, scoping, assessment, notification, reporting, decision-making) drawing on the CBD (1992, Art. 14).
Reviews applicable international legal instruments, including UNCLOS (Arts. 192, 206), the 1994 Part XI Agreement, the London Convention/Protocol, the Fish Stocks Agreement (1995), the FAO Code of Conduct (1995), UNGA resolution 61/105 (2006), and FAO Deep-Sea Fisheries Guidelines (2008).
Surveys secondary literature on EIA trends, outcomes, and good practice (Morgan 2012; Cashmore 2004; Jay et al. 2007; IUCN 2015; UNEP/CBD 2009; Warner 2015).
Identifies open policy questions for future governance of high-seas fisheries.


Key findings

EIA use is widespread and growing, but poor-quality assessments are common and the process is often undermined by economic imperatives.
Current EIA obligations in ABNJ are fragmented across sectoral instruments (seabed mining, waste dumping, fisheries) rather than integrated under a single comprehensive framework.
Bottom-fishing impact assessments frequently fail to meet FAO Guidelines; they are often incomplete, subject to considerable scientific uncertainty, and lack holistic, cumulative, or transboundary scope.
Vulnerable Marine Ecosystems (VMEs) remain largely open to fishing; there is limited willingness to broaden EIA scope beyond managed target stocks.
Good-practice elements identified include transparency, stakeholder engagement, review mechanisms, and the ability to impose mitigation conditions or disallow activities posing substantial harm.
Existing models for transboundary EIA (Madrid Protocol/ATS, Espoo Convention) could inform ABNJ governance but have not been applied to high-seas fisheries.
Key unresolved questions include whether to strengthen or impose more stringent obligations, whether EIA should cover existing as well as new fisheries, how to integrate fisheries EIA with other sectors and Area-Mineral-Bottom Technologies (AMBTs), and how to ensure meaningful participation beyond RFMO parties.


Limitations

The document is a workshop presentation (slide deck), not a peer-reviewed article; it lacks formal methodology, data analysis, or case-study evidence.
It is descriptive and normative rather than analytical; the "food for thought" section presents open questions without proposing or testing solutions.
No empirical assessment of specific EIA outcomes in ABNJ fisheries is provided; claims about poor-quality assessments are attributed to secondary sources without independent verification.
The scope is limited to a single author's synthesis; no comparative analysis across jurisdictions or sectors is conducted.
Information on the specific workshop context, audience, or subsequent policy uptake is not included in the document.

