@inproceedings{PackageMarineBiodiversity2020,
	address = {Royal Society of Edinburgh},
	title = {Towards a {Package}: {Marine} {Biodiversity} {Beyond} {National} {Jurisdiction}},
	month = jan,
	year = {2020},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This document is a draft agenda (as of 13 January 2020) for a two-day Royal Society of Edinburgh meeting held on 22–23 January 2020, convened to facilitate structured discussion among 20–30 participants—diplomats, policy makers, scientists, activists, industry experts, and academics—on the negotiation of a legally binding instrument (ILBI) on marine biodiversity beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ). The stated aim was to explore key issues and areas of divergence identified in the November 2019 draft ILBI, develop possible positions and solutions, and ultimately circulate a short position paper to assist further multilateral discussion. The meeting was held under Chatham House rules to encourage open challenge and development of ideas.

Methods
The event was structured as a series of moderated discussion sessions rather than a research study. Key methodological features included:

Pre-circulation of short information summaries on key background points and possible positions, drawing on the November 2019 draft ILBI and its identified areas of divergence.
Eight themed sessions across two days, each featuring two short presentations (2 × 5 minutes) followed by a 30-minute moderated discussion.
Named moderators (Lydia Slobodian, David Johnson) tasked with encouraging challenge and development of possible solutions.
Remote participation available for those unable to attend in person; notes taken by student rapporteurs.
A public panel session, a film screening (Ocean Song), and an informal dinner as supplementary engagement activities.
Post-meeting circulation of a short position paper synthesising points explored, consistent with the Chatham House nature of the discussions.


Key findings
This document is a meeting agenda and planning document, not a research output. It does not report empirical findings, data analysis, or conclusions. However, it identifies the following thematic areas as focal points of divergence and discussion in the BBNJ negotiations, each mapped to specific articles of the November 2019 draft ILBI:

Marine genetic resources (MGRs) and intellectual property rights, trade secrets, and technology transfer (Arts. 4, 10, 11, 12, 42–45).
MGRs and digital sequence information (DSI), including definitions and scope (Arts. 1, 8, 10, 11, 13, 42).
Access and benefit sharing, including level of obligation, scope, and delivery (Arts. 7, 10, 10bis, 11, 13).
Capacity building and technology transfer (CB\&TT), drawing on bilateral lessons and deep-sea research challenges (Arts. 32, 42–44, 46, 47, 51, 52, Annex II).
Environmental impact assessment and assessing deep-sea ecosystems, including vulnerability and monetary valuation (Arts. 21bis, 22, 23, 24, 29, 31, 35, 38).
BBNJ principles, pragmatism, and linkages to EBSA, post-2020 CBD targets, SDGs, and the Ocean Decade (Preamble, Arts. 2, 4, 5, 23, 25–27, 39, Annex I).
Balancing conservation and sustainable use, multi-stakeholder engagement, and the role of science (Arts. 14, 15, 17, 22, 34, 35, 37, 43, 49, 51).
Renewing momentum toward consensus at the fourth Intergovernmental Committee (IGC4) (Preamble, Arts. 2, 6).


Limitations

The document is a draft agenda, not a completed research paper; no findings, data, or conclusions are reported.
It is a single-event planning document and does not capture the actual discussions, positions reached, or outcomes of the meeting.
Participant perspectives, the content of presentations, and the substance of discussions are not included.
The referenced position paper to be circulated afterwards is not provided in this document.
No authorship, peer review, or institutional affiliation details are given beyond the Royal Society of Edinburgh venue.

