@inproceedings{wrightEnvironmentalImpactAssessment2019,
	title = {Environmental {Impact} {Assessment} in {Areas} {Beyond} {National} {Jurisdiction}},
	booktitle = {Pew {Charitable} {Trusts} workshop "{Protecting} at least 30\% of the ocean by 2030 and {Conservation} \& sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction"},
	author = {Wright, Glen},
	month = nov,
	year = {2019},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This 2019 workshop presentation by Glen Wright (Pew Charitable Trusts / IDDRI), delivered at a meeting in Amman, Jordan on conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), examines how Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) requirements can be strengthened and incorporated into a new international agreement. The goal is to review existing legal provisions, identify coverage gaps, and evaluate the President's Draft Text to propose how EIA best practices could be embedded in treaty language.

Methods
The presentation is a policy analysis rather than an empirical study. Wright's approach includes:

Reviewing the EIA process (screening, scoping, assessment, notification, reporting, decision-making) and trends in its application.
Surveying existing international instruments that contain EIA or related obligations, including the CBD (1992), UNCLOS (Arts. 192, 204–206), the 1994 Part XI Agreement, the London Convention/Protocol, the Fish Stocks Agreement, the Espoo Convention (1991), and the CCAMLR/Madrid Protocol.
Identifying sectors and activities in ABNJ not yet covered by any EIA regime (e.g., cables, pipelines, marine scientific research, bioprospecting, shipping, military activities).
Analyzing specific articles of the President's Draft Text (Arts. 22–41) to assess thresholds, coverage, institutional arrangements, stakeholder participation, decision-making, and review mechanisms.


Key findings

EIA use is widespread and growing, but poor-quality assessments are common and economic pressures threaten rigorous application.
Significant gaps exist: many ABNJ activities (seabed installations, high-seas shipping, emerging uses) fall outside current sectoral EIA regimes.
A new agreement could reaffirm obligations, bring coherence to the global EIA system, set a clear uniform process, and address cumulative, transboundary, and strategic impacts.
A strong threshold aligned with the precautionary principle is desirable; a tiered approach (as in CCAMLR) could balance ambition with pragmatism.
Decision-making authority should rest with a competent authority rather than the activity proponent, with transparency and dispute-resolution mechanisms.
Broad notification and consultation—including adjacent coastal states, regional IGOS, and the general public—should be mandatory minimum standards.
Strategic Environmental Assessment (SEA) provisions need further detail and could be supported by a scientific/technical body.
The treaty should complement, not undermine, existing sectoral frameworks while setting an agreed global standard.


Limitations

The document is a workshop presentation and policy advocacy piece, not a peer-reviewed research article; no original empirical data or statistical analysis is presented.
It analyzes a draft text that was still under negotiation at the time of writing, so conclusions are provisional.
The EIA process is explicitly described as "simplified," and the treatment of legal instruments is high-level rather than a detailed doctrinal analysis.
As a single-author presentation from an advocacy organization (Pew/IDDRI), it reflects a particular policy perspective rather than a balanced academic review.
No comparative case-study evidence is provided to support claims about EIA effectiveness or the quality of existing assessments.

