@techreport{ProtectingSitesPotential2020,
	title = {Protecting sites of potential {Outstanding} {Universa} l {Value} in marine {Areas} {Beyond} {National} {Jurisdiction}: {The} practical modalities},
	url = {https://unesdoc.unesco.org/ark:/48223/pf0000374415},
	institution = {UNESCO},
	year = {2020},
	note = {TITLE-CLEAN 2026-09-10: local:midword\_spaces;
TITLE-CLEAN-RESTORE 2026-09-10: from log trunc; was: Protecting sites of potential Outstanding Universal Value in marine Areas Beyond National Jurisdicti},
	keywords = {BBNJ, ABMT / MPAs, not undermine, IFB, OA:unknown, author:Glen Wright},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This UNESCO World Heritage Centre publication (2020) aims to provide the World Heritage Committee with an overview of practical modalities and recommended next steps for advancing the protection of sites of potential Outstanding Universal Value (OUV) in marine areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ). It responds to the 2011 independent evaluation by the UNESCO external auditor, which recommended that States establish "workable provisions adapted for the High Seas" to preserve natural heritage of OUV not dependent on State sovereignty.

Methods
The document is a summary of an expert workshop held in Monte Carlo, Monaco, on 11–12 December 2018, co-chaired with the Natural Resources Defense Council. Participants included experts in international law, marine ecology, geology, policy, and World Heritage governance. The analysis builds on the 2016 joint UNESCO-IUCN report, World Heritage in the High Seas: An Idea Whose Time Has Come, and examines the legal text of the 1972 World Heritage Convention, its Operational Guidelines, UNCLOS provisions, and ongoing UN negotiations for an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) on marine biodiversity in ABNJ.

Key findings

Nothing in the text of the 1972 Convention or the Operational Guidelines explicitly excludes ABNJ sites from the Convention's ambit; the gap is characterized as a historical oversight rather than an intentional limitation.
Of the 50 marine sites on the World Heritage List (across 37 countries), all are located within national jurisdiction. ABNJ covers approximately half the planet's surface and is entirely excluded from current protection mechanisms under the Convention.
Five sites were identified in the 2016 report as potentially meeting OUV criteria: the Costa Rica Thermal Dome, the White Shark Café, the Sargasso Sea, the Lost City Hydrothermal Field, and the Atlantis Bank.
The workshop recommended focusing on the Costa Rica Thermal Dome and the Sargasso Sea as priority case studies, given existing stewardship efforts and scientific documentation.
Specific amendments to the Operational Guidelines are advisable to clarify the nomination, inscription, management, and reporting processes for ABNJ sites, including who may nominate, how protection is ensured, and how state-of-conservation reporting would function.
States Parties could use Tentative Lists to address thematic gaps, and harmonization of Tentative Lists at regional and thematic levels is encouraged.
Synergies exist with the ongoing UNCLOS Intergovernmental Conference negotiating an ILBI that may include area-based management tools, including marine protected areas, which could provide a management structure for inscribed ABNJ World Heritage sites.


Limitations

The document is a summary of expert workshop discussions and does not constitute a statement of policy, decision, or position on behalf of UNESCO.
The gender dimension was not specifically analyzed in the workshop proceedings; the authors note it should be considered in future detailed reflections on management.
A more detailed legal review of the Operational Guidelines and proposed amendments was still underway at the time of publication, with results expected by the end of 2020.
No overarching legal framework for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in ABNJ existed at the time; the ILBI negotiations were still in progress (final session planned for March 2020).
The document does not present original empirical research; it synthesizes prior legal analysis, the 2016 UNESCO-IUCN report, and expert opinion.

