Objective
This document reports on the third session of the Preparatory Committee (PrepCom 3), convened under UN General Assembly Resolution 69/292, held 27 March – 7 April 2017 at UN Headquarters in New York. The PrepCom's mandate is to make substantive recommendations to the General Assembly on the elements of a draft text of an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) under UNCLOS for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ). PrepCom 3 specifically aimed to advance increasingly detailed proposals on the possible elements of the ILBI and to prepare the way for PrepCom 4 (July 2017), which would recommend to the General Assembly whether to convene an intergovernmental conference (IGC) to finalize negotiations.

Methods
The session was structured around informal working groups and plenary sessions, facilitated by designated country representatives. A Chair's non-paper, compiled from written submissions received up to December 2016 (plus a supplement), provided the working framework. Delegates addressed the following topic areas in turn:

Marine genetic resources (MGRs), including access and benefit-sharing, intellectual property rights, and a clearinghouse mechanism
Area-based management tools (ABMTs), including marine protected areas (MPAs)
Environmental impact assessments (EIAs)
Capacity building and marine technology transfer (CB\&TT)
Cross-cutting issues: scope of the ILBI, its relationship with existing instruments, and institutional arrangements

The document is published as the Earth Negotiations Bulletin (Vol. 25, No. 129) by the International Institute for Sustainable Development (IISD) Reporting Services, authored by Elisa Morgera, Asheline Appleton, Tallash Kantai, and Asterios Tsioumanis, with editorial oversight by Pamela Chasek.

Key findings

PrepCom 3 was broadly regarded as a positive step forward, with delegates exchanging increasingly detailed substantive proposals on all five topic areas.
Significant divisions persisted on the scope of MGRs, particularly whether fish (as commodities versus genetic resources) should be included, and whether definitions should cover derivatives and digital sequence information.
Debates on ABMTs and MPAs touched on the role of regional and sectoral bodies, the integration of traditional knowledge, and the level of ambition for conservation obligations.
Cross-cutting discussions highlighted the need to clarify the ILBI's relationship with UNCLOS, the CBD, the Nagoya Protocol, and other existing instruments, as well as the design of institutional arrangements (COP, secretariat, scientific body).
The editorial analysis noted that many remaining questions (e.g., mandatory top-down regulation versus a horizontal, persuasive approach; private-sector engagement; cost-effectiveness) are more suited to an IGC than a PrepCom.
No donor country pledged financial support for the future ILBI, a point flagged by NGOs as a worrying sign for inclusiveness.
The session concluded with a request for an updated, streamlined Chair's non-paper and draft substantive recommendations for PrepCom 4.


Limitations

The document is a negotiation reporting bulletin, not a peer-reviewed scholarly article; it records and lightly analyzes diplomatic exchanges rather than presenting original empirical research.
The provided text is truncated (indicated by ellipses), so full coverage of the ABMT, EIA, CB\&TT, and cross-cutting working-group discussions is incomplete.
