Objective
The fourth session of the Preparatory Committee (PrepCom) on marine biodiversity beyond areas of national jurisdiction (BBNJ), convened 10–21 July 2017 at UN Headquarters in New York, was the final session mandated by UN General Assembly Resolution 69/292. Its purpose was to finalize substantive recommendations on the elements of a draft text of an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) under the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) for the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction (ABNJ), enabling the General Assembly to decide before the end of its 72nd session whether to convene an intergovernmental conference (IGC).

Methods
The session employed a multi-track negotiation format. Key procedural elements included:

Plenary sessions for general statements and consideration of the chapeau to draft recommendations.
Informal working groups facilitated by designated chairs on five topic areas: marine genetic resources (MGRs) and benefit-sharing; area-based management tools (ABMTs) including marine protected areas (MPAs); environmental impact assessments (EIAs); capacity building and marine technology transfer (CB\&TT); and cross-cutting issues.
Use of the Chair's indicative suggestions (circulated 31 May 2017) and a streamlined non-paper on elements of a draft ILBI (circulated 6 July 2017) as reference documents.
Informal closed-door negotiations, bilateral consultations with Chair Carlos Sobral Duarte (Brazil), and regional coordination meetings extending into the evening of the final day.


Key findings
The PrepCom outcome was adopted by consensus after intensive informal negotiations. The document identifies the following principal results:

A set of non-exclusive elements of a draft ILBI text that generated convergence among most delegations, covering the 2011 "package" of issues (MGRs/benefit-sharing, ABMTs/MPAs, EIAs, CB\&TT, and cross-cutting matters).
A list of main issues on which divergence of views persists, with the explicit indication that neither the convergent elements nor the divergent issues reflect full consensus.
A recommendation to the UN General Assembly to take a decision, as soon as possible, on convening an IGC to elaborate the text of the agreement.
Most delegations considered the PrepCom had completed its mandate, though a minority cautioned against prejudging the Assembly's decision.

The document also provides a historical overview tracing the BBNJ process from the 59th General Assembly session (2004) through nine Working Group meetings (2006–2015), the Rio+20 commitment (2012), Resolution 69/292 (2014), and PrepCom sessions 1–3 (2016–2017).

Limitations

This is a negotiation reporting service (Earth Negotiations Bulletin, IISD), not a peer-reviewed scholarly article; it records procedural and substantive developments rather than presenting original research.
The provided text is partial, with sections elided (marked by […]), meaning the full detail of draft ILBI elements, the closing plenary, and the brief analysis are incomplete.
