Objective
The first session of the Intergovernmental Conference (IGC) on an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) under the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ) convened from 4–17 September 2018 at UN Headquarters in New York. The session aimed to conduct substantive discussions on the four elements of the "package" agreed in 2011: marine genetic resources (MGRs) including benefit-sharing; environmental impact assessments (EIAs); area-based management tools (ABMTs) including marine protected areas (MPAs); and capacity building and marine technology transfer (CB\&TT). The broader mandate, set by UN General Assembly resolution 72/249 (December 2017), was to elaborate the text of the ILBI as soon as possible, with four IGC sessions scheduled through early 2020.

Methods
The document is a reporting service summary (Earth Negotiations Bulletin, Vol. 25 No. 179, published by IISD) rather than a peer-reviewed research article. The IGC-1 process involved:

Consideration of a President's "aid to discussions" document identifying areas for further debate without treaty text.
Informal working groups on each of the four package elements, facilitated by designated states (Belize, New Zealand, Netherlands, Palau).
General statements by delegations on procedural expectations and ILBI content.
Plenary discussions on the way forward, including the preparation of a zero draft or equivalent document for IGC-2.
Consensus-based decision-making, with the IGC organizational meeting (April 2018) setting the procedural framework.


Key findings

IGC-1 made progress in clarifying delegations' positions on the four package elements and tabled more detailed options for the ABMT process.
Multiple delegations (G-77/China, EU, Switzerland, IUCN, High Seas Alliance) urged a shift to text-based negotiations via a zero draft before IGC-2; others (US, Norway, Japan) preferred a less formal document reflecting the spectrum of options.
President Lee (Singapore) proposed preparing a document—deliberately not labelled a "zero draft"—containing treaty language and reflecting options on all four elements, to be circulated well in advance of IGC-2.
On ABMTs, three governance models were debated: a global decision-making body with full mandate; a hybrid model sharing responsibilities with regional and sectoral bodies; and a regional approach limiting the ILBI to setting standards and principles.
New substantive proposals emerged, including interim measures to prevent degradation pending MPA establishment, and temporary ABMTs for species recovery alongside permanent MPAs as an "insurance policy" for ecosystem resilience.
The closing atmosphere was described as positive, with most delegations and civil society expressing confidence in the President's ability to produce a workable textual basis for IGC-2.


Limitations

The document is a negotiation reporting summary, not a peer-reviewed scholarly publication; it reflects procedural and diplomatic positions rather than empirical research.
The provided text is partial, with sections elided (marked by "[…]"), meaning full coverage of MGRs, EIAs, CB\&TT, and institutional arrangements is incomplete.
No treaty text was produced at IGC-1; the session was explicitly limited to identifying areas for further discussion.
The document itself notes that scientific information on BBNJ, "albeit insufficient," underpins the urgency of the negotiations.
Significant divergences remained on core issues (e.g., benefit-sharing models, governance architecture), and the document does not resolve them.
