Objective
The Second Session of the Intergovernmental Conference (IGC-2) on Biodiversity in Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (BBNJ), held 25 March–5 April 2019 at UN Headquarters in New York, aimed to elaborate the text of an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) under the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS). The instrument is intended to address the conservation and sustainable use of marine biodiversity in areas beyond national jurisdiction, structured around four agreed package elements: marine genetic resources (MGRs); area-based management tools including marine protected areas; environmental impact assessments (EIAs); and capacity building and marine technology transfer.

Methods
This document is a negotiation report published by the International Institute for Sustainable Development (IISD) as part of its Earth Negotiations Bulletin series. It is not a peer-reviewed research article but a structured summary of multilateral deliberations. Delegates worked from the IGC President's Aid to Negotiations, which presented options organized along the four package elements. Informal working groups, each facilitated by a designated delegate (e.g., Janine Coye-Felson for MGRs, Alice Revell for ABMTs, René Lefeber for EIAs, Olai Uludong for capacity building), met daily from 25 March to 5 April, with facilitators reporting to plenary. The report also includes a brief historical overview of the BBNJ process (from the 2004 Working Group through IGC-1) and an analytical section interpreting the negotiations.

Key findings

Convergence was achieved on promoting coherence and synergies with existing frameworks, recognizing benefit-sharing as part of conservation and sustainable use, and affirming that EIAs are mutually supportive with other instruments.
Delegates could not bridge major gaps on the scope of the instrument, whether benefit-sharing would be monetary or non-monetary, and the overarching governing principles—specifically the tension between the "common heritage of humankind" and the "freedom of the high seas."
On MGRs, debates centered on geographical, material, and temporal scope; access and benefit-sharing modalities; intellectual property rights; and monitoring of utilization. The inclusion of digital sequence information and derivatives remained contested.
On EIAs, the vast majority supported outlining assessment steps, but disagreement persisted on the compulsory and binding character of the exercise, the responsible entity, and the role of strategic environmental assessments.
President Rena Lee (Singapore) indicated her intention to circulate a concise "no-options" document containing treaty text ahead of IGC-3 (19–30 August 2019), and to revise the meeting format toward more informal, in-depth negotiations.
Experienced negotiators cautioned that the core underlying disagreement between common heritage and high seas freedom could "catch the process in the undertow," while noting that technical divergences on individual elements would likely be resolved through trade-offs.


Limitations

The document is a reporting summary of a single negotiation session, not a peer-reviewed scholarly analysis; it reflects the positions and statements of delegates rather than independent empirical findings.
The report itself notes that scientific information on BBNJ, while growing, remains "insufficient," particularly regarding the richness and vulnerability of deep-sea ecosystems.
No agreement or draft treaty text was produced at IGC-2; the process was in its second of four scheduled sessions, with substantial unresolved divergence on foundational principles.
The analytical section ("Caught in the Undertow," "Getting to Shore") offers interpretive commentary by the ENB authors but is not subject to formal peer review.
Specific positions of individual delegations are summarized selectively; the full record of statements and proposals is not reproduced.

