Objective
The Third Session of the Intergovernmental Conference (IGC-3) on the Conservation and Sustainable Use of Marine Biodiversity of Areas Beyond National Jurisdiction (BBNJ), held 19–30 August 2019 at UN Headquarters in New York, aimed to conduct the first round of textual negotiations on a "zero draft" for an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) under UNCLOS. The draft, prepared by IGC President Rena Lee (Singapore), addressed general provisions, cross-cutting issues, and the four elements of the 2011 "package": marine genetic resources (MGRs) and benefit-sharing; area-based management tools (ABMTs) including marine protected areas (MPAs); environmental impact assessments (EIAs); and capacity building and transfer of marine technology (CB\&TT).

Methods
This document is a negotiation report (Earth Negotiations Bulletin, Vol. 25 No. 218, published by IISD, 1 September 2019), not a peer-reviewed research article. The process was multilateral diplomatic negotiation: more than 400 participants from governments, international organizations, civil society, and academia deliberated on the zero draft text. Discussions were organized around the four package elements and cross-cutting issues (objectives, general principles, relationship with other instruments, institutional arrangements, dispute settlement, and funding). A revised draft for IGC-4 (March 2020) was to be developed from the discussions and submissions.

Key findings
Deliberations were described as productive, with a cooperative spirit noted by delegates and observers. Specific progress included:

MGRs and benefit-sharing: Convergence on temporal scope, inclusion of benefit-sharing modalities in the ILBI rather than deferring to the COP, referencing non-monetary benefits, and including a definition of MGRs.
ABMTs/MPAs: Agreement on developing proposals based on best available science (including traditional knowledge of indigenous peoples and local communities), submission of proposals by states parties, and adopting streamlined, outcome-oriented objectives.
EIAs: New provisions proposed to address gaps and streamline text; support for strategic environmental assessments, though further development was needed.
CB\&TT: Convergence on most listed objectives, cooperation at all levels, types and categories of CB\&TT, and the desirability of a clearing-house mechanism.
Cross-cutting issues: Progress on objectives, general principles, relationship with other instruments, institutional arrangements, dispute settlement, and funding, though full consensus was not reached.


Limitations
Significant divergence remained. Delegates noted that areas of convergence and divergence "do not stand on equal ground," with vast disagreement persisting on the substance of provisions and the foundational premise of the agreement. Unresolved issues included:

Whether to include fish as a commodity or only for genetic properties; scope of MGRs (in situ, ex situ, digital sequence information, derivatives).
Whether benefit-sharing should be mandatory or voluntary, and its modalities and triggers.
The role of global, regional, and sectoral bodies in ABMTs; whether MPA processes should be distinguished from ABMTs.
EIA operational provisions: thresholds, criteria, degree of internationalization, and relationship with other EIA processes.
Whether CB\&TT obligations should be voluntary or mandatory; who should benefit; terms and conditions; and concerns about imposing obligations on the private sector.
The overarching legal principle: common heritage of humankind versus freedom of the high seas.

The document also notes that scientific information on BBNJ, while revealing richness and vulnerability, remains insufficient. Several delegates cautioned that a fifth IGC session may be needed and warned against forcing adoption of a "half-baked agreement."
