@inproceedings{FourthSessionIntergovernmental2022,
	address = {United Nations, New York City, NY, USA},
	title = {Fourth session of the {Intergovernmental} conference on an international legally binding instrument under the {United} {Nations} {Convention} on the {Law} of the {Sea} on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction},
	url = {https://www.un.org/bbnj/content/fourth-substantive-session},
	month = mar,
	year = {2022},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
The Fourth Session of the Intergovernmental Conference (IGC-4), held 7–18 March 2022 at UN Headquarters in New York, was the final session mandated by UN General Assembly resolution 72/249 to elaborate the text of an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) under the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ). The session aimed to conclude negotiations on the four elements of the 2011 package: marine genetic resources (MGRs) including benefit-sharing, area-based management tools (ABMTs) including marine protected areas (MPAs), environmental impact assessments (EIAs), and capacity building and transfer of marine technology (CB\&TT), as well as cross-cutting issues and institutional arrangements.

Methods
This document is a reporting bulletin (Earth Negotiations Bulletin, Vol. 25 No. 225) by the International Institute for Sustainable Development, summarizing the proceedings of IGC-4. The negotiation process itself employed the following approaches:

Informal-informal meeting format with text-based negotiations on a revised draft treaty text.
Small group discussions on similar proposals and cross-regional drafting exercises to produce consensus text.
Joint textual proposals submitted by delegations, a first in the IGC process.
Stocktaking sessions and facilitation by designated facilitators (e.g., René Lefeber on EIAs).
COVID-19 restrictions limited in-room participation to two representatives per delegation and restricted observer access.


Key findings

Delegates did not conclude the instrument and requested IGC President Rena Lee to seek a UN General Assembly decision to convene a fifth session (IGC-5) in August 2022.
The session was widely described as the "most productive meeting" of the IGC process, with "unprecedented progress" on all four package elements.
MGRs / ABS: Diverging views persisted on the access and benefit-sharing mechanism. A proposal combining an ABS mechanism with a clearinghouse mechanism and an open self-declaratory notification system gained traction.
ABMTs / MPAs: Entrenched positions softened; delegations took small steps toward agreement on the establishment and governance of MPAs. Persistent differences remained on whether the Conference of the Parties or other global/regional/sectoral bodies would hold designation authority.
EIAs: Delegates agreed to base future negotiations on a cross-regional proposal for a tiered approach, incorporating bodies operationalized by the BBNJ agreement. Consensus was not reached on ultimate decision-making responsibility.
CB\&TT: Convergence emerged on the need for an effective, well-resourced, needs-based mechanism, though modalities and the mandatory-versus-voluntary character remained contested.
Institutional arrangements: Consensus on a capable, well-resourced secretariat; general support for assessed contributions; flexibility on the Global Environment Facility as a potential financial mechanism; and a proposal for a non-adversarial dispute-prevention committee alongside a technical dispute-resolution committee.


Limitations

The document is a negotiation summary bulletin, not a peer-reviewed scholarly article; it reports positions and progress rather than presenting original research findings.
Negotiations were incomplete: no consensus was reached on the full text, and several areas were noted as not yet discussed.
COVID-19 restrictions limited in-person participation to two delegates per delegation and severely curtailed observer engagement, potentially affecting the breadth of input.
The bulletin acknowledges that scientific information on BBNJ biodiversity, while growing, remains "albeit insufficient."
Diverging views on core issues (ABS mechanism, MPA governance, CB\&TT modality, secretariat structure) persisted, meaning the instrument's final shape remained undetermined at the time of writing.

