@inproceedings{FifthSessionIntergovernmental2022,
	address = {United Nations, New York City, NY, USA},
	title = {Fifth session of the {Intergovernmental} conference on an international legally binding instrument under the {United} {Nations} {Convention} on the {Law} of the {Sea} on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction},
	url = {https://www.un.org/bbnj/fifth_substantive_session},
	month = aug,
	year = {2022},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
The Fifth Session of the Intergovernmental Conference (IGC-5), held 15–26 August 2022 at UN Headquarters in New York, aimed to elaborate and finalize the text of an international legally binding instrument under the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ). The session was an additional meeting beyond the four mandated by UN General Assembly resolution 72/249 (2017), convened after IGC-4 (March 2022) failed to reach agreement. The goal was to achieve consensus on the four elements of the 2011 "package": marine genetic resources (MGRs) and benefit-sharing, area-based management tools (ABMTs) including marine protected areas, environmental impact assessments (EIAs), and capacity building and transfer of marine technology (CB\&TT), as well as cross-cutting and institutional provisions.

Methods
This document is a negotiation reporting bulletin (Earth Negotiations Bulletin, Vol. 25 No. 240, published by the International Institute for Sustainable Development), not a peer-reviewed research article. The underlying process involved:

Consensus-based multilateral negotiations among state delegations, with IGC President Rena Lee (Singapore) facilitating.
Textual negotiations based on a further revised draft text (A/CONF.232/2022/5) incorporating proposals from IGC-4.
Presidential consultations held behind closed doors to develop "high-level package compromises."
Delegations submitting individual and joint textual proposals to the draft agreement.


Key findings

Delegates were unable to reach full consensus; the session was suspended to be resumed at a later date (TBD at time of writing).
On MGRs and benefit-sharing: progress on application provisions and notification; persistent divergence on the access and benefit-sharing (ABS) mechanism, monetary benefit-sharing, and intellectual property rights.
On EIAs: significant advances on planned/proposed activities and strategic environmental assessments; unresolved differences on decision-making authority, thresholds, and an area-based versus impact-based approach.
On ABMTs/MPAs: delegates agreed or came close to agreement on most provisions, including preparation, review of proposals, and decision-making.
On CB\&TT: delegates agreed to establish a CB\&TT committee and acknowledged it as an essential element; disagreement remained on funding modalities.
Progress was also noted on compliance, dispute settlement, general principles, international cooperation, and sovereign immunity.
President Lee issued a "refreshed" text on 26 August 2022; some states endorsed it as a negotiating basis while others preferred an earlier (21 August) version, citing lack of full consensus.
Multiple delegates described IGC-5 as "the closest we have come to reaching consensus" and "the most productive so far," expressing optimism that a resumed session could finalize the treaty.


Limitations

The document is a summary reporting bulletin, not a full verbatim record or peer-reviewed analysis; it reflects the authors' editorial framing.
No consensus was reached, so no final treaty text or binding outcome exists to evaluate.
The resumed session date was undetermined at the time of publication, leaving the ultimate outcome unknown.
Confidential presidential consultations limited transparency; the full content of closed-door compromises is not publicly documented in this bulletin.
The document does not include quantitative data, scientific evidence, or impact assessments; it reports diplomatic positions and procedural developments only.

