@inproceedings{ResumedFifthSession2023,
	address = {United Nations, New York City, NY, USA},
	title = {Resumed fifth session of the {Intergovernmental} conference on an international legally binding instrument under the {United} {Nations} {Convention} on the {Law} of the {Sea} on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction},
	url = {https://www.un.org/bbnj/fifth_substantive_session},
	month = mar,
	year = {2023},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This Earth Negotiations Bulletin (IISD, 2023) documents the resumed fifth session (IGC-5.2) of the Intergovernmental Conference on an international legally binding instrument (ILBI) under the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ). The session, held 20 February–4 March 2023 at UN Headquarters in New York, aimed to resolve outstanding issues left from IGC-5.1 (August 2022) and conclude approximately fifteen years of negotiations on a new high-seas biodiversity treaty.

Methods
The document is a negotiation report rather than an empirical study. The process it describes involved:

Over 400 delegates representing governments, UN specialized agencies, NGOs, and academia.
A first week of open views on the draft text, followed by an updated draft issued over the mid-meeting weekend.
A second week focused on "cleaning" the text, with particular attention to interlinked provisions on marine genetic resources (MGRs), benefit-sharing, and finance.
Closed-door presidential consultations lasting over 36 hours, led by IGC President Rena Lee (Singapore).
Establishment of an open-ended informal working group for technical edits and harmonization across six UN official languages, with a further resumed session (IGC-5.3) to be convened for formal adoption.


Key findings

Delegates reached agreement on the overall text of the BBNJ treaty, welcomed with sustained standing ovations.
The most contentious provisions concerned MGRs and benefit-sharing (Articles 11–12), including the inclusion of digital sequence information (DSI) and monetary benefit-sharing modalities to be decided by the Conference of the Parties (COP).
A special fund was established; developed-country parties will make annual contributions at 50\% of their assessed contribution to the COP budget until modalities are finalized.
Area-based management tools (ABMTs), including marine protected areas, were agreed with strong proposal, review, and decision-making modalities.
Environmental impact assessments (EIAs) adopted a tiered approach: screening when effects "may have more than a minor or transitory effect," followed by a full EIA at the UNCLOS Article 206 threshold.
Capacity building and technology transfer (CB\&TT) was the first package element finalized.
Both the principle of the common heritage of humankind and the freedom of the high seas were included in Article 5 (general principles).
A robust institutional architecture was established: a COP, a stand-alone secretariat, a Scientific and Technical Body, and a clearing-house mechanism.
The treaty requires 60 ratifications to enter into force; the timeline for reaching this threshold is uncertain.


Limitations

The text was agreed in closed-door consultations but was not formally reviewed and adopted in plenary; technical edits and multilingual harmonization remained pending at the time of publication.
The full treaty text is not reproduced in this bulletin; the authors note that a detailed analysis "will need to wait the text's formal adoption."
The document is a negotiation summary, not a peer-reviewed scholarly article, and reflects the reporting perspective of IISD.
Entry into force depends on 60 ratifications, with no guaranteed timeline, leaving the treaty's operational impact uncertain at the time of writing.
Several substantive details (e.g., exact monetary benefit-sharing modalities, DSI implementation) were deferred to future COP decisions, meaning the final operational framework was not yet fixed.

