@inproceedings{FurtherResumedFifth2023,
	address = {United Nations, New York City, NY, USA},
	title = {Further resumed fifth session of the {Intergovernmental} conference on an international legally binding instrument under the {United} {Nations} {Convention} on the {Law} of the {Sea} on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction},
	url = {https://www.un.org/bbnj/fifth_substantive_session},
	month = jun,
	year = {2023},
	annote = {Summary (qwen3.8:latest):

Objective
This document reports on the Further Resumed Fifth Session (IGC-5.3) of the Intergovernmental Conference convened to adopt an international legally binding instrument under the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) on the conservation and sustainable use of marine biological diversity of areas beyond national jurisdiction (BBNJ). The session, held 19–20 June 2023 at UN Headquarters in New York, aimed to finalize and adopt the Agreement following technical editing of the negotiated text. The broader objective of the IGC process, running since 2018, was to address threats to high-seas biodiversity—including climate change, plastic pollution, overfishing, habitat destruction, ocean acidification, and underwater noise—through a new multilateral treaty covering four elements: marine genetic resources (MGRs) and benefit-sharing; area-based management tools (ABMTs) including marine protected areas (MPAs); environmental impact assessments (EIAs); and capacity building and transfer of marine technology (CB\&TT).

Methods
The document is a reporting bulletin published by the International Institute for Sustainable Development (IISD) as part of the Earth Negotiations Bulletin series. It summarizes the proceedings of IGC-5.3, including the adoption of the agenda, the report of the informal open-ended working group on technical editing, the adoption of the BBNJ Agreement, a general exchange of views, and the consideration of the Conference Report to the UN General Assembly. The bulletin also provides a brief history of the BBNJ negotiation process (2004 Working Group through IGC-5.2) and a brief analytical section on the significance of the Agreement and steps toward implementation. Over 200 delegates representing governments, civil society, academia, and international organizations participated.

Key findings

The BBNJ Agreement was adopted by consensus on 19 June 2023, becoming the third implementing agreement under UNCLOS (after the 1994 Seabed Authority Agreement and the 1995 Fish Stocks Agreement).
The Agreement contains provisions on MGRs and benefit-sharing (Part II, Arts. 9–16), ABMTs/MPAs (Part III, Arts. 17–26), EIAs (Part IV, Arts. 27–39), CB\&TT (Part V, Arts. 40–46), institutional arrangements, financial mechanisms, compliance, and dispute settlement.
The Russian Federation recorded a statement distancing itself from the consensus, raising legal, factual, and financial objections; Jamaica (for CARICOM) noted this was an "unprecedented approach" to disassociation.
The Agreement opens for signature on 20 September 2023 and requires 60 instruments of ratification to enter into force.
Delegates linked the Agreement to the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, particularly the 30x30 target for protected areas by 2030.
Estimated costs for robust MPA protection covering 30\% of the high seas are approximately USD 7 billion in establishment costs and over USD 1 billion in annual operating costs (Blue Nature Alliance estimate).


Limitations

The document is a conference reporting bulletin, not a peer-reviewed scholarly article; it summarizes proceedings rather than presenting original empirical research.
Scientific information on ABNJ biodiversity is described in the text as "insufficient," limiting the evidence base for the Agreement's design.
Implementation challenges remain unresolved: the "not undermining" provision (Art. 5) requires further clarification; synergies with Regional Fisheries Management Organizations and other sectoral bodies are uncertain; and significant financial resources are needed for ratification and implementation.
The timeline for entry into force is uncertain, as UNCLOS took 12 years and the Fish Stocks Agreement over six years to enter into force.
The Russian Federation's objections and potential non-participation of major high-seas actors could compromise the Agreement's effectiveness.

