@inproceedings{zotero-item-51274,
	address = {Jardin des Plantes, Paris},
	title = {Neptune {Forum}},
	url = {https://mission-neptune.com/en/the-forum},
	month = jun,
	year = {2026},
	annote = {Objective
The Neptune Forum (8 June 2026, Muséum national d'Histoire naturelle, Paris) served as the first major public presentation of Mission Neptune since its launch at UNOC3 in Nice (June 2025). Its stated aims were to assess progress made since the Third United Nations Ocean Conference, review advances in ocean diplomacy, and chart next steps for international action. The overarching conviction articulated throughout the proceedings was that sustainable ocean protection is contingent on first understanding the ocean better.

Methods
The Forum was structured as a three-day public and expert programme under the banner "Les Rêves de l'Océan." Key structural elements included:

More than 500 participants from 43 nationalities and over 60 high-level speakers (one-third French, two-thirds international).
Over 5,000 visitors across public programming: documentary screenings (three world premieres), a photographic exhibition (Prince Hussain Aga Khan), and an immersive film experience (Ocean Dreams).
Live satellite connections linking Paris with French Polynesia, Antarctica, China, Singapore, Kenya, and the World Bank.
Co-presidency by François Houllier (IFREMER) and Antje Boetius (MBARI); Scientific Council co-chaired by Margaret Leinen and Vladimir Ryabinin.
Thematic sessions spanning blue diplomacy, ocean governance, science and observation, innovation, and cultural mobilization.

The second edition of the Starfish Barometer, developed by a committee of 29 scientists from 14 countries, was presented as a scientific benchmark.

Key findings

More than 80\% of the world's coral reefs have experienced bleaching events; global ocean observation capacities are declining while the ocean continues to absorb {\textasciitilde}25\% of anthropogenic CO₂ and {\textasciitilde}90\% of excess heat.
Approximately one in five fish consumed globally originates from illegal, unreported, or unregulated fishing (FAO).
France announced three new highly protected marine zones (Audierne Bay, Guadeloupe, French Southern and Antarctic Territories), raising the share of French waters under strong protection to {\textasciitilde}14.68\%.
The European Commission introduced the Ocean Eye initiative within the European Ocean Pact, integrating GOOS observation, Mission Neptune exploration, and Mercator Ocean International's Digital Twin of the Ocean.
Environmental DNA (eDNA) technology, presented by the Minderoo Foundation, enables species identification from a single water sample, with thousands of kits already deployed globally.
The Neptune Fellows programme was launched with four inaugural researchers (MBARI, Scripps), and the first Neptune Chairs (Tahiti, Sharjah, Rabat, Paris) were announced.
Six concluding messages were issued, including the need to bridge the knowledge–action gap, the threat to observation infrastructures, the necessity of equity and inclusion, the role of culture and emotion, the case for treating ocean exploration as a strategic priority comparable to space programmes, and France's invitation to other states to strengthen commitments.


Limitations
The document is a conference proceedings report rather than a peer-reviewed research article; it does not present original empirical data, statistical analyses, or a formal research methodology beyond the forum format. The Starfish Barometer is referenced but its full findings are not reproduced in this text. No explicit limitations section is provided by the authors. The report reflects the perspectives and priorities of Mission Neptune's founding partners and French government stakeholders, which may shape the framing of issues presented. Specific quantitative data (e.g., exact bleaching percentages, observation capacity decline rates) are cited without full sourcing within the document.
