Parallel paper sessions across multiple rooms (Tweekerken and SPP7 buildings) covering the eleven tracks.
Keynote lectures with discussants and chairs (e.g., Lucas Chancel on political economy of energy regimes; Christina Eckes on climate litigation and democracy; Olivier De Schutter on escaping growth dependencies via the UN Roadmap Project).
Closed Special Sessions (CSS) on topics such as post-growth enterprise design, ecological macroeconomic modelling, value pluralism in governance, and climate-model worldviews.
Open Special Sessions (OSS) on circular economy metrics, degrowth scenarios, temporal dimensions of sustainability, non-anthropocentric nature-based solutions, and teaching ecological economics.
Best Student Papers (BSP) session and an ESEE General Assembly.


Key findings
As a conference program, no empirical findings are reported. The thematic scope signals the field's current research priorities:

Critique and alternatives to GDP as a measure of prosperity, including doughnut frameworks and safe-and-just-space analysis.
Post-growth and degrowth macroeconomic frameworks, public finance, and monetary policy reform.
Intersectional justice, rights of nature, and non-anthropocentric perspectives in sustainability governance.
Transitions in energy, materials, circularity, and the bioeconomy.
Behavioral change, sufficiency, demand-side measures, and lifestyle transformation.
Institutional innovation, deliberative democracy, citizen assemblies, and legal innovations (rights of nature, commons law).
Methodological and philosophical foundations of ecological economics as a discipline.


Limitations
Several limitations apply to using this document as a source:

The document is a schedule and program, not a peer-reviewed article; it contains no abstracts, data, or results.
