@techreport{zotero-item-51313,
	title = {Renewables-{Based} {Economy} {Tracker} 2026: {How} {Renewables} are {Transforming} {Economies} and {Societies}},
	url = {https://rbe.ren21.net/tracker},
	month = jul,
	year = {2026},
	annote = {Objective
The Renewables-Based Economy (RBE) Tracker 2026, produced by REN21, aims to measure and make visible the systemic transformation that renewable energy drives across economies, societies, governance systems, and the environment. It explicitly moves beyond traditional deployment metrics (capacity, investment, jobs) to capture how renewables reshape industrial development, energy security, finance, and public policy. The Tracker is intended to give policymakers, investors, and practitioners a data-driven framework for identifying structural barriers and seizing the full economic opportunity of the energy transition, particularly in a context of multiple global crises and geopolitical disruption.

Methods
The report is produced through REN21's network-driven, crowd-sourced methodology, combining local expert insight with rigorous peer review. Key methodological features include:

A multi-stakeholder network partnership spanning governments (20+ countries), inter-governmental organisations (IEA, IRENA, World Bank, UNDP, UNIDO, etc.), industry associations (GWEC, SolarPower Europe, IHA, etc.), NGOs, and academic institutions.
Assessment across four interconnected dimensions: energy systems and energy security; economy; society and governance; and environment and climate.
Contributions from hundreds of experts worldwide, with a large share of research conducted on a voluntary basis.
Data drawn from multiple external sources, including the IEA (World Energy Balances, Tracking SDG7, Household Energy Affordability), IRENA (Renewable Energy and Jobs, Circular Economy), World Bank (energy access, off-grid solar market trends), GOGLA (off-grid solar sales), UNFCCC (NDC Registry, National Adaptation Plans), and the REN21 Policy Database.
An interactive RBE Hub (rbe.ren21.net) providing accessible data packs and figures to complement the printed report.

The Tracker represents an evolution of REN21's long-standing Renewables Global Status Report (GSR), first published in 2005, shifting the analytical lens from sector deployment to whole-economy impact.

Key findings
The provided text is largely front matter (membership lists, foreword, acknowledgements, contributor lists, and reference citations), so detailed analytical findings are only partially visible. What can be extracted includes:

Renewables are described as no longer theoretical: Uruguay is cited as a two-decade case of shifting from imported fossil fuels to locally produced renewable energy, stabilising electricity costs and creating new economic opportunities.
China's CO₂ emissions have been flat or falling for 21 months (as of 2026), and India's 2025 CO₂ growth rate was the slowest in two decades, suggesting renewables are displacing fossil fuel growth in major economies.
Off-grid solar PAYGo sales in sub-Saharan Africa reached record levels in the first half of 2025, while cash sales dropped to a five-year low, indicating a market maturation shift.
European renewable energy communities are growing rapidly, though the European Court of Auditors (2026) notes their potential is "yet to be fulfilled."
Circular-economy challenges are highlighted for renewable energy materials, including end-of-life recycling of critical minerals, wind turbine blades, and photovoltaic components, with significant uncertainty in recycled-materials market valuations.
Fossil fuel dependence is identified as a driver of economic instability, geopolitical tension, and social disruption, positioning renewables as a foundation for stability and competitiveness.


Limitations

The document text supplied for this summary is heavily truncated (multiple "[…]" markers), so the full analytical chapters, country-level data, and detailed indicator scores are not available for review.
A large share of the underlying research was conducted on a voluntary basis, which may introduce uneven depth across regions and dimensions.
The report is a status tracker and policy-oriented synthesis rather than a peer-reviewed empirical study; it aggregates secondary data from IEA, IRENA, World Bank, and other sources without presenting original econometric modelling.
As a 2026 publication, several cited datasets (e.g., IEA World Energy Balances 2023, IRENA Jobs Review 2025) reflect earlier reference years, so the most current annual figures may not yet be incorporated.
The four-dimension framework, while comprehensive, is new in this 2026 edition; longitudinal comparability with prior GSR editions is not explicitly addressed in the available text.

